{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.prompt.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-09-06 15:38:05 GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-09-06 22:38:05 +07 (Asia/Bangkok)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: claude\n     4\tSession-ID: e425d8bd-ad4c-44f7-aaed-54cb3196fd0f\n     5\t# Task: dao.jing.v2 migration plan\n     6\tRole: Lead System Architect\n     7\tImplementers:\n     8\t- Model: claude-fable-5-1 | Assigned: 2026-09-06 22:38:05 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: Architect primary per team.md; this is a migration-strategy and boundary decision, not an implementation\n     9\t\n    10\tRepository root: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld (branch `dao.stream-redesign-v2`).\n    11\t\n    12\tProduce a **draft implementation plan** migrating `dao.jing` off v1 `dao.stream`\n    13\tonto `dao.stream`. This is design work, not implementation. Do not edit any\n    14\tfile: emit the complete plan document as the body of your final response. The\n    15\torchestrator promotes it to `docs/design/dao.jing.v2.implementation-plan.md`\n    16\tafter independent review.\n    17\t\n    18\tRead first:\n    19\t- docs/design/datom.world.md\n    20\t- docs/design/dao.jing.md (especially: The intake pool, Storage ignorance,\n    21\t  Cursor tracking and recovery, Implemented surface, Open items)\n    22\t- docs/design/dao.stream.md (the v2 contract — superior to everything below)\n    23\t- docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md (especially: \"What v1\n    24\t  callers lose, and what replaces it\", \"Boundary of this plan\", \"End condition\")\n    25\t- docs/design/dao.runtime.implementation-plan.md and\n    26\t  docs/design/yin.repl.implementation-plan.md — **shape precedents only**:\n    27\t  match their structure, altitude, and the discipline of naming what is\n    28\t  deferred and why. Do not copy their content.\n    29\t- src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc, src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc,\n    30\t  src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc, src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc\n    31\t- src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc, src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc,\n    32\t  src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc, src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc/ws.cljc\n    33\t- test/dao/stream/conformance.cljc, test/dao/jing_test.cljc,\n    34\t  test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc\n    35\t\n    36\t## State already established — do not re-derive\n    37\t\n    38\tThe dao.stream slice (Phases 1-5) is implemented on clj, cljs (Node) and\n    39\tcljd, including the cljd ws transport. Four consumers have migrated under their\n    40\town plans: `yin.vm`, `dao.runtime`, `dao.await`, `yin.repl`.\n    41\t`dao.jing` is next because `dao.space.{index,schema}` require it, and\n    42\t`dao.space` on the v2 contract is what ends ADR-0003's time-boxed exception.\n    43\tThe full local suite was reported green by the user on this revision; you are\n    44\tnot asked to run or re-verify tests.\n    45\t\n    46\t`dao.jing`'s v1 surface is small and contains **no** `closed?`, `strict-vec`,\n    47\t`drain-one!` or `take!!` call. It is these sites only:\n    48\t- src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:391 — `ds/next` in the observer pool walk\n    49\t- src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:83,149,175,190,207 — `ds/next`, `ds/append!`,\n    50\t  `ds/close!`, and `ds/open!` of `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}`\n    51\t\n    52\t## The four questions the plan must settle\n    53\t\n    54\tThese are the reason an Architect is drafting this rather than an implementer\n    55\tdoing a mechanical port. Answer each explicitly; do not leave one as a phase\n    56\tthat \"will decide\".\n    57\t\n    58\t1. **The observer (`dao.jing/observe-step!`, jing.cljc:391).** v2 cursors are\n    59\t   minted by the stream and opaque; `dao.jing.md` documents pool entries as\n    60\t   `{:stream <ref>, :cursor {:position n}, :status s}` and describes v1's bare\n    61\t   `:ok`/`:blocked`/`:end`/`:daostream/gap` returns. Specify the v2 entry\n    62\t   shape, the cursor discipline (retain exactly the successor `next` returns;\n    63\t   never arithmetic), and how `gap` resynchronization works when the cursor is\n    64\t   opaque. Say what changes in `dao.jing.md` prose and what stays.\n    65\t\n    66\t2. **`dao.jing.file` and the missing append-log (the hard one).** The file\n    67\t   content store is backed by the v1 `:append-log` transport via `ds/open!` +\n    68\t   `dao.stream.log`. The stream v2 plan explicitly defers \"Other transports\n    69\t   (file, UDP, log, relation, apply/RPC layers)\" — **there is no v2\n    70\t   append-log.** Decide between, and justify against `dao.jing.md` §Storage\n    71\t   ignorance and the principle that the well is passive and\n    72\t   payload-agnostic:\n    73\t   (a) this plan owns a v2 append-log transport phase, which then owes the\n    74\t       Phase 1 conformance suite; or\n    75\t   (b) the file backend's durable log is a storage implementation detail that\n    76\t       should never have been a `dao.stream` at all, and the migration is a\n    77\t       removal of that coupling rather than a port; or\n    78\t   (c) something you argue for instead.\n    79\t   Whichever you choose, name what it costs and what it defers.\n    80\t\n    81\t3. **`dao.jing.remote`.** It wraps v1 `dao.stream.rpc.client` / `rpc.ws` and\n    82\t   its JVM-only `connect-content!` is a *synchronous* constructor.\n    83\t   `dao.stream.rpc` is poll-shaped and state-machine-valued\n    84\t   (`client-state`, `serve-once!`, explicit completions), built for the REPL.\n    85\t   Reconcile a synchronous content `get`/`put` against a polling RPC without\n    86\t   reintroducing blocking (`take!!` is gone by construction, and blocking is\n    87\t   unimplementable on cljs/cljd). If the honest answer is that remote content\n    88\t   needs a driver its caller owns, say so and specify it.\n    89\t\n    90\t4. **Phase boundaries and the end condition.** Give ordered phases, each with\n    91\t   its own completion criteria and host matrix (clj, cljs Node, cljd — note\n    92\t   the `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)` reader-conditional trap, `:cljd` first). State\n    93\t   the end condition, whether `dao.jing.v2` is a parallel namespace or an\n    94\t   in-place migration (justify against how the four prior consumers did it),\n    95\t   and what the naming decision is at the end.\n    96\t\n    97\tAlso state explicitly what is **out of scope**: `dao.jing.dht` /\n    98\t`dao.jing.dht.node` (UDP, deferred by the stream plan), `dao.space`'s own\n    99\tmigration, canonical encoding, durable observer checkpoints, and anything else\n   100\tyou judge to belong to a different plan. Distinguish architectural decisions\n   101\tfrom implementation gaps from intentionally deferred work.\n   102\t\n   103\tConstraints: enforce the six non-negotiable invariants. No hidden global state\n   104\t— note that v1's `defopen`/`open!` registry is gone in v2 and jing must not\n   105\treinvent it; `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` is already an explicit closed\n   106\tdispatch and is the precedent. Do not broaden scope beyond `dao.jing*`. Do not\n   107\tedit files. Produce the complete deliverable in this run without waiting for\n   108\tapproval; a plan that promises a plan is an unfinished turn.\n   109\t\n   110\tBegin the final response exactly with:\n   111\tCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\n   112\tCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n   113\tCoding-Agent: claude\n   114\tSession-ID: e425d8bd-ad4c-44f7-aaed-54cb3196fd0f\n   115\t\n   116\tThen the plan document, followed by a short list of the decisions you made that\n   117\tyou consider most likely to be contested in review.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, fundamental primitives (tuples, streams), design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n     6\t\n     7\t## Core Philosophy\n     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams. Persistent facts are immutable tuples and datoms `[e a v t m]`.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    19\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    22\t\n    23\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    24\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    25\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is events on a stream.\n    26\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    27\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    28\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    31\t\n    32\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    33\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata).\n    34\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly. Streams carry whatever values the consumer needs.\n    35\t\n    36\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    37\t\n    38\t## Streams\n    39\t\n    40\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    41\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    42\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    43\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    44\t\n    45\tFunctions can take anything and return anything.\n    46\tAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in Yin.VM that communicate with the outside world through DaoStream. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n    47\t\n    48\tSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n    49\tSee [`docs/design/agent.tzu.md`](./agent.tzu.md) and [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for agent and VM architecture.\n    50\t\n    51\t### Host Boundaries\n    52\t\n    53\tHost-specific boundaries are isolated with DaoStream and interpreters of\n    54\tDaoStream. A host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call.\n    55\t\n    56\tDaoStream handle operations are the one sanctioned function-shaped contact\n    57\twith that boundary: their exhaustive outcome maps are stream protocol, not an\n    58\tapplication callback. Above those operations, portable code still expresses\n    59\thost work as effect values and observes outcomes as stream data.\n    60\t\n    61\tPortable code appends an effect value describing what must happen. A host\n    62\tinterpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and appends the\n    63\toutcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by identity in the\n    64\tdata; neither side holds a reference to the other.\n    65\t\n    66\tAn adapter that exposes a function for portable code to call is not an\n    67\tinterpreter. It isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the\n    68\teffect never appears as an emission.\n    69\t\n    70\tA callback is an event on a stream. That is the same rule in the other\n    71\tdirection: when the host initiates -- a socket receives, a connection drops, a\n    72\ttimer fires -- the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\n    73\tnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n    74\t\n    75\tConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\n    76\tto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n    77\t\n    78\t**An adapter is a map of those functions. That is all an adapter is.** It is a\n    79\tvalue, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files, timers and windows\n    80\tall deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is what they call. The\n    81\thost library is handed the map. Each entry takes the host's arguments and\n    82\treturns one value; the append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry\n    83\tcannot return a value to the host and cannot invoke application code. The\n    84\tadapter is handed only the deposit operation and its own host resource. If its\n    85\tdeposit channel fails, it may close that resource to make the boundary\n    86\tobservably gone; this is teardown, not an upward callback. Host types are\n    87\tclassified into plain data here -- a transform is the last place holding a host\n    88\terror object, and no host type may cross onto the stream.\n    89\t\n    90\tWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n    91\tadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n    92\ttransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read, and it does\n    93\tnot need to. This is what keeps the layering one-way: a boundary depends on\n    94\tnothing above it, because whoever constructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n    95\t\n    96\tA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n    97\tnone of those events semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol\n    98\tvalidation happen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport\n    99\tmachinery, not an event the adapter is obliged to deposit. Once the transport\n   100\thas produced a valid host event, data is syntax (axiom 2), so worth is not a\n   101\tproperty of the datum -- an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to\n   102\tanother that does not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys\n   103\tthe perspectives that have not been taken. How much is retained is the medium's\n   104\tretention policy, which is operational; what an event means is interpretation.\n   105\tNeither is the depositor's decision.\n   106\t\n   107\tAn adapter that takes a function to invoke has relocated the callback, not\n   108\tremoved it, even when every value it passes is plain data. An adapter whose\n   109\tcallback has a meaningful return value is worse: that is a synchronous call in\n   110\tboth directions wearing the shape of an event.\n   111\t\n   112\tHost events are ordinary values on an ordinary stream. A transport may use a\n   113\tqualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and additive evolution;\n   114\tthat envelope remains plain data and creates no separate event mechanism. A\n   115\tcomposition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary for facts that it\n   116\towns. Neither requires a bespoke bus or callback API.\n   117\t\n   118\tHost error types never cross the boundary. A transform function classifies them\n   119\tand emits a qualified value.\n   120\t\n   121\tA host with no implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported\n   122\tresult. That is a correct outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n   123\t\n   124\t## Design Principles\n   125\t\n   126\tCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\n   127\tStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n   128\t\n   129\tSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n   130\tSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n   131\t\n   132\t## Code Style\n   133\t\n   134\t- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n   135\t- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n   136\t- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n   137\t- Avoid cleverness.\n   138\t- Name things after what they do to streams.\n   139\t\n   140\t## Development Workflow\n   141\t\n   142\t- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n   143\t- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n   144\t- Do not optimize prematurely.\n   145\t- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n   146\t- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n   147\t\n   148\t### Problem Decomposition\n   149\t\n   150\tIf a problem appears complex:\n   151\t1. Decompose it into streams.\n   152\t2. Identify invariants.\n   153\t3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n   154\t4. Make causality explicit.\n   155\t5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n   156\t\n   157\tFor advanced topics (parallel transport, capability tokens, entanglement):\n   158\tSee [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md)\n   159\t\n   160\t## Operational & Specialized Guides\n   161\t\n   162\t- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n   163\t- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n   164\t- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`.\n   165\t- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/website.md`](../agents/website.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
