{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '331,630p' collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | nl -ba -v331\"","aggregated_output":"   331\t`dao.jing.v2` and `dao.jing.v2.remote` contain no host branch. No new file may\n   332\tcontain a bare `#?(:clj …)` form — `#?(:clj …)` does not exclude code from the\n   333\tcljd build; `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)` with `:cljd` first is the only safe spelling,\n   334\tand `:cljd` in tail position silently fails. Full cljd namespace compilation\n   335\tgates every phase.\n   336\t\n   337\t## Divergence register\n   338\t\n   339\tEvery place the v2 pieces deliberately differ from v1, with the reason.\n   340\t\n   341\t| v1 behavior                                                                                      | v2                                                                                                   | why                                                                                                   |\n   342\t| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   343\t| `observer-state` takes streams; cursor is `{:position 0}`                                        | takes `{:stream :cursor}` members; the composition mints the cursor                                  | every valid cursor comes from the stream; the anchor is the composition's policy                       |\n   344\t| signals `:ok`, `:blocked`, `:end`, `:daostream/gap`; malformed results throw                     | the seven `:dao.stream/…` outcomes; three defect outcomes reported as data with `:member` and `:result` | the result convention; an outcome outside the closed set still throws, as a transport defect         |\n   345\t| a gap is reported, cursor unchanged, never resynced                                              | same rule, extended to `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor`, `transport-error`; `adopt-cursor` is the resync | one rule for every non-progress outcome; resync is a composition decision                         |\n   346\t| `dao.jing.file` opens `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}`; handle carries `:log`                    | private framed file; handle carries `:path :state :write-lock` and the three fns, no `:log`          | Decision 2                                                                                            |\n   347\t| `file_test` counts records with `ds/next` over the raw log                                       | `dao.jing.file/records` returns the decoded records of a path; tests use it                          | the framing is private; the test reads through the backend's own reader                              |\n   348\t| `content-client` is a handle; `:get-content-fn` returns the value                                | `client-state` / `request-*` / `step`; results are completions correlated by id                       | no operation waits; unimplementable otherwise on cljs/cljd                                            |\n   349\t| `connect-content!` — synchronous, JVM-only                                                       | gone; the composition attaches through `dao.stream.ws` and builds the client from the whole `attach!` result | the constructor waited; attachment outcome arrives as deposited data                         |\n   350\t| malformed presence envelope throws inside `get`                                                  | completes as an `:error` with a malformed-response code                                              | it surfaces inside a driver step, where an exception has nowhere to go                                |\n   351\t| `close-fn` guards concurrent closes with a lock and retries on failure                           | gone with the handle; `close!` on the ws handle is idempotent and answers `ok`                       | the client holds no host resource; the handle does                                                    |\n   352\t| `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate opens to a handle                                                  | unchanged on v1; no v2 coordinate in this plan                                                       | it would open into a composition, which is `dao.space`'s to design with hydration                     |\n   353\t\n   354\t**v1-only tests deliberately not mirrored:** `file_test`'s direct\n   355\t`{:dao.stream/type :append-log}` opens and `count-records` (replaced by\n   356\t`records`); `remote_test`'s `network-invalid-url-test` (a bad URL is\n   357\t`invalid-descriptor` or a deposited `/transport-error`, tested in Phase J3b as\n   358\tdata), `close-failure-retry-test` (no close-fn to fail), and every\n   359\t`with-server` case built on `rpc-ws/start!` (replaced by the J3 compositions).\n   360\t\n   361\t## Phase J0 — What exists\n   362\t\n   363\tRecorded so the phases start from evidence.\n   364\t\n   365\t- `dao.stream` (protocols, outcome sets, `validate-outcome`), the ring\n   366\t  buffer with `make-attacher`, `dao.stream.apply` (envelope, `serve-once!`),\n   367\t  `dao.stream.rpc` (`client-state`, `request!`, `poll!`, `take-completed`,\n   368\t  `take-diagnostics`, `unsent?`, `rebind`), `dao.stream.rpc.ws`\n   369\t  (`decoder`, `init-client`, `rebind`), `dao.stream.ws` with host\n   370\t  `connect!` seams on clj, cljs and cljd, `dao.stream.serving`, and the\n   371\t  conformance harness. All green per the user on this revision.\n   372\t- `dao.jing` core and `dao.jing.mem` are stream-free.\n   373\t- `test/dao/jing_test.cljc` observer cases (lines 322–561) are the behavioral\n   374\t  evidence for J2; `test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc` for J1;\n   375\t  `test/dao/jing/remote_test.cljc` for J3; `test/dao/stream/rpc_test.cljc`\n   376\t  is the shape precedent for the socket-free J3 tests.\n   377\t\n   378\t## Phase J1 — `dao.jing.file` without a stream\n   379\t\n   380\t- Replace the five `ds/*` sites with a private framed-file section:\n   381\t  `open-frames!` (create parent dirs, open read/write, truncate an incomplete\n   382\t  tail exactly as `dao.stream.log/make-log-stream` does), `append-frame!`\n   383\t  (length prefix + bytes at the current end, then sync), `replay-frames`\n   384\t  (every complete record from offset 0), `close-frames!`. Three host branches,\n   385\t  `:cljd` first.\n   386\t- `put` is unchanged in order: validate, lock, closed check, presence check,\n   387\t  append and sync, then swap the content map, then `:inserted`. `close!` stays\n   388\t  idempotent. Both drop `dao.stream` and `dao.stream.log` from the requires.\n   389\t- Public `records`: `(records path)` returns the decoded, validated\n   390\t  `[address payload]` vector of a file, for tests and diagnostics; it opens,\n   391\t  replays and closes.\n   392\t- **On-disk compatibility test.** A golden byte sequence for one known record\n   393\t  is written to a temp path by hand; `create-content-file` must read it and\n   394\t  `records` must return it. This is what proves the framing did not drift.\n   395\t- Port `file_test`: `handle-shape-test` asserts no `:log` and no `:stream`;\n   396\t  every `count-records` becomes `(count (records path))`; the corrupt-record\n   397\t  and raw-duplicate cases write their raw frames through `append-frame!`.\n   398\t  `observer-convergence-test` moves to J2 (it needs the v2 observer).\n   399\t\n   400\tDeliverable: `dao.jing.file` requires no `dao.stream*` namespace;\n   401\t`file_test` green on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd with `dao.stream` absent from\n   402\tits requires. Consumers (`dao.space.*`, `dao.data.btree_durability_test`) pass\n   403\tunchanged, confirmed by running them rather than by inspection.\n   404\t\n   405\t## Phase J2 — `dao.jing.v2`, the observer\n   406\t\n   407\t`src/cljc/dao/jing/v2.cljc`: `observer-state`, `observe-step!`, `adopt-cursor`,\n   408\tper *Decision 1*. Requires `dao.jing` and `dao.stream` only.\n   409\t\n   410\tTests, `test/dao/jing/v2_test.cljc`, over v2 ring buffers created with\n   411\t`ringbuffer/create!` and cursors minted with `stream/cursor … :dao.stream/oldest`:\n   412\t\n   413\t- every observer case in `jing_test.cljc` lines 322–561, ported: plain-data\n   414\t  state, empty pool blocked, all blocked, hashing and retrieval, equal\n   415\t  payloads from two streams converge, blocked/ended members do not starve\n   416\t  others, all ended, drains then end, fair round-robin, strict interleave,\n   417\t  cursor advances only after successful materialization. Cursor assertions\n   418\t  become successor-equality assertions (`(= (:dao.stream/cursor res) …)`)\n   419\t  never position arithmetic.\n   420\t- **gap**: a capacity-2 ring buffer with three appends; the signal is\n   421\t  `:dao.stream/gap` with `:member 0` and a `:cursor`; the member cursor is\n   422\t  unchanged; the other member still progresses; the gap reports again on the\n   423\t  next turn; after `adopt-cursor` with the recovery cursor the member\n   424\t  materializes the live value.\n   425\t- **defect outcomes**: a reified reader handle scripted to answer\n   426\t  `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor`, `transport-error` (and, for the\n   427\t  totality test, each outcome in `dao.stream/outcomes-next`); assert the\n   428\t  signal, `:member`, `:result`, unchanged cursor, and that an outcome outside\n   429\t  the declared set throws.\n   430\t- **members round-trip**: `(observer-state (remove-nth (:members st) i))`\n   431\t  keeps the remaining members' cursors.\n   432\t- `observer-convergence-test` from `file_test`, over the J1 file backend.\n   433\t\n   434\tDeliverable: `dao.jing.v2` on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd; `Testing\n   435\tdao.jing.v2-test` confirmed in the Node output; no namespace under\n   436\t`dao.jing.v2*` requires `dao.stream`.\n   437\t\n   438\t## Phase J3 — `dao.jing.v2.remote`\n   439\t\n   440\t**J3a — socket-free, all three hosts.** `src/cljc/dao/jing/v2/remote.cljc`\n   441\tper *Decision 3*. Tests, `test/dao/jing/v2/remote_test.cljc`, mirror\n   442\t`rpc_test.cljc`: request and response media are two ring buffers; the server\n   443\tside is `apply/server-state` + `apply/serve-once!` over `default-handlers`\n   444\ton a `dao.jing.mem` store; the client is `rpc/client-state` over the same\n   445\tmedia, wrapped by `client-state`.\n   446\t\n   447\t- put then get round-trips through `step`; duplicate put completes `:present`;\n   448\t  absent get completes `:found? false`; a stored `nil` and a stored\n   449\t  `{:found? true :value \"hello\"}` round-trip exactly (the envelope-shaped\n   450\t  payload case from v1's `network-presence-envelope-test`).\n   451\t- `request-*` on a non-segment address throws before allocation; a handler\n   452\t  rejecting a hash-mismatched address completes as `:error`.\n   453\t- a scripted writer answering `full` once: `pending-request`, then `busy` on a\n   454\t  second request, then `requested` after a step; exactly one id allocated.\n   455\t- gap on the response medium and `/detached` complete every outstanding\n   456\t  request as `:lost`; malformed responses complete as `:error` with the\n   457\t  malformed-response code and never as `:found?`.\n   458\t- completions publish exactly once; `:completed` empty after `step`.\n   459\t- `default-handlers` on a handle without `:put-content-fn` throws at\n   460\t  construction.\n   461\t\n   462\t**J3b — over `dao.stream.ws`, same process, all three hosts.** A serving\n   463\tcomposition assembled in the test from `dao.stream.serving` and the host\n   464\t`connect!`/`bind!` seams, exactly as `test/dao/stream/ws_test.cljc` and\n   465\t`serving_test.cljc` do; the served stream is a capacity-1 identity anchor as\n   466\tthe REPL's D3 settled. The client mints its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor on\n   467\tits traffic medium *before* `attach!`, builds the RPC state with\n   468\t`rpc.ws/init-client` from the whole `attach!` result, and wraps it with\n   469\t`client-state`. Prove: put and get round-trip over the wire; two clients see\n   470\teach other's writes; the file backend survives a stop, restart and reattach\n   471\t(v1's `network-file-restart-test`); killing the connection completes the\n   472\toutstanding request as `:lost` with `/detached` and `rebind` after a fresh\n   473\t`attach!` resumes with the same cursor; a descriptor naming a path nothing\n   474\tserves completes as `:lost` with `/not-found`.\n   475\t\n   476\tDeliverable: J3a green on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd; J3b green on clj and cljs\n   477\t(Node), and on cljd through the cljd ws lane. The cljd test regenerates\n   478\t`test/cljd-out/`; one process owns it at a time.\n   479\t\n   480\t## Phase J4 — Design prose\n   481\t\n   482\t`dao.jing.md`, exactly the sections *Decision 1* names, plus: *Implemented\n   483\tsurface* describes `dao.jing.file` as \"a content-addressed store backed by a\n   484\tprivate framed append-only file\" and `dao.jing.v2.remote` as handlers plus a\n   485\tclient state machine the composition drives, and states that `jing/materialize!`\n   486\tand `jing/get` are local operations that do not apply to a remote store. The\n   487\t*Reads* paragraph that says a reader \"may access the target … through a remote\n   488\ttransport (`dao.jing.remote`, `dao.jing.dht`)\" gains \"as a request whose answer\n   489\tarrives as a completion\". `dao.jing.dht.md` is not edited.\n   490\t\n   491\tThe v1 observer's docstrings in `dao.jing` are not edited; they go with it.\n   492\t\n   493\t## Phase J5 — Deletion and the naming decision\n   494\t\n   495\tGated on `dao.space`'s migration: it is the last consumer of the v1 observer\n   496\t(through its tests) and of v1 `dao.jing.remote` (through `dao.jing.coordinate`\n   497\tand its tests). When that gate opens:\n   498\t\n   499\t- delete `observer-state` and `observe-step!` from `dao.jing` and its\n   500\t  `dao.stream` require; delete the observer section of `jing_test.cljc`;\n   501\t- delete `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc` and `test/dao/jing/remote_test.cljc`;\n   502\t  `dao.jing.coordinate`'s `:dao.jing/remote` branch is deleted or re-pointed\n   503\t  under `dao.space`'s plan, not here;\n   504\t- take **one explicit decision**, mirroring the stream plan's end condition.\n   505\t  The recommendation is to **fold back**: move the v2 observer into `dao.jing`\n   506\t  and rename `dao.jing.v2.remote` to `dao.jing.remote`, in the same change as\n   507\t  `dao.stream`'s rename if that is the decision there. DaoJing's namespace\n   508\t  names appear on no wire and in no coordinate — coordinate types are\n   509\t  keywords like `:dao.jing/file`, unaffected — so only requires change. An\n   510\t  undecided coexistence is a defect of the migration, not a steady state.\n   511\t\n   512\t## Host matrix\n   513\t\n   514\t| phase | clj | cljs (Node) | cljd | notes                                                                                     |\n   515\t| ----- | --- | ----------- | ---- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   516\t| J1    | ✓   | ✓           | ✓    | three host branches in `dao.jing.file`, `:cljd` first; Node uses synchronous `fs`         |\n   517\t| J2    | ✓   | ✓           | ✓    | pure `.cljc`, no host branch                                                              |\n   518\t| J3a   | ✓   | ✓           | ✓    | pure `.cljc`, no host branch                                                              |\n   519\t| J3b   | ✓   | ✓           | ✓    | composition uses the per-host `connect!` seam; cljd through the cljd ws lane              |\n   520\t| J4    | —   | —           | —    | prose                                                                                     |\n   521\t| J5    | ✓   | ✓           | ✓    | deletions; confirm every `dao.jing*` test namespace still appears in each host's output   |\n   522\t\n   523\tPer the standing rule, confirm `Testing dao.jing.v2-test` and `Testing\n   524\tdao.jing.v2.remote-test` appear in the Node output rather than assuming\n   525\tdiscovery.\n   526\t\n   527\t## Invariants, checked\n   528\t\n   529\t- **No hidden global state.** `dao.jing.v2` holds no atom. `dao.jing.file`'s\n   530\t  state atom and lock are per-handle and explicit, as `dao.jing.mem`'s are.\n   531\t  No `defopen`, no dispatch table, no `defonce`.\n   532\t- **No implicit control flow.** `observe-step!` and `step` return; the\n   533\t  composition calls again. Nothing self-schedules.\n   534\t- **No callbacks.** None in any new public surface. The ws adapter map is the\n   535\t  transport's, confined to J3b's composition.\n   536\t- **No shared mutable state.** A content handle is owned by whoever created\n   537\t  it; the RPC state is a value threaded by one driver.\n   538\t- **Interpretation and execution stay separate.** The observer interprets the\n   539\t  pool; the backend executes the write; the remote client interprets\n   540\t  completions; the serving composition executes the socket.\n   541\t- **No assumed graphs.** Nothing here relates content addresses to each other.\n   542\t\n   543\t## Boundary of this plan\n   544\t\n   545\t**Untouched:** `dao.jing` core and its v1 observer until J5; `dao.jing.mem`;\n   546\t`dao.jing.remote` until J5; `dao.jing.coordinate`; `dao.jing.dht`,\n   547\t`dao.jing.dht.kad`, `dao.jing.dht.node`; every `dao.space*` and\n   548\t`dao.data.btree*` namespace and test; `dao.stream` v1 in every form.\n   549\t\n   550\t**Architectural decisions**, made here: the durable log is not a stream\n   551\t(Decision 2); remote content is a driven state machine, not a handle\n   552\t(Decision 3); the observer's cursor and non-progress discipline (Decision 1);\n   553\tparallel-versus-in-place by whether a v1 consumer remains (Decision 4).\n   554\t\n   555\t**Implementation gaps**, left open knowingly: the v2 remote client has no\n   556\tcoordinate and cannot be reached from `dao.jing.coordinate/open!`; the\n   557\tserving side exists only as test compositions.\n   558\t\n   559\t**Intentionally deferred**, each to a named home:\n   560\t\n   561\t- `dao.jing.dht` and `dao.jing.dht.node` — UDP transport, deferred by the\n   562\t  stream plan; `dht.cljc` itself requires no `dao.stream`, and `node.cljc`'s\n   563\t  `dao.stream.transit` require goes with the UDP transport's own migration.\n   564\t- `dao.space`'s own migration: `publish-index!`'s v1 `ds/append!` into the\n   565\t  intake pool, `snapshot-datoms`' `{:position 0}` walk, the published-index\n   566\t  `defopen`, `DaoStreamLog`'s writer face, and switching its tests to\n   567\t  `dao.jing.v2`.\n   568\t- The `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate under v2 and async B-tree hydration\n   569\t  (`dao.data.btree.md` §5.4) — `dao.space`'s query-side plan; this client is\n   570\t  the \"async DaoJing backend\" that section waits for.\n   571\t- A content-serving endpoint as production infrastructure — the composition\n   572\t  that starts, serves and stops a content server belongs to whatever product\n   573\t  surface needs it, as the REPL's R4 did for the REPL.\n   574\t- Canonical encoding; durable observer checkpoints and a long-running runner;\n   575\t  explicit materialization acknowledgement; garbage collection — the four\n   576\t  open items of `dao.jing.md`, unchanged by this plan. Checkpoints gain only\n   577\t  the shape constraint that the persisted value is the transport's own cursor,\n   578\t  whose serializability the contract leaves TBD.\n   579\t- A v2 file or append-log transport — until a consumer needs cursors over a\n   580\t  durable file.\n   581\t\n   582\t## End condition\n   583\t\n   584\tComplete when all of the following hold on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd:\n   585\t\n   586\t- `dao.jing.file` requires no `dao.stream*` namespace, reads every content\n   587\t  file v1 wrote, and its suite runs with no stream fixture.\n   588\t- `dao.jing.v2` observes v2 reader handles with stream-minted cursors, is\n   589\t  total over `dao.stream/outcomes-next` by a declaration-driven test,\n   590\t  retains only successors and adopted cursors, and requires only `dao.jing`\n   591\t  and `dao.stream`.\n   592\t- `dao.jing.v2.remote` round-trips put and get through `dao.stream.apply`\n   593\t  and `dao.stream.rpc` over ring buffers on all three hosts and over\n   594\t  `dao.stream.ws` on each, completing lost requests as data.\n   595\t- `dao.jing.md` describes the v2 observer, the stream-free file backend and\n   596\t  the driven remote client.\n   597\t- No namespace under `dao.jing.v2*` requires `dao.stream` or\n   598\t  `dao.stream.rpc.*`.\n   599\t- Once `dao.space` has migrated: the v1 observer and v1 `dao.jing.remote` are\n   600\t  deleted, and the naming decision has been taken explicitly.\n   601\t\n   602\t---\n   603\t\n   604\t## Decisions most likely to be contested in review\n   605\t\n   606\t1. **Decision 2 (b)** — removing the stream from the file backend rather than\n   607\t   building a v2 log transport. The counter-argument is Axiom 1 read\n   608\t   literally; the answer is that the log has one reader who is its only\n   609\t   writer, and that `dao.jing.mem` already sets the precedent.\n   610\t2. **Keeping the on-disk framing** byte-for-byte instead of taking the chance\n   611\t   to change it. Chosen for compatibility with files that exist; the format\n   612\t   is now private, so it can change later without a transport version.\n   613\t3. **A `dao.jing.v2` that contains only the observer**, rather than a\n   614\t   wholesale parallel namespace like the four prior consumers. Chosen to\n   615\t   avoid two address-minting paths.\n   616\t4. **Rewriting `dao.jing.file` in place** while every prior plan promised \"no\n   617\t   existing implementation is modified\". Chosen because there is nothing to be\n   618\t   beside and its consumers' surface does not change; the risk is confined to\n   619\t   its own suite plus the `dao.space` and btree suites, which J1 runs.\n   620\t5. **Non-progress outcomes are re-reported every turn** and never resolved by\n   621\t   the observer, including `transport-error`. A reviewer may prefer\n   622\t   auto-adopting the gap cursor since materialization is idempotent; the plan\n   623\t   holds that acceptability of loss is the composition's call.\n   624\t6. **`busy` on `request-*` while an envelope is unsent**, rather than queuing\n   625\t   inside the client. Chosen so the client owns no queue and the driver\n   626\t   decides; the REPL's driver already works this way.\n   627\t7. **Leaving `dao.jing.coordinate`'s `:dao.jing/remote` on v1** and deferring\n   628\t   the v2 remote coordinate to `dao.space`'s plan. The alternative — a\n   629\t   coordinate that opens into a composition — would have this plan designing\n   630\t   the query side's hydration.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,330p' collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | nl -ba -v1\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-06 15:46:07 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-06 22:46:07 +07 (Asia/Bangkok)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: claude\n     4\tSession-ID: e425d8bd-ad4c-44f7-aaed-54cb3196fd0f\n     5\t\n     6\t# dao.jing on DaoStream v2 — the content-observer slice\n     7\t\n     8\tStatus: draft migration plan, derived from and subordinate to\n     9\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) (the contract), [`dao.jing.md`](./dao.jing.md)\n    10\t(the storage-observer design) and [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Its\n    11\ttransport prerequisite is\n    12\t[`dao.stream.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.stream.implementation-plan.md),\n    13\tPhases 1–5, which are implemented on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd. It follows the\n    14\tshape of the `dao.runtime` and `yin.repl` plans and is the fifth consumer\n    15\tmigration. This document is transient: it is consumed as its phases complete.\n    16\tDrafted 2026-09-06. No phase has started.\n    17\t\n    18\t## Context\n    19\t\n    20\t`dao.jing` is next because `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.schema` require it,\n    21\tand `dao.space` on the v2 contract is what ends ADR-0003's time-boxed\n    22\texception. The v1 surface inside `dao.jing*` is small — one `ds/next` in the\n    23\tobserver walk (`jing.cljc:391`) and five sites in the file backend\n    24\t(`jing/file.cljc:83,149,175,190,207`) — with no `closed?`, `strict-vec`,\n    25\t`drain-one!` or `take!!` anywhere. A mechanical port is not what makes it hard.\n    26\tWhat makes it hard is that two of the three stream-touching pieces have no v2\n    27\tcounterpart to port *to*: there is no v2 append-log transport, and there is no\n    28\tv2 RPC that returns a value synchronously. Each of those is a boundary decision,\n    29\tsettled below before any phase runs.\n    30\t\n    31\t## The problem, by piece\n    32\t\n    33\t| piece                                                    | v1 mechanism                                                                                               | v2 counterpart                                                            |\n    34\t| -------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n    35\t| `dao.jing/observer-state`, `observe-step!`               | `ds/next` on any v1 reader; inline `{:position 0}` cursors; bare `:ok`/`:blocked`/`:end`/`:daostream/gap`  | reader surface: `cursor`, `next`; opaque cursors; outcome maps            |\n    36\t| `dao.jing.file/create-content-file`                      | `ds/open!` of `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}`; `ds/next` to replay; `ds/append!` + fsync; `ds/close!`     | **none** — the stream plan defers file/log transports                     |\n    37\t| `dao.jing.remote/connect-content!`, `content-client`     | `dao.stream.rpc.client/call!` — synchronous on the JVM, Promise/Future elsewhere; JVM-only constructor     | `dao.stream.rpc` — poll-shaped, state-machine-valued, all three hosts  |\n    38\t| `dao.jing.remote/default-handlers`                       | handler map `{op (fn [& args] value)}`                                                                     | same shape; `dao.stream.apply/dispatch-request` applies it unchanged   |\n    39\t| `dao.jing.mem`, `dao.jing.coordinate`, hashing, `materialize!`, `get`, `close!` | stream-free                                                                         | unchanged                                                                 |\n    40\t\n    41\tConsumers outside `dao.jing*` that reach these pieces, named so the boundary is\n    42\tvisible: `dao.space.index` (`jing-coordinate/open!` inside its `defopen`, and\n    43\tv1 `ds/append!` into the intake pool), `dao.space.schema`, `dao.space.transactor`,\n    44\t`dao.data.btree.storage` (handle API only), `yin.vm.space`, and the tests under\n    45\t`test/dao/space/` and `test/dao/data/` that drive the v1 observer over v1 intakes\n    46\tand open remote clients through `connect-content!`. None of them is migrated\n    47\there.\n    48\t\n    49\t## Strategy\n    50\t\n    51\tThe same two narrowings as the sibling plans, with one deliberate difference in\n    52\twhere the v2 namespace boundary falls.\n    53\t\n    54\t1. **Parallel where a v1 consumer still needs the v1 shape; in place where\n    55\t   nothing is being replaced.** The four prior consumers were rebuilt wholesale\n    56\t   beside their v1 twins because their entire surface was stream-shaped. Most\n    57\t   of `dao.jing` is not: content addressing, `materialize!`, `get`, `close!`\n    58\t   and `dao.jing.mem` never touch a stream. Duplicating them into a `dao.jing.v2`\n    59\t   would create a second minting path for content addresses — two sources of\n    60\t   truth for identity, which is worse than an unusual namespace layout. So:\n    61\t   - `dao.jing` core stays as it is. Its v1 observer functions stay untouched\n    62\t     until `dao.space` migrates, because `dao.space`'s tests drive them over v1\n    63\t     intakes today.\n    64\t   - The v2 observer is a new namespace, **`dao.jing.v2`**, requiring `dao.jing`\n    65\t     for `materialize!` and `dao.stream` for the reader surface. It is the\n    66\t     part of DaoJing that lives on the stream contract, and nothing else.\n    67\t   - The remote adapter is a new namespace, **`dao.jing.v2.remote`**, beside\n    68\t     the untouched v1 `dao.jing.remote`, which `dao.jing.coordinate` and the\n    69\t     `dao.space` tests keep using until their own plan.\n    70\t   - `dao.jing.file` is rewritten **in place**, because its migration is a\n    71\t     removal of a coupling, not a port (see *Decision 2*). There is no v2\n    72\t     transport to sit beside; its consumers use only `create-content-file` and\n    73\t     the handle API, which do not change.\n    74\t2. **The content-observer slice, and nothing else.** One observer, one durable\n    75\t   backend, one remote client state machine with its handlers. Not the\n    76\t   canonical encoding, not durable checkpoints, not a content-serving endpoint\n    77\t   as production infrastructure, not `dao.space`'s intake appends, not the DHT.\n    78\t\n    79\tNo compatibility facade, alias, or dual protocol. The v1 observer and v1\n    80\tremote are evidence about behavior, not constraints on the v2 shape.\n    81\t\n    82\t## Decisions\n    83\t\n    84\tSettled here so no phase decides them alone.\n    85\t\n    86\t### Decision 1 — The observer: entry shape, cursor discipline, gap\n    87\t\n    88\t**Entry shape.** A pool member is `{:stream <v2 reader handle>, :cursor <opaque>,\n    89\t:status s}`. The composition mints the cursor and hands both in:\n    90\t\n    91\t```clojure\n    92\t(jing.v2/observer-state [{:stream h1 :cursor c1} {:stream h2 :cursor c2}])\n    93\t;; => {:members [{:stream h1 :cursor c1 :status :pending} ...] :next 0}\n    94\t```\n    95\t\n    96\t`observer-state` no longer takes bare streams and fabricates `{:position 0}`.\n    97\tIt cannot: every valid cursor comes from the stream, and the anchor is the\n    98\tcomposition's policy — `:dao.stream/oldest` for a fresh pool, a kept cursor for\n    99\ta resumed one — not the observer's. A member without a cursor, or whose stream\n   100\tdoes not satisfy the reader surface, is a composition defect detected before\n   101\tany operation runs and throws, which is the one place the contract permits an\n   102\texception. `observer-state` accepts exactly the member shape it stores, so\n   103\t`(observer-state (:members st))` rebuilds a pool from a previous state; that is\n   104\thow a composition drops a member without touching another member's cursor.\n   105\t\n   106\t**Cursor discipline.** The observer retains exactly the successor `next`\n   107\treturns, after `materialize!` succeeds, and never anything else. There is no\n   108\tarithmetic on a cursor anywhere in `dao.jing.v2`, and no test inspects a\n   109\tcursor's shape — the conformance harness's own rule. The only other way a\n   110\tmember's cursor changes is `adopt-cursor`:\n   111\t\n   112\t```clojure\n   113\t(jing.v2/adopt-cursor state i cursor) ; cursor came from the stream: minted, successor, or gap recovery\n   114\t```\n   115\t\n   116\twhich is the composition passing back a value the stream handed out.\n   117\t\n   118\t**Signals are the contract's outcome set, total.** `observe-step!` returns\n   119\t`{:state s' :signal k ...}` where `k` is one of the seven keywords in\n   120\t`dao.stream/outcomes-next`, and no other:\n   121\t\n   122\t| signal                                                   | when                                                                   | extra keys                          | member cursor |\n   123\t| -------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------- |\n   124\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                                         | a payload was materialized                                             | `:address`                          | successor     |\n   125\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`                                    | the pool is empty, or every non-ended member is blocked                | —                                   | unchanged     |\n   126\t| `:dao.stream/end`                                        | every member has ended                                                 | —                                   | unchanged     |\n   127\t| `:dao.stream/gap`                                        | member `i`'s position was evicted                                      | `:member i`, `:cursor` (recovery)   | unchanged     |\n   128\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch`, `/invalid-cursor`, `/transport-error` | member `i`'s read failed                                   | `:member i`, `:result` (the outcome map) | unchanged |\n   129\t\n   130\tThe last four are reported immediately, the member's `:status` records the\n   131\toutcome, the scheduling index moves past that member, and the member is polled\n   132\tagain on its next turn — and reports again. That is v1's gap rule (\"never\n   133\tauto-resynchronized\") applied to every non-`ok`/`blocked`/`end` outcome, one\n   134\trule instead of four. The reason it is right for an intake pool: a `gap` on a\n   135\tcontent intake means payloads were appended, acknowledged to their publisher,\n   136\tand never materialized. Materialization is idempotent and replay-safe, so\n   137\tadopting the recovery cursor is *safe*; whether it is *acceptable* is a question\n   138\tonly the composition can answer, since it knows whether the publisher will\n   139\tre-append. The observer must not answer it. `cursor-mismatch` and\n   140\t`invalid-cursor` are composition defects; `transport-error` may be transient.\n   141\tAll three are resolved the same way: the composition adopts a cursor it obtained\n   142\tfrom the stream, or rebuilds the pool without that member. A test iterates\n   143\t`dao.stream/outcomes-next` and asserts every outcome produces a branch, so a\n   144\tfuture contract outcome fails the test rather than falling into a default.\n   145\t\n   146\t**Materialization failure is unchanged.** `materialize!` throwing propagates\n   147\tand leaves the caller-owned state untouched. The storage backend is not a\n   148\tstream and is not under the stream contract; its failures stay exceptions, as\n   149\t`dao.jing.md` §Materialization rule already specifies.\n   150\t\n   151\t**What changes in `dao.jing.md`, and what stays.** Changes: *The intake pool*\n   152\t— the entry shape sentence gains \"the cursor is minted by the composition from\n   153\tan anchor of its choosing, and is retained exactly as the stream returns it\";\n   154\t*Cursor tracking and recovery* — the four bare results become the seven\n   155\t`:dao.stream/…` outcomes with the table above, and the sentence about resync\n   156\tbeing the caller's decision is generalized to the three defect outcomes;\n   157\t*Implemented surface* — names `dao.jing.v2` for the observer and the\n   158\tstream-free file backend; *Open items* — durable checkpoints gain the sentence\n   159\tthat a checkpoint stores whatever cursor value the transport minted, and that\n   160\twhether such a value survives serialization is transport-owned and TBD in the\n   161\tcontract. Stays, untouched: *Definition*, *Publication from an agent*,\n   162\t*Materialization rule*, *Canonical encoding*, *Storage ignorance*, *Physical\n   163\tintake versus semantic composition*, *Reads*, *Resource lifecycle*, *Lineage*.\n   164\tNothing semantic about DaoJing moves; the migration changes how the observer\n   165\treads, not what it is.\n   166\t\n   167\t### Decision 2 — `dao.jing.file`: the durable log was never a stream (option b)\n   168\t\n   169\t**Decision.** The file backend's durable log is a storage implementation\n   170\tdetail. The migration removes the `dao.stream` coupling; it does not build a\n   171\tv2 append-log transport, and it does not use one.\n   172\t\n   173\t**Why, against §Storage ignorance and the passive well.** Four reasons, in\n   174\torder of weight:\n   175\t\n   176\t1. **The log has no second reader and no second writer, ever.** Inside\n   177\t   `create-content-file` the stream is opened, replayed once by its own\n   178\t   handle, appended by its own `put`, and closed by its own `close`. No\n   179\t   cursor is handed out, no descriptor is projected, nothing attaches, and\n   180\t   the log never sits in any pool. Every property the contract exists to\n   181\t   guarantee — multiple independent cursors, non-destructive reads, portable\n   182\t   reachability, attachment lifecycle — is unused. A stream with exactly one\n   183\t   reader who is also its only writer is a file with a protocol wrapped\n   184\t   around it.\n   185\t2. **Making the on-disk layout a `dao.stream` transport made DaoJing's storage\n   186\t   format a public transport type.** `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}` is a\n   187\t   name any composition may open, so the record framing of a content file\n   188\t   became something other code could depend on. §Storage ignorance is about\n   189\t   the well not interpreting what it stores; the symmetric discipline is that\n   190\t   nothing outside the well interprets *how* it stores. Removing the transport\n   191\t   restores that: the framing is private to `dao.jing.file`.\n   192\t3. **A transport with one consumer owes the conformance suite for nobody.**\n   193\t   Option (a) would have this plan own a v2 append-log, its manifest, its\n   194\t   exclusion reasons, its inducers for every declared outcome, and its reader\n   195\t   laws on three hosts — for a transport whose sole consumer never mints a\n   196\t   second cursor. The stream plan deferred \"file, UDP, log\" transports until a\n   197\t   consumer needed them; DaoJing's file backend is not that consumer.\n   198\t4. **The parallel is `dao.jing.mem`.** The in-memory backend holds its content\n   199\t   in a private atom behind `:put-content-fn`/`:get-content-fn`; nobody\n   200\t   proposes that atom should be a stream. The file backend is the same handle\n   201\t   with the addition of durability. `dao.jing.md` already says \"backend\n   202\t   effects are explicit functions, not a protocol or hidden state\"; a hidden\n   203\t   stream inside the effect is the thing that sentence excludes.\n   204\t\n   205\t**Against Axiom 1.** \"All IO and data flow through append-only streams\" governs\n   206\tflow across boundaries — the intake pool is the stream boundary of DaoJing, and\n   207\tit is where every payload enters. The write behind `:put-content-fn` is the\n   208\tbackend effect at the far side of interpretation, the KV in Datomic's picture,\n   209\tnot a flow anyone observes. Nothing crosses a host boundary here that a second\n   210\tparty could read; there is no perspective on the log that is not DaoJing's own.\n   211\t\n   212\t**What it costs.** `dao.jing.file` gains a private framed-file section — open\n   213\tor create, truncate an incomplete tail, append a length-prefixed record and\n   214\tsync, replay every record — with a `#?(:cljd … :clj … :cljs …)` branch per host\n   215\t(`RandomAccessFile`, Node's synchronous `fs`, `dart:io`'s `RandomAccessFile`),\n   216\tlifted from `dao.stream.log` by copy, never by require. Roughly one hundred\n   217\tlines that currently live in a namespace slated for deletion with v1. The\n   218\ton-disk format is kept byte-for-byte — 4-byte big-endian length prefix, UTF-8\n   219\t`pr-str` of `[address payload]` — so every existing content file stays\n   220\treadable, and a test proves it against a golden record.\n   221\t\n   222\t**What it defers.** A v2 durable-log transport, to whichever plan brings its\n   223\tfirst real consumer: durable observer checkpoints, or a `dao.space` local\n   224\tstream that must survive a restart. If and when one exists, `dao.jing.file`\n   225\t*may* be re-based on it as a private choice; nothing in this plan requires or\n   226\tforbids that.\n   227\t\n   228\t**Option (c), considered and rejected:** a v2 file transport kept internal to\n   229\t`dao.jing.file` — never in a host dispatch table, never with a manifest. A\n   230\ttransport with no manifest and no conformance evidence is worse than no\n   231\ttransport: it wears the contract's interface without owing its laws.\n   232\t\n   233\t### Decision 3 — `dao.jing.remote`: remote content needs a driver its caller owns\n   234\t\n   235\t**The honest answer is yes.** A synchronous `get`/`put` over a polling RPC is\n   236\tnot implementable without waiting: on cljs and cljd there is nothing to wait\n   237\twith, and on the JVM a spin-poll inside `:get-content-fn` would be `take!!`\n   238\treintroduced by another name, diverging per host exactly where the contract\n   239\twas chosen to make hosts agree. So the v2 remote adapter does **not** produce\n   240\ta content handle. `jing/materialize!` and `jing/get` cannot be called against\n   241\ta remote store under v2. What replaces them is a client state machine the\n   242\tcaller steps, in the exact shape `yin.repl` already drives.\n   243\t\n   244\t**Server side — `dao.jing.v2.remote/default-handlers`.** The handler map is\n   245\talready the v2 shape: `dao.stream.apply/dispatch-request` calls\n   246\t`(apply handler args)` and wraps the value in a correlated response. The two\n   247\tops stay `:jing/put-content` (returns `:inserted` or `:present`) and\n   248\t`:jing/get-content` (returns the exact presence envelope\n   249\t`{:found? boolean, :value value}`; local not-found sentinels never cross the\n   250\twire). The address is validated against the payload hash before the local\n   251\tstore is touched, as today. A serving composition runs `apply/serve-once!` per\n   252\tattachment with this map. DaoJing owns the handlers; it does not own an\n   253\tendpoint — see *Boundary*.\n   254\t\n   255\tThe v1 `default-handlers` is duplicated (about twenty lines), not required:\n   256\tv1 `dao.jing.remote` requires `dao.stream.rpc.*` on the JVM, and pulling that\n   257\tinto a v2 namespace would make the new namespace v1-dependent. The duplication\n   258\tends at Phase J5.\n   259\t\n   260\t**Client side — a state machine, stepped by the composition.**\n   261\t\n   262\t```clojure\n   263\t(jing.v2.remote/client-state rpc-state)      ; wraps dao.stream.rpc client state; no cursor is fabricated\n   264\t(jing.v2.remote/request-put state address payload) ; => {:outcome k :state s' :id n?}\n   265\t(jing.v2.remote/request-get state address)         ; => {:outcome k :state s' :id n?}\n   266\t(jing.v2.remote/step state budget)                 ; => {:state s' :completions [...]}\n   267\t```\n   268\t\n   269\t- `request-put` and `request-get` validate the address as `jing/get` does —\n   270\t  a non-segment address is a defect in the caller and throws before any\n   271\t  request is allocated — then call `rpc/request!`. Their `:outcome` is the\n   272\t  RPC layer's: `requested`, `pending-request` (the writer answered `full`;\n   273\t  the envelope is retained and re-attempted on the next request or step),\n   274\t  `request-undeliverable`, or `terminal`. One addition: while `rpc/unsent?`\n   275\t  holds, both return `:dao.jing.v2.remote/busy` and allocate nothing, because\n   276\t  `rpc/request!` would silently retry the unsent envelope and ignore the new\n   277\t  operation. A caller must observe `busy` and try again after a step.\n   278\t- `step` calls `rpc/poll!` with the budget, takes completions and diagnostics\n   279\t  exactly once, and decodes each completion into DaoJing vocabulary:\n   280\t  `{:id n :op :jing/put-content :result :inserted}` or `:present`;\n   281\t  `{:id n :op :jing/get-content :found? true :value v}` or `:found? false`;\n   282\t  `{:id n :op … :error {code message}}` for a handler error or unknown\n   283\t  operation; `{:id n :op … :lost reason}` for a request the RPC layer reported\n   284\t  lost (`gap`, `/detached`, `/ended`, `/not-found`, `/transport-error`, or an\n   285\t  append that could not be delivered). A response whose value is not the exact\n   286\t  presence envelope, or not one of `:inserted`/`:present`, is a malformed\n   287\t  response and completes as `:error` with a `dao.jing.v2.remote/malformed-response`\n   288\t  code — the v1 rule that presence envelopes are exact, kept, but as data\n   289\t  rather than an exception, because it now surfaces inside a driver step.\n   290\t- Nothing here loops on `blocked`, holds a clock, retries on its own, or\n   291\t  touches a socket. Timeouts, reattachment (`rpc.ws/rebind` on `/detached`),\n   292\t  cadence, and what to do with a lost request are the driver's.\n   293\t- The namespace is pure `.cljc` with no host branch at all. The v1 adapter's\n   294\t  network client was JVM-only; the v2 client runs on all three hosts by\n   295\t  construction. That widening is the concrete gain of the polling shape.\n   296\t\n   297\t**The `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate.** `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` turns\n   298\t`{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :url …}` into a synchronous handle that\n   299\t`dao.space.index`'s published-index `defopen` then reads through `jing/get` to\n   300\trestore B-tree nodes. Under v2 that coordinate cannot yield a handle; it would\n   301\tyield a client state plus an `attach!` result plus a driver obligation — a\n   302\tcomposition, not a value. This plan leaves `dao.jing.coordinate` and its\n   303\t`:dao.jing/remote` branch **untouched on v1**. The v2 remote client has no\n   304\tcoordinate here. Reading remote content on the query side is the async\n   305\thydration `dao.data.btree.md` §5.4 defers \"until an async DaoJing backend\n   306\texists\" — this client is that backend, and the hydration and the coordinate\n   307\tthat opens into it belong to `dao.space`'s migration plan, whose index\n   308\t`defopen` is the only consumer.\n   309\t\n   310\t### Decision 4 — Namespace layout, in one table\n   311\t\n   312\t| namespace               | this plan                                          | v1 dependency after this plan | consumer that keeps v1 alive                          |\n   313\t| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n   314\t| `dao.jing`              | untouched; v1 observer stays until J5              | `dao.stream` (observer only)  | `dao.space.*` tests via `observe-step!` on v1 intakes |\n   315\t| `dao.jing.v2`           | **new** — the observer on v2                       | none                          | —                                                     |\n   316\t| `dao.jing.file`         | **in-place**: stream coupling removed              | none                          | —                                                     |\n   317\t| `dao.jing.mem`          | untouched                                          | none                          | —                                                     |\n   318\t| `dao.jing.remote`       | untouched                                          | `dao.stream.rpc.*` (clj)      | `dao.jing.coordinate`, `dao.space` tests              |\n   319\t| `dao.jing.v2.remote`    | **new** — handlers + client state machine          | none                          | —                                                     |\n   320\t| `dao.jing.coordinate`   | untouched                                          | via `dao.jing.remote` (clj)   | `dao.space.index`                                     |\n   321\t| `dao.jing.dht*`         | untouched                                          | `dao.stream.transit`, UDP     | out of scope (see *Boundary*)                         |\n   322\t\n   323\t**No registry, restated.** v1's `defopen`/`open!` is gone in v2 and nothing in\n   324\t`dao.jing*` reinvents it. `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` is already the precedent:\n   325\ta closed `case` over coordinate types, an explicit code change to add one. The\n   326\tv2 observer takes handles; the v2 remote takes an RPC state built from handles\n   327\tthe composition attached; neither looks anything up by name.\n   328\t\n   329\t**Reader-conditional discipline.** `dao.jing.file` keeps three-branch\n   330\t`#?(:cljd … :clj … :cljs …)` forms with `:cljd` first, as it does today.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '30,180p' collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | nl -ba -v30\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '30,180p' collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | nl -ba -v30\"","aggregated_output":"    30\t\n    31\t## The problem, by piece\n    32\t\n    33\t| piece                                                    | v1 mechanism                                                                                               | v2 counterpart                                                            |\n    34\t| -------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n    35\t| `dao.jing/observer-state`, `observe-step!`               | `ds/next` on any v1 reader; inline `{:position 0}` cursors; bare `:ok`/`:blocked`/`:end`/`:daostream/gap`  | reader surface: `cursor`, `next`; opaque cursors; outcome maps            |\n    36\t| `dao.jing.file/create-content-file`                      | `ds/open!` of `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}`; `ds/next` to replay; `ds/append!` + fsync; `ds/close!`     | **none** — the stream plan defers file/log transports                     |\n    37\t| `dao.jing.remote/connect-content!`, `content-client`     | `dao.stream.rpc.client/call!` — synchronous on the JVM, Promise/Future elsewhere; JVM-only constructor     | `dao.stream.rpc` — poll-shaped, state-machine-valued, all three hosts  |\n    38\t| `dao.jing.remote/default-handlers`                       | handler map `{op (fn [& args] value)}`                                                                     | same shape; `dao.stream.apply/dispatch-request` applies it unchanged   |\n    39\t| `dao.jing.mem`, `dao.jing.coordinate`, hashing, `materialize!`, `get`, `close!` | stream-free                                                                         | unchanged                                                                 |\n    40\t\n    41\tConsumers outside `dao.jing*` that reach these pieces, named so the boundary is\n    42\tvisible: `dao.space.index` (`jing-coordinate/open!` inside its `defopen`, and\n    43\tv1 `ds/append!` into the intake pool), `dao.space.schema`, `dao.space.transactor`,\n    44\t`dao.data.btree.storage` (handle API only), `yin.vm.space`, and the tests under\n    45\t`test/dao/space/` and `test/dao/data/` that drive the v1 observer over v1 intakes\n    46\tand open remote clients through `connect-content!`. None of them is migrated\n    47\there.\n    48\t\n    49\t## Strategy\n    50\t\n    51\tThe same two narrowings as the sibling plans, with one deliberate difference in\n    52\twhere the v2 namespace boundary falls.\n    53\t\n    54\t1. **Parallel where a v1 consumer still needs the v1 shape; in place where\n    55\t   nothing is being replaced.** The four prior consumers were rebuilt wholesale\n    56\t   beside their v1 twins because their entire surface was stream-shaped. Most\n    57\t   of `dao.jing` is not: content addressing, `materialize!`, `get`, `close!`\n    58\t   and `dao.jing.mem` never touch a stream. Duplicating them into a `dao.jing.v2`\n    59\t   would create a second minting path for content addresses — two sources of\n    60\t   truth for identity, which is worse than an unusual namespace layout. So:\n    61\t   - `dao.jing` core stays as it is. Its v1 observer functions stay untouched\n    62\t     until `dao.space` migrates, because `dao.space`'s tests drive them over v1\n    63\t     intakes today.\n    64\t   - The v2 observer is a new namespace, **`dao.jing.v2`**, requiring `dao.jing`\n    65\t     for `materialize!` and `dao.stream` for the reader surface. It is the\n    66\t     part of DaoJing that lives on the stream contract, and nothing else.\n    67\t   - The remote adapter is a new namespace, **`dao.jing.v2.remote`**, beside\n    68\t     the untouched v1 `dao.jing.remote`, which `dao.jing.coordinate` and the\n    69\t     `dao.space` tests keep using until their own plan.\n    70\t   - `dao.jing.file` is rewritten **in place**, because its migration is a\n    71\t     removal of a coupling, not a port (see *Decision 2*). There is no v2\n    72\t     transport to sit beside; its consumers use only `create-content-file` and\n    73\t     the handle API, which do not change.\n    74\t2. **The content-observer slice, and nothing else.** One observer, one durable\n    75\t   backend, one remote client state machine with its handlers. Not the\n    76\t   canonical encoding, not durable checkpoints, not a content-serving endpoint\n    77\t   as production infrastructure, not `dao.space`'s intake appends, not the DHT.\n    78\t\n    79\tNo compatibility facade, alias, or dual protocol. The v1 observer and v1\n    80\tremote are evidence about behavior, not constraints on the v2 shape.\n    81\t\n    82\t## Decisions\n    83\t\n    84\tSettled here so no phase decides them alone.\n    85\t\n    86\t### Decision 1 — The observer: entry shape, cursor discipline, gap\n    87\t\n    88\t**Entry shape.** A pool member is `{:stream <v2 reader handle>, :cursor <opaque>,\n    89\t:status s}`. The composition mints the cursor and hands both in:\n    90\t\n    91\t```clojure\n    92\t(jing.v2/observer-state [{:stream h1 :cursor c1} {:stream h2 :cursor c2}])\n    93\t;; => {:members [{:stream h1 :cursor c1 :status :pending} ...] :next 0}\n    94\t```\n    95\t\n    96\t`observer-state` no longer takes bare streams and fabricates `{:position 0}`.\n    97\tIt cannot: every valid cursor comes from the stream, and the anchor is the\n    98\tcomposition's policy — `:dao.stream/oldest` for a fresh pool, a kept cursor for\n    99\ta resumed one — not the observer's. A member without a cursor, or whose stream\n   100\tdoes not satisfy the reader surface, is a composition defect detected before\n   101\tany operation runs and throws, which is the one place the contract permits an\n   102\texception. `observer-state` accepts exactly the member shape it stores, so\n   103\t`(observer-state (:members st))` rebuilds a pool from a previous state; that is\n   104\thow a composition drops a member without touching another member's cursor.\n   105\t\n   106\t**Cursor discipline.** The observer retains exactly the successor `next`\n   107\treturns, after `materialize!` succeeds, and never anything else. There is no\n   108\tarithmetic on a cursor anywhere in `dao.jing.v2`, and no test inspects a\n   109\tcursor's shape — the conformance harness's own rule. The only other way a\n   110\tmember's cursor changes is `adopt-cursor`:\n   111\t\n   112\t```clojure\n   113\t(jing.v2/adopt-cursor state i cursor) ; cursor came from the stream: minted, successor, or gap recovery\n   114\t```\n   115\t\n   116\twhich is the composition passing back a value the stream handed out.\n   117\t\n   118\t**Signals are the contract's outcome set, total.** `observe-step!` returns\n   119\t`{:state s' :signal k ...}` where `k` is one of the seven keywords in\n   120\t`dao.stream/outcomes-next`, and no other:\n   121\t\n   122\t| signal                                                   | when                                                                   | extra keys                          | member cursor |\n   123\t| -------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------- |\n   124\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                                         | a payload was materialized                                             | `:address`                          | successor     |\n   125\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`                                    | the pool is empty, or every non-ended member is blocked                | —                                   | unchanged     |\n   126\t| `:dao.stream/end`                                        | every member has ended                                                 | —                                   | unchanged     |\n   127\t| `:dao.stream/gap`                                        | member `i`'s position was evicted                                      | `:member i`, `:cursor` (recovery)   | unchanged     |\n   128\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch`, `/invalid-cursor`, `/transport-error` | member `i`'s read failed                                   | `:member i`, `:result` (the outcome map) | unchanged |\n   129\t\n   130\tThe last four are reported immediately, the member's `:status` records the\n   131\toutcome, the scheduling index moves past that member, and the member is polled\n   132\tagain on its next turn — and reports again. That is v1's gap rule (\"never\n   133\tauto-resynchronized\") applied to every non-`ok`/`blocked`/`end` outcome, one\n   134\trule instead of four. The reason it is right for an intake pool: a `gap` on a\n   135\tcontent intake means payloads were appended, acknowledged to their publisher,\n   136\tand never materialized. Materialization is idempotent and replay-safe, so\n   137\tadopting the recovery cursor is *safe*; whether it is *acceptable* is a question\n   138\tonly the composition can answer, since it knows whether the publisher will\n   139\tre-append. The observer must not answer it. `cursor-mismatch` and\n   140\t`invalid-cursor` are composition defects; `transport-error` may be transient.\n   141\tAll three are resolved the same way: the composition adopts a cursor it obtained\n   142\tfrom the stream, or rebuilds the pool without that member. A test iterates\n   143\t`dao.stream/outcomes-next` and asserts every outcome produces a branch, so a\n   144\tfuture contract outcome fails the test rather than falling into a default.\n   145\t\n   146\t**Materialization failure is unchanged.** `materialize!` throwing propagates\n   147\tand leaves the caller-owned state untouched. The storage backend is not a\n   148\tstream and is not under the stream contract; its failures stay exceptions, as\n   149\t`dao.jing.md` §Materialization rule already specifies.\n   150\t\n   151\t**What changes in `dao.jing.md`, and what stays.** Changes: *The intake pool*\n   152\t— the entry shape sentence gains \"the cursor is minted by the composition from\n   153\tan anchor of its choosing, and is retained exactly as the stream returns it\";\n   154\t*Cursor tracking and recovery* — the four bare results become the seven\n   155\t`:dao.stream/…` outcomes with the table above, and the sentence about resync\n   156\tbeing the caller's decision is generalized to the three defect outcomes;\n   157\t*Implemented surface* — names `dao.jing.v2` for the observer and the\n   158\tstream-free file backend; *Open items* — durable checkpoints gain the sentence\n   159\tthat a checkpoint stores whatever cursor value the transport minted, and that\n   160\twhether such a value survives serialization is transport-owned and TBD in the\n   161\tcontract. Stays, untouched: *Definition*, *Publication from an agent*,\n   162\t*Materialization rule*, *Canonical encoding*, *Storage ignorance*, *Physical\n   163\tintake versus semantic composition*, *Reads*, *Resource lifecycle*, *Lineage*.\n   164\tNothing semantic about DaoJing moves; the migration changes how the observer\n   165\treads, not what it is.\n   166\t\n   167\t### Decision 2 — `dao.jing.file`: the durable log was never a stream (option b)\n   168\t\n   169\t**Decision.** The file backend's durable log is a storage implementation\n   170\tdetail. The migration removes the `dao.stream` coupling; it does not build a\n   171\tv2 append-log transport, and it does not use one.\n   172\t\n   173\t**Why, against §Storage ignorance and the passive well.** Four reasons, in\n   174\torder of weight:\n   175\t\n   176\t1. **The log has no second reader and no second writer, ever.** Inside\n   177\t   `create-content-file` the stream is opened, replayed once by its own\n   178\t   handle, appended by its own `put`, and closed by its own `close`. No\n   179\t   cursor is handed out, no descriptor is projected, nothing attaches, and\n   180\t   the log never sits in any pool. Every property the contract exists to\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '181,360p' collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | nl -ba -v181\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '181,360p' collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | nl -ba -v181\"","aggregated_output":"   181\t   guarantee — multiple independent cursors, non-destructive reads, portable\n   182\t   reachability, attachment lifecycle — is unused. A stream with exactly one\n   183\t   reader who is also its only writer is a file with a protocol wrapped\n   184\t   around it.\n   185\t2. **Making the on-disk layout a `dao.stream` transport made DaoJing's storage\n   186\t   format a public transport type.** `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}` is a\n   187\t   name any composition may open, so the record framing of a content file\n   188\t   became something other code could depend on. §Storage ignorance is about\n   189\t   the well not interpreting what it stores; the symmetric discipline is that\n   190\t   nothing outside the well interprets *how* it stores. Removing the transport\n   191\t   restores that: the framing is private to `dao.jing.file`.\n   192\t3. **A transport with one consumer owes the conformance suite for nobody.**\n   193\t   Option (a) would have this plan own a v2 append-log, its manifest, its\n   194\t   exclusion reasons, its inducers for every declared outcome, and its reader\n   195\t   laws on three hosts — for a transport whose sole consumer never mints a\n   196\t   second cursor. The stream plan deferred \"file, UDP, log\" transports until a\n   197\t   consumer needed them; DaoJing's file backend is not that consumer.\n   198\t4. **The parallel is `dao.jing.mem`.** The in-memory backend holds its content\n   199\t   in a private atom behind `:put-content-fn`/`:get-content-fn`; nobody\n   200\t   proposes that atom should be a stream. The file backend is the same handle\n   201\t   with the addition of durability. `dao.jing.md` already says \"backend\n   202\t   effects are explicit functions, not a protocol or hidden state\"; a hidden\n   203\t   stream inside the effect is the thing that sentence excludes.\n   204\t\n   205\t**Against Axiom 1.** \"All IO and data flow through append-only streams\" governs\n   206\tflow across boundaries — the intake pool is the stream boundary of DaoJing, and\n   207\tit is where every payload enters. The write behind `:put-content-fn` is the\n   208\tbackend effect at the far side of interpretation, the KV in Datomic's picture,\n   209\tnot a flow anyone observes. Nothing crosses a host boundary here that a second\n   210\tparty could read; there is no perspective on the log that is not DaoJing's own.\n   211\t\n   212\t**What it costs.** `dao.jing.file` gains a private framed-file section — open\n   213\tor create, truncate an incomplete tail, append a length-prefixed record and\n   214\tsync, replay every record — with a `#?(:cljd … :clj … :cljs …)` branch per host\n   215\t(`RandomAccessFile`, Node's synchronous `fs`, `dart:io`'s `RandomAccessFile`),\n   216\tlifted from `dao.stream.log` by copy, never by require. Roughly one hundred\n   217\tlines that currently live in a namespace slated for deletion with v1. The\n   218\ton-disk format is kept byte-for-byte — 4-byte big-endian length prefix, UTF-8\n   219\t`pr-str` of `[address payload]` — so every existing content file stays\n   220\treadable, and a test proves it against a golden record.\n   221\t\n   222\t**What it defers.** A v2 durable-log transport, to whichever plan brings its\n   223\tfirst real consumer: durable observer checkpoints, or a `dao.space` local\n   224\tstream that must survive a restart. If and when one exists, `dao.jing.file`\n   225\t*may* be re-based on it as a private choice; nothing in this plan requires or\n   226\tforbids that.\n   227\t\n   228\t**Option (c), considered and rejected:** a v2 file transport kept internal to\n   229\t`dao.jing.file` — never in a host dispatch table, never with a manifest. A\n   230\ttransport with no manifest and no conformance evidence is worse than no\n   231\ttransport: it wears the contract's interface without owing its laws.\n   232\t\n   233\t### Decision 3 — `dao.jing.remote`: remote content needs a driver its caller owns\n   234\t\n   235\t**The honest answer is yes.** A synchronous `get`/`put` over a polling RPC is\n   236\tnot implementable without waiting: on cljs and cljd there is nothing to wait\n   237\twith, and on the JVM a spin-poll inside `:get-content-fn` would be `take!!`\n   238\treintroduced by another name, diverging per host exactly where the contract\n   239\twas chosen to make hosts agree. So the v2 remote adapter does **not** produce\n   240\ta content handle. `jing/materialize!` and `jing/get` cannot be called against\n   241\ta remote store under v2. What replaces them is a client state machine the\n   242\tcaller steps, in the exact shape `yin.repl` already drives.\n   243\t\n   244\t**Server side — `dao.jing.v2.remote/default-handlers`.** The handler map is\n   245\talready the v2 shape: `dao.stream.apply/dispatch-request` calls\n   246\t`(apply handler args)` and wraps the value in a correlated response. The two\n   247\tops stay `:jing/put-content` (returns `:inserted` or `:present`) and\n   248\t`:jing/get-content` (returns the exact presence envelope\n   249\t`{:found? boolean, :value value}`; local not-found sentinels never cross the\n   250\twire). The address is validated against the payload hash before the local\n   251\tstore is touched, as today. A serving composition runs `apply/serve-once!` per\n   252\tattachment with this map. DaoJing owns the handlers; it does not own an\n   253\tendpoint — see *Boundary*.\n   254\t\n   255\tThe v1 `default-handlers` is duplicated (about twenty lines), not required:\n   256\tv1 `dao.jing.remote` requires `dao.stream.rpc.*` on the JVM, and pulling that\n   257\tinto a v2 namespace would make the new namespace v1-dependent. The duplication\n   258\tends at Phase J5.\n   259\t\n   260\t**Client side — a state machine, stepped by the composition.**\n   261\t\n   262\t```clojure\n   263\t(jing.v2.remote/client-state rpc-state)      ; wraps dao.stream.rpc client state; no cursor is fabricated\n   264\t(jing.v2.remote/request-put state address payload) ; => {:outcome k :state s' :id n?}\n   265\t(jing.v2.remote/request-get state address)         ; => {:outcome k :state s' :id n?}\n   266\t(jing.v2.remote/step state budget)                 ; => {:state s' :completions [...]}\n   267\t```\n   268\t\n   269\t- `request-put` and `request-get` validate the address as `jing/get` does —\n   270\t  a non-segment address is a defect in the caller and throws before any\n   271\t  request is allocated — then call `rpc/request!`. Their `:outcome` is the\n   272\t  RPC layer's: `requested`, `pending-request` (the writer answered `full`;\n   273\t  the envelope is retained and re-attempted on the next request or step),\n   274\t  `request-undeliverable`, or `terminal`. One addition: while `rpc/unsent?`\n   275\t  holds, both return `:dao.jing.v2.remote/busy` and allocate nothing, because\n   276\t  `rpc/request!` would silently retry the unsent envelope and ignore the new\n   277\t  operation. A caller must observe `busy` and try again after a step.\n   278\t- `step` calls `rpc/poll!` with the budget, takes completions and diagnostics\n   279\t  exactly once, and decodes each completion into DaoJing vocabulary:\n   280\t  `{:id n :op :jing/put-content :result :inserted}` or `:present`;\n   281\t  `{:id n :op :jing/get-content :found? true :value v}` or `:found? false`;\n   282\t  `{:id n :op … :error {code message}}` for a handler error or unknown\n   283\t  operation; `{:id n :op … :lost reason}` for a request the RPC layer reported\n   284\t  lost (`gap`, `/detached`, `/ended`, `/not-found`, `/transport-error`, or an\n   285\t  append that could not be delivered). A response whose value is not the exact\n   286\t  presence envelope, or not one of `:inserted`/`:present`, is a malformed\n   287\t  response and completes as `:error` with a `dao.jing.v2.remote/malformed-response`\n   288\t  code — the v1 rule that presence envelopes are exact, kept, but as data\n   289\t  rather than an exception, because it now surfaces inside a driver step.\n   290\t- Nothing here loops on `blocked`, holds a clock, retries on its own, or\n   291\t  touches a socket. Timeouts, reattachment (`rpc.ws/rebind` on `/detached`),\n   292\t  cadence, and what to do with a lost request are the driver's.\n   293\t- The namespace is pure `.cljc` with no host branch at all. The v1 adapter's\n   294\t  network client was JVM-only; the v2 client runs on all three hosts by\n   295\t  construction. That widening is the concrete gain of the polling shape.\n   296\t\n   297\t**The `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate.** `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` turns\n   298\t`{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :url …}` into a synchronous handle that\n   299\t`dao.space.index`'s published-index `defopen` then reads through `jing/get` to\n   300\trestore B-tree nodes. Under v2 that coordinate cannot yield a handle; it would\n   301\tyield a client state plus an `attach!` result plus a driver obligation — a\n   302\tcomposition, not a value. This plan leaves `dao.jing.coordinate` and its\n   303\t`:dao.jing/remote` branch **untouched on v1**. The v2 remote client has no\n   304\tcoordinate here. Reading remote content on the query side is the async\n   305\thydration `dao.data.btree.md` §5.4 defers \"until an async DaoJing backend\n   306\texists\" — this client is that backend, and the hydration and the coordinate\n   307\tthat opens into it belong to `dao.space`'s migration plan, whose index\n   308\t`defopen` is the only consumer.\n   309\t\n   310\t### Decision 4 — Namespace layout, in one table\n   311\t\n   312\t| namespace               | this plan                                          | v1 dependency after this plan | consumer that keeps v1 alive                          |\n   313\t| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n   314\t| `dao.jing`              | untouched; v1 observer stays until J5              | `dao.stream` (observer only)  | `dao.space.*` tests via `observe-step!` on v1 intakes |\n   315\t| `dao.jing.v2`           | **new** — the observer on v2                       | none                          | —                                                     |\n   316\t| `dao.jing.file`         | **in-place**: stream coupling removed              | none                          | —                                                     |\n   317\t| `dao.jing.mem`          | untouched                                          | none                          | —                                                     |\n   318\t| `dao.jing.remote`       | untouched                                          | `dao.stream.rpc.*` (clj)      | `dao.jing.coordinate`, `dao.space` tests              |\n   319\t| `dao.jing.v2.remote`    | **new** — handlers + client state machine          | none                          | —                                                     |\n   320\t| `dao.jing.coordinate`   | untouched                                          | via `dao.jing.remote` (clj)   | `dao.space.index`                                     |\n   321\t| `dao.jing.dht*`         | untouched                                          | `dao.stream.transit`, UDP     | out of scope (see *Boundary*)                         |\n   322\t\n   323\t**No registry, restated.** v1's `defopen`/`open!` is gone in v2 and nothing in\n   324\t`dao.jing*` reinvents it. `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` is already the precedent:\n   325\ta closed `case` over coordinate types, an explicit code change to add one. The\n   326\tv2 observer takes handles; the v2 remote takes an RPC state built from handles\n   327\tthe composition attached; neither looks anything up by name.\n   328\t\n   329\t**Reader-conditional discipline.** `dao.jing.file` keeps three-branch\n   330\t`#?(:cljd … :clj … :cljs …)` forms with `:cljd` first, as it does today.\n   331\t`dao.jing.v2` and `dao.jing.v2.remote` contain no host branch. No new file may\n   332\tcontain a bare `#?(:clj …)` form — `#?(:clj …)` does not exclude code from the\n   333\tcljd build; `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)` with `:cljd` first is the only safe spelling,\n   334\tand `:cljd` in tail position silently fails. Full cljd namespace compilation\n   335\tgates every phase.\n   336\t\n   337\t## Divergence register\n   338\t\n   339\tEvery place the v2 pieces deliberately differ from v1, with the reason.\n   340\t\n   341\t| v1 behavior                                                                                      | v2                                                                                                   | why                                                                                                   |\n   342\t| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   343\t| `observer-state` takes streams; cursor is `{:position 0}`                                        | takes `{:stream :cursor}` members; the composition mints the cursor                                  | every valid cursor comes from the stream; the anchor is the composition's policy                       |\n   344\t| signals `:ok`, `:blocked`, `:end`, `:daostream/gap`; malformed results throw                     | the seven `:dao.stream/…` outcomes; three defect outcomes reported as data with `:member` and `:result` | the result convention; an outcome outside the closed set still throws, as a transport defect         |\n   345\t| a gap is reported, cursor unchanged, never resynced                                              | same rule, extended to `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor`, `transport-error`; `adopt-cursor` is the resync | one rule for every non-progress outcome; resync is a composition decision                         |\n   346\t| `dao.jing.file` opens `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}`; handle carries `:log`                    | private framed file; handle carries `:path :state :write-lock` and the three fns, no `:log`          | Decision 2                                                                                            |\n   347\t| `file_test` counts records with `ds/next` over the raw log                                       | `dao.jing.file/records` returns the decoded records of a path; tests use it                          | the framing is private; the test reads through the backend's own reader                              |\n   348\t| `content-client` is a handle; `:get-content-fn` returns the value                                | `client-state` / `request-*` / `step`; results are completions correlated by id                       | no operation waits; unimplementable otherwise on cljs/cljd                                            |\n   349\t| `connect-content!` — synchronous, JVM-only                                                       | gone; the composition attaches through `dao.stream.ws` and builds the client from the whole `attach!` result | the constructor waited; attachment outcome arrives as deposited data                         |\n   350\t| malformed presence envelope throws inside `get`                                                  | completes as an `:error` with a malformed-response code                                              | it surfaces inside a driver step, where an exception has nowhere to go                                |\n   351\t| `close-fn` guards concurrent closes with a lock and retries on failure                           | gone with the handle; `close!` on the ws handle is idempotent and answers `ok`                       | the client holds no host resource; the handle does                                                    |\n   352\t| `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate opens to a handle                                                  | unchanged on v1; no v2 coordinate in this plan                                                       | it would open into a composition, which is `dao.space`'s to design with hydration                     |\n   353\t\n   354\t**v1-only tests deliberately not mirrored:** `file_test`'s direct\n   355\t`{:dao.stream/type :append-log}` opens and `count-records` (replaced by\n   356\t`records`); `remote_test`'s `network-invalid-url-test` (a bad URL is\n   357\t`invalid-descriptor` or a deposited `/transport-error`, tested in Phase J3b as\n   358\tdata), `close-failure-retry-test` (no close-fn to fail), and every\n   359\t`with-server` case built on `rpc-ws/start!` (replaced by the J3 compositions).\n   360\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v1\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v1\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Design\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: agreed design target. This document is the DaoStream contract. It does\n     4\tnot redesign Yin.VM. Yin.VM is a downstream consumer and will be addressed only\n     5\tafter this contract is implemented and tested.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Purpose\n     8\t\n     9\tThis contract derives from the foundational axioms and non-negotiable\n    10\tinvariants of [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Two axioms bear on it\n    11\tdirectly, named here once so the sections below can cite rather than restate\n    12\tthem:\n    13\t\n    14\t1. **Everything is a Stream** — all IO and data flow through append-only\n    15\t   streams.\n    16\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics** — data is syntax; semantics emerge\n    17\t   only through interpretation. One truth, many perspectives.\n    18\t\n    19\tDaoStream is the passive substrate for IO in datom.world — passive because\n    20\tinterpretation and execution must not collapse into one layer. Axiom 1 is\n    21\twhat makes this the layer every host boundary crosses. A stream carries\n    22\tvalues and decides nothing about them:\n    23\t\n    24\t- **What the values mean** is not its business — Axiom 2. An element\n    25\t  judged, filtered, or typed at this layer destroys the perspectives nobody\n    26\t  has taken yet.\n    27\t- **Which interpreter observes them** is not recorded — a registry of who\n    28\t  is listening would be hidden global state.\n    29\t- **When an interpreter runs** is not its decision — \"do not introduce\n    30\t  callbacks; every callback is events on a stream\", seen from the stream's\n    31\t  side: DaoStream invokes nothing, so control is never inverted and\n    32\t  causality stays explicit (see The IO Model).\n    33\t\n    34\tThe Invariants below are these denials in operational form.\n    35\t\n    36\tThe design separates five concepts:\n    37\t\n    38\t1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.\n    39\t2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the\n    40\t   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.\n    41\t3. A **logical-stream identity** is transport-independent plain data shared by\n    42\t   every handle and descriptor for one stream.\n    43\t4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\n    44\t   stream; it includes the logical-stream identity but is not that identity.\n    45\t5. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one\n    46\t   logical stream.\n    47\t\n    48\tCreation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification or an\n    49\tidentity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\n    50\tstate.\n    51\t\n    52\tSeven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see\n    53\tResult Convention):\n    54\t\n    55\t| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |\n    56\t|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|\n    57\t| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |\n    58\t| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\n    59\t| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\n    60\t| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\n    61\t| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |\n    62\t| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |\n    63\t| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |\n    64\t\n    65\tA handle implements the subset of the **handle surface** its transport\n    66\tdeclares (see Surfaces). `create!` and `attach!` are not part of it: they\n    67\tconsume no handle and produce one.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n    88\t\n    89\tEvery clause below must be consistent with these invariants, and most derive\n    90\tfrom one directly. Where a clause is instead an interoperability decision — the\n    91\tdescriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\n    92\tstated as a decision rather than dressed as a deduction. A proposed addition\n    93\tthat contradicts an invariant does not belong in this contract; one that merely\n    94\tcannot be deduced from them must say which decision it is and why.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Result Convention\n    97\t\n    98\tEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n    99\t\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   114\t  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n   115\t  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n   116\t  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions\n   117\t  and never callbacks.\n   118\t- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`.\n   119\t\n   120\t## The IO Model\n   121\t\n   122\tThree models are available for expressing IO. DaoStream is deliberately the\n   123\tthird.\n   124\t\n   125\t**Blocking (synchronous) IO.** The operation parks until it has an answer,\n   126\tthen returns it. Control flow stays sequential and the call site reads as\n   127\tordinary code, which is the whole of its appeal. The cost is that something\n   128\tmust wait — a thread per pending operation, held for its duration — and it\n   129\trequires a host that can park at all.\n   130\t\n   131\t**Callback (asynchronous) IO.** The operation registers a continuation and\n   132\treturns at once; the runtime invokes the continuation when the answer\n   133\tarrives. Nothing waits, so one thread carries many pending operations: this\n   134\tis the model that scales. Its cost is inverted control. The caller no longer\n   135\tdecides when it observes the answer, and the inversion is viral — on a host\n   136\twithout blocking, a promise cannot be absorbed inside a function, because\n   137\tawaiting returns to the caller at the first suspension point and makes the\n   138\tcaller's own result a promise in turn. Whatever is reached from a callback\n   139\tis reached from a callback.\n   140\t\n   141\t**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is\n   142\ttrue now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\n   143\tnothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.\n   144\tThis is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.\n   145\t\n   146\t`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream\n   147\thas a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\n   148\tposition holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n   149\tanswer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\n   150\tcallback; the Result Convention's \"never exceptions and never callbacks\" is\n   151\tthis model stated as a rule about results.\n   152\t\n   153\tThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\n   154\tcannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n   155\tcalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\n   156\tdata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\n   157\tDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\n   158\treported back. It is a question about now.\n   159\t\n   160\t### Why this model and not the other two\n   161\t\n   162\tThe hosts do not offer the same choices. The JVM has both blocking and\n   163\tasynchronous IO. JavaScript and Dart have only the asynchronous kind: there\n   164\tis no blocking read to call and no way to wait for one, because the single\n   165\tevent loop that would deliver the answer is the one a waiting call would be\n   166\toccupying — waiting deadlocks rather than merely costing a thread.\n   167\t\n   168\tA blocking contract is therefore unimplementable on most of the targets. A\n   169\tcallback contract is implementable on all of them and inverts control on all\n   170\tof them, including the host that never needed it. Non-blocking polling is\n   171\tthe only one of the three that means the same thing everywhere — the same\n   172\toperations, the same outcome sets, the same control flow — which is what\n   173\tlets one surface serve clj, cljs, and cljd without the shape of the API\n   174\tdiffering by host.\n   175\t\n   176\t### What it costs\n   177\t\n   178\tPolling without readiness notification is O(n) in streams observed: a reader\n   179\tholding ten thousand idle streams must ask all ten thousand to learn which\n   180\thave data. This is `select` without `select`, and it is a real limit rather\n   181\tthan an oversight — the extension that answers it is reserved below. The\n   182\tcost also lands where it can be paid: DaoStream schedules nothing, so\n   183\tcadence belongs to the runtime driving the interpreters, and a readiness\n   184\tmechanism belongs there too.\n   185\t\n   186\t### The readiness extension\n   187\t\n   188\tThis contract has no readiness or waiter extension. A reader that receives\n   189\t`:dao.stream/blocked` retries `next` when it chooses; retry cadence is the\n   190\tconcern of the interpreter or the runtime driving it, not of the stream.\n   191\t\n   192\tA future readiness extension would be additive: an optional protocol on\n   193\ttransports that support it, plus optional keys in operation results (for\n   194\texample a wake token list on `append!`), which the open-map rule already\n   195\tpermits. Nothing in this contract needs to change for that; no consumer of\n   196\tthis contract may depend on it existing.\n   197\t\n   198\t## Creation and Attachment\n   199\t\n   200\tCreation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an\n   201\texisting one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.\n   202\t\n   203\t- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a\n   204\t  new logical stream. `descriptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\n   205\t  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\n   206\t  `:dao.stream/identity`, the transport-independent logical-stream identity.\n   207\t  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\n   208\t  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\n   209\t  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n   210\t  no failure outcome.\n   211\t- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\n   212\t  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching\n   213\t  never creates.\n   214\t- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only\n   215\t  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a\n   216\t  request to load a plugin.\n   217\t\n   218\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization\n   219\tboundary. An in-memory stream whose admission declaration permits host\n   220\treferences can carry a handle intact. A dynamically delivered handle travels\n   221\tas composition data together with its declared surface, for example\n   222\t`{:dao.stream/handle h :dao.stream/surface #{:reader :writer}}`; the receiving\n   223\tinterpreter uses only that declared surface, and attaching to what it already\n   224\tholds is meaningless. A composition may omit the wrapper only when the\n   225\treceiver's fixed contract already declares the surface. But a handle does not\n   226\tsurvive encoding, and any\n   227\ttransport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —\n   228\tis a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those\n   229\tboundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a\n   230\ttransport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a\n   231\tvalue it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)\n   232\t`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and\n   233\tresolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the\n   234\tstreams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the\n   235\tfiles a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no\n   236\tregistry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving\n   237\tsomewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n   238\thost, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —\n   239\thonestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.\n   240\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '241,480p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v241\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '241,480p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v241\"","aggregated_output":"   241\t### Envelopes\n   242\t\n   243\tA **creation specification** is a map with one required key:\n   244\t\n   245\t- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the\n   246\t  dispatch key.\n   247\t\n   248\tEvery other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace\n   249\t(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler\n   250\t— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its\n   251\town keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.\n   252\t\n   253\tThe **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\n   254\treference. It is reachability data, not the logical-stream identity itself.\n   255\tIts exact definition is not designed yet; these properties are settled, and\n   256\tthe key set that realizes them is TBD:\n   257\t\n   258\t- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch\n   259\t  key.\n   260\t- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   261\t  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n   262\t  the same logical stream through different endpoints; their reachability data\n   263\t  may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n   264\t  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n   265\t- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is\n   266\t  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is\n   267\t  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n   268\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.\n   269\t- A transport whose attachments are distinguishable assigns each attachment\n   270\t  an opaque, structurally serializable value, unique among the attachments\n   271\t  that transport can tell apart. Every attachment handle the transport mints\n   272\t  has such an identity, whether the handle is returned by `attach!` or\n   273\t  minted from a host connection accepted without an `attach!` call. An\n   274\t  `attach!` success map carries the value under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   275\t  When no `attach!` call produces the handle, the transport specification\n   276\t  names the deposited event or other stream-native mechanism by which the\n   277\t  composition receives the identity. Wherever an answer or event for that\n   278\t  attachment is displaced onto another channel, the same value correlates it\n   279\t  there. The key and correlation rule are the contract's; minting,\n   280\t  representation, and the server-side delivery mechanism are the\n   281\t  transport's. Transports that cannot distinguish attachments omit it.\n   282\t- It carries no authorization. Nothing in this contract gates who may attach:\n   283\t  a descriptor is a name, and a host that can reach what it names attaches.\n   284\t  Whether attachment should be gated at all, and by what, is a question this\n   285\t  contract does not answer and does not reserve room for — an outcome or key\n   286\t  held open for an undesigned mechanism constrains that mechanism's design\n   287\t  from a document with no business specifying it. Should gating arrive, it\n   288\t  arrives as an addition, which the open-map rule and this section's own\n   289\t  transport-owned keys already permit.\n   290\t\n   291\tFor the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`\n   292\ton a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,\n   293\ta value that can be sent on a stream and reconstructed on the other end, and\n   294\t`:dao.stream/identity`, equal through every handle and descriptor for the\n   295\tlogical stream,\n   296\twhere `attach!` on the reconstructed value attaches to the stream it names —\n   297\tprovided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and\n   298\tAttachment on reachability), and allowing that a transport which cannot know\n   299\tthat at call time reports it later as data.\n   300\t\n   301\tBoth envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive\n   302\tthe host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is\n   303\tstructural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or\n   304\tidentity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —\n   305\ta consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.\n   306\t\n   307\t`create!` outcomes:\n   308\t\n   309\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |\n   310\t|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   311\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists, its identity settled at once. Implies not that every resource backing it has finished being acquired. | `:dao.stream/handle` |\n   312\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |\n   313\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |\n   314\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |\n   315\t\n   316\t`attach!` outcomes:\n   317\t\n   318\t| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |\n   319\t|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   320\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle exists through which this attachment is operated. Implies neither that a far end has confirmed the stream it names nor that establishment has completed. Where either answer is one only the far end can give, it arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\n   321\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |\n   322\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |\n   323\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |\n   324\t\n   325\tWhere a transport acquires its medium through a process rather than at once —\n   326\tbinding a port, opening a handle through an API that only answers later — `ok`\n   327\tstill means the logical stream exists: its identity is minted, its sequence is\n   328\tempty, and the handle is operable. What it does not mean is that the stream can\n   329\tyet accept values. The handle reports that through its own operations, exactly\n   330\tas any other transient condition is reported, and the result of the acquisition\n   331\tarrives as data.\n   332\t\n   333\tThis is permitted only where logical existence is genuinely independent of the\n   334\tresource. A transport that cannot mint identity and lifecycle before its\n   335\tacquisition completes is not creating a stream and deferring its medium; it is\n   336\tdeferring the creation itself, and `ok` would be false. Such a transport does\n   337\tnot implement `create!` — what it needs is a request whose result arrives\n   338\tlater, which is an ordinary interpreter over streams, not an operation here.\n   339\t\n   340\tCreation defers differently from attachment, and the difference is why this is\n   341\ta caveat rather than a new outcome. `attach!` defers a **remote confirmation**:\n   342\tan answer only a far end can give, which is why it arrives as deposited data.\n   343\t`create!` has no far end. What it defers is **local acquisition**, whose\n   344\tfailure surfaces through the handle the caller already holds. A \"not yet\"\n   345\toutcome would have to return no handle — leaving the caller an identity it\n   346\tcannot operate, and nothing to retry against.\n   347\t\n   348\t### Host Dispatch\n   349\t\n   350\tWhen a creation specification or portable descriptor arrives dynamically —\n   351\tfor example off a stream — the host looks at it and dispatches on\n   352\t`:dao.stream/type`. The type resolves to a map that has the transport's\n   353\t`:dao.stream/create` and `:dao.stream/attach` implementations:\n   354\t\n   355\t```clojure\n   356\t{:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!\n   357\t                         :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}\n   358\t :dao.stream/ws         {:dao.stream/attach ws/attach!}}\n   359\t```\n   360\t\n   361\tThe entries are per-transport and may be partial. A transport that creates\n   362\tno streams supplies no `:dao.stream/create` — the WebSocket transport owns\n   363\tno stream, it serves streams the host composed, so nothing there creates\n   364\tone. A creation specification naming it finds no create implementation and\n   365\treturns `:dao.stream/not-found`, the same answer as an absent transport and\n   366\tthe correct one: nothing here creates that.\n   367\t\n   368\tEntry values are **host-composed**: ordinarily closures produced by a\n   369\ttransport constructor that already received whatever composition state the\n   370\ttransport needs — a boundary's deposit destination and its admission\n   371\tdeclaration, a directory some composition keeps. The bare vars above are\n   372\tschematic. A bare namespace var could reach that state only through namespace\n   373\tglobals, which is the hidden state this contract retires.\n   374\t\n   375\tThis table is ordinary data owned by the host, and the dispatch is an\n   376\tordinary map lookup done by the host — DaoStream neither performs nor\n   377\tstandardizes it. A host that cannot dispatch on `:dao.stream/type` returns\n   378\t`:dao.stream/not-found` — never creation or discovery.\n   379\t\n   380\tCode that knows its transport statically calls the transport's functions\n   381\tdirectly; no dispatch is involved. There is no ambient namespace-global\n   382\tregistry and no namespace-load registration side effect.\n   383\t\n   384\t## Surfaces\n   385\t\n   386\tA handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can\n   387\thonor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n   388\t(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\n   389\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   390\t\n   391\t`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it with both\n   392\treachability and logical-stream identity, because both outlive any attachment.\n   393\tAn operation a handle does not\n   394\tdeclare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there\n   395\tis no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n   396\tasked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may\n   397\tanswer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n   398\toperation exists.\n   399\t\n   400\tA transport may also offer operations of its own beside the public surface —\n   401\tanswering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n   402\ttrails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n   403\toperations, and nothing here defines or constrains them. **Code written\n   404\tagainst this contract may not reach for one**: it must work on any transport\n   405\tdeclaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n   406\tdefinition absent from some of them.\n   407\t\n   408\tAn interpreter may still use one, on the same terms as any other thing a\n   409\tcomposition supplies. What it may not do is *find* it — reach through a handle\n   410\tfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\n   411\tit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\n   412\tcomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\n   413\tpolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\n   414\tthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n   415\tparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n   416\tcontract-generic may be built on it.\n   417\t\n   418\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   419\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   420\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   421\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   422\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   423\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   424\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   425\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   426\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   427\tdefect. (`dao.stream.ws.md` specifies one such transport and the admission\n   428\trule in detail.)\n   429\t\n   430\tA boundary that can no longer deposit must make itself **observably gone**. Its\n   431\tone permitted action is closing the host resource it itself holds; it invokes\n   432\tnothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing. This is the sole\n   433\taction a boundary adapter takes beyond appending, and it is not a judgement\n   434\tabout any event — it is the same action for every cause. One corner is\n   435\tirreducible: when the deposit that failed was the boundary's only channel, the\n   436\trecord of its own departure has nowhere to land, and the failure is observable\n   437\tonly to whoever holds the deposit result and through subsequent operations on\n   438\tthe handle.\n   439\t\n   440\tA transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it\n   441\texcludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is\n   442\timpossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never\n   443\treports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n   444\tevicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a reported `gap` and never\n   445\tas `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\n   446\tthat cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An\n   447\texclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.\n   448\t\n   449\t### Where the asynchrony goes\n   450\t\n   451\tThe IO Model makes this contract non-blocking; hosts are asynchronous\n   452\twhether or not it is. A network message arrives when the runtime says so, in\n   453\ta callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does\n   454\tnot pretend otherwise; it confines the inversion to one place. The host's\n   455\tboundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what\n   456\tarrived. Past that append the event holds a position in a sequence, and\n   457\tevery reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,\n   458\twhen it chooses.\n   459\t\n   460\tThis is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but\n   461\tthat the inversion spreads through everything it touches; depositing into a\n   462\tretained sequence pays that cost once, at the boundary, and returns ordinary\n   463\tcode to everyone above. The reader-surface rule above is the same thing\n   464\tstated structurally: a transport that retains nothing has no reader surface,\n   465\tand its inbound events are deposited onto a stream that does.\n   466\t\n   467\t## Cursors\n   468\t\n   469\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   470\t\n   471\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   472\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   473\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   474\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   475\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   476\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   477\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   478\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   479\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   480\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
