{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '481,680p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v481\"","aggregated_output":"   481\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   482\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   483\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   484\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   485\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   486\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   487\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   488\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   489\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   490\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   491\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   492\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   493\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   494\t- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n   495\t  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n   496\t  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n   497\t  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n   498\t- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   499\t  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n   500\t  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives\n   501\t  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the\n   502\t  descriptor envelope.\n   503\t- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   504\t  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n   505\t  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n   506\t- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n   507\t  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a\n   508\t  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.\n   509\t- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and\n   510\t  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a\n   511\t  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.\n   512\t\n   513\t## Reading\n   514\t\n   515\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   516\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   517\tanswer.\n   518\t\n   519\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   520\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   521\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   522\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   523\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   524\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   525\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   526\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   527\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   528\t\n   529\tReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\n   530\tposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\n   531\tother. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no\n   532\ttake. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one\n   533\treader's progress every other reader's data loss.\n   534\t\n   535\t## Writing\n   536\t\n   537\t`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\n   538\t\n   539\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\n   540\t|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\n   541\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n   542\t| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n   543\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |\n   546\t\n   547\tAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\n   548\thandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\n   549\tring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\n   550\ta transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered\n   551\toutbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,\n   552\tnot here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n   553\tanswer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n   554\t\n   555\tThe writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\n   556\tretry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.\n   557\t\n   558\t## Close\n   559\t\n   560\t`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome set is `{ok}` — no\n   561\tother outcome exists for it. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope\n   562\tis also `:dao.stream/ok`.\n   563\t\n   564\t`ok` is the local lifecycle transition, not a report on releasing host\n   565\tresources: what the handle is on is closed from that moment, irrevocably.\n   566\tA transport whose close must also flush or release something that can fail\n   567\tdeclares where that failure surfaces — an ordinary deposit on a channel it\n   568\tnames — because the outcome set has no room for it and the transition has\n   569\talready happened.\n   570\t\n   571\t`close!` closes **what the handle is on**. A handle explicitly designated by\n   572\tthe transport as the logical stream's owner closes that logical stream; a\n   573\tserver-side accepted-connection handle is an attachment and does not gain\n   574\townership merely by being on the serving host. An attached\n   575\thandle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the\n   576\tstream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin\n   577\tlater. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared\n   578\tnature.\n   579\t\n   580\t`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of\n   581\twhat *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\n   582\tand `cursor-mismatch` is logical-stream-relative.) After an attachment\n   583\tcloses, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers\n   584\t`ok` with reachability and identity — both outlive any attachment; a reader surface, where the\n   585\thandle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this\n   586\thandle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are\n   587\tminted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,\n   588\tnot to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.\n   589\t\n   590\tClosing an attachment freezes what that handle can still observe: values\n   591\tappended to the logical stream afterwards are not observable through it, so\n   592\t`end` is reached at the position the stream had reached when the attachment\n   593\tclosed. And a logical stream that has itself been closed remains attachable —\n   594\t`attach!` answers `ok` and the new handle reads retained history to `end` —\n   595\tbecause close changes future availability, not identity or reachability.\n   596\t\n   597\tClosing the logical stream changes future availability only:\n   598\t\n   599\t- Subsequent `append!` returns `:dao.stream/closed`.\n   600\t- A reader that exhausts retained history receives `:dao.stream/end` instead\n   601\t  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.\n   602\t- Retained history remains readable. Close erases nothing; eviction is a\n   603\t  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.\n   604\t\n   605\tThere is no `closed?` predicate in the public surface. Operation results are\n   606\tauthoritative; a predicate answer is stale the moment it is returned.\n   607\t\n   608\t## Concurrency\n   609\t\n   610\tEach logical stream is one append-only sequence, and a handle's writer\n   611\tsurface is on exactly one sequence — for some transports the logical stream\n   612\titself, for others the ordered outbound path toward a stream elsewhere (see\n   613\tWriting). A successfully appended value occupies exactly one definite position\n   614\tin the sequence this handle's writer surface is on, and every operation's\n   615\toutcome reflects that sequence at one moment. DaoStream guarantees nothing\n   616\tfurther about concurrent operations.\n   617\t\n   618\tThis follows from Axiom 2. The sequence a handle's surfaces are on is the one\n   619\ttruth for that handle; each outcome is one perspective on it at one moment. A\n   620\treader that observes\n   621\t`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a\n   622\tcontradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global\n   623\tview exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is\n   624\tanswered by the sequence the handle's writer surface is on — the value has a\n   625\tposition in it or it does not — and where that sequence is not observable from\n   626\tthis handle, the answer is read where it is.\n   627\tWhat an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —\n   628\ta merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,\n   629\tand interpretation happens above the stream.\n   630\t\n   631\t## Retention and Gaps\n   632\t\n   633\tRetention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\n   634\tstream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records\n   635\tthem through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\n   636\tthe oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\n   637\tbuffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\n   638\t`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n   639\tpermanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\n   640\toutcomes and standardizes no policy.\n   641\t\n   642\t**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\n   643\tpolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\n   644\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n   645\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   646\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   647\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   648\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a reader that falls behind is\n   649\ttold what it missed.\n   650\t\n   651\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   652\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   653\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   654\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   655\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader must be able to know it\n   656\tmissed values.\n   657\t\n   658\t## Composition\n   659\t\n   660\tDaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything\n   661\tthat transforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its\n   662\town cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely\n   663\toutside this contract, needing no support from it. As in a Unix pipeline, the\n   664\tpipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes on either end.\n   665\t\n   666\t## Explicitly Absent\n   667\t\n   668\tAbsent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants:\n   669\t\n   670\t- **an ambient registry** — a namespace-global dispatch table, or registration\n   671\t  as a load-time side effect. Host dispatch is ordinary data the host owns.\n   672\t- **a `closed?` predicate** — a predicate answer is stale the moment it\n   673\t  returns; operation results are authoritative.\n   674\t- **destructive reads** — a read that removes what it observes makes one\n   675\t  reader's progress every other reader's data loss.\n   676\t- **`seek`** — every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and\n   677\t  kept cursors cover repositioning.\n   678\t- **a blocking take** — no operation waits.\n   679\t- **waiter registration** — there is no readiness extension.\n   680\t- **throwing conveniences** — operational outcomes are data.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
