{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,200p' docs/design/dao.jing.md | nl -ba -v1\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,200p' docs/design/dao.jing.md | nl -ba -v1\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoJing: The Content-Addressed Storage Observer\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: implemented. The observer (`observer-state` / `observe-step!`) and the\n     4\tplain-data content-store handles described here are the current\n     5\t`src/cljc/dao/jing*.cljc` code. What remains open — the final canonical\n     6\tencoding, durable observer checkpoints, explicit materialization\n     7\tacknowledgement, garbage collection, and async hydration — is listed under\n     8\t*Open items and current limitations*.\n     9\t\n    10\t**Related documents:**\n    11\t\n    12\t- `docs/design/dao.space.md` — the tuple space built by interpreters above\n    13\t  storage\n    14\t- `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` — the owner-side covered-index publisher\n    15\t- `docs/design/dao.space.query.md` — the reader-side index consumer\n    16\t- `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md` — the DHT distribution backend\n    17\t- `docs/design/dao.stream.md` — the append-only stream primitive\n    18\t- `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` — the covered-index node format\n    19\t- `docs/design/datom.md` — the datom and tuple specification\n    20\t- `docs/design/adr/0001-dao-space-as-storage-boundary.md` — the storage-boundary\n    21\t  decision\n    22\t- `docs/datomic.md` — the Datomic storage architecture that informs the\n    23\t  separation of storage and interpretation\n    24\t\n    25\t## Definition\n    26\t\n    27\tA `dao.jing` observes an explicit pool of intake `dao.stream` values and\n    28\tmaterializes their elements into content-addressed key-value storage.\n    29\t\n    30\tFor every opaque payload `x` arriving through any stream in the pool, DaoJing\n    31\tperforms the same storage operation:\n    32\t\n    33\t```text\n    34\tbytes = canonical-encode(x)\n    35\tkey   = content-hash(bytes)\n    36\tKV[key] = x                 ; insert if absent\n    37\t```\n    38\t\n    39\tDaoJing knows how to:\n    40\t\n    41\t- observe the streams in its configured pool;\n    42\t- retain the operational cursor needed to continue reading each stream;\n    43\t- canonically encode an element;\n    44\t- derive its content address;\n    45\t- insert it idempotently into the materialization target; and\n    46\t- retrieve content by its address.\n    47\t\n    48\tDaoJing does not know whether an element is a datom, B-tree node, covered\n    49\tindex, manifest, program, image, or any other kind of value. Meaning belongs\n    50\tto the interpreter that produced or consumes the content.\n    51\t\n    52\tThis makes DaoJing suitable storage for **any point in Datom.world's moduli\n    53\tspace of database interpreters**. DaoSpace can store covered indexes and\n    54\tindexed snapshots in it; DaoField can store metric indexes; another interpreter\n    55\tcan store graph, document, columnar, model, or other materializations. All are\n    56\topaque values at this boundary.\n    57\t\n    58\tDaoJing is itself an interpretation of DaoStream, but only at the\n    59\trepresentation level: it interprets each emitted value as content to encode,\n    60\thash, and materialize in a key/value store. That operation assigns content\n    61\tidentity and retrieval semantics while remaining devoid of domain semantics.\n    62\tDaoJing is therefore shared storage beneath the database moduli space, not a\n    63\tsemantic database point within it.\n    64\t\n    65\tDaoJing maintains no membership registry, no mutable roots, no CAS records,\n    66\tand no delete operation. Its only object of discourse is the strict content\n    67\taddress `:segment/sha256-<hash>`, and its only semantics are insert-if-absent\n    68\tmaterialization plus content reads.\n    69\t\n    70\t## The intake pool\n    71\t\n    72\tThe pool contains the streams through which content is submitted for\n    73\tmaterialization. It is supplied explicitly when the observer state is\n    74\tconstructed. DaoJing performs no stream registration or discovery and\n    75\tmaintains no shared membership record.\n    76\t\n    77\tThe pool is an intake topology, not a semantic namespace. DaoJing does not use\n    78\tthe identity of the source stream when deriving an element's key, and it does\n    79\tnot attach source identity to the stored value:\n    80\t\n    81\t```text\n    82\tstream A emits x ─┐\n    83\t                  ├── hash(canonical-encode(x)) ──► the same KV entry\n    84\tstream B emits x ─┘\n    85\t```\n    86\t\n    87\tIdentical content therefore converges on the same address regardless of which\n    88\tpool member carried it. Pool order, source identity, and arrival order do not\n    89\tchange content identity.\n    90\t\n    91\tDaoJing must distinguish pool members operationally long enough to maintain a\n    92\tcursor for each one. That association is observer state only. It is not\n    93\twritten into the content address or materialized payload and has no semantic\n    94\tmeaning. In the implemented observer each member entry is plain data —\n    95\t`{:stream <ref>, :cursor {:position n}, :status s}` — an operational record,\n    96\tnothing more.\n    97\t\n    98\tIf a storage backend partitions content into physical buckets or shards, that\n    99\tplacement is a backend concern, normally derived from the content hash. It is\n   100\tnot derived from the source stream.\n   101\t\n   102\t## Publication from an agent\n   103\t\n   104\tAn agent writes ordinary working data to its local `dao.stream`. That local\n   105\tstream is not automatically a member of DaoJing's intake pool.\n   106\t\n   107\tWhen the agent calls `dao.space.index/publish-index!`, the index publisher:\n   108\t\n   109\t1. reads the agent's local stream;\n   110\t2. constructs the covered indexes;\n   111\t3. selects an intake stream from the DaoJing pool;\n   112\t4. appends the immutable index payloads, including the top-level manifest, to\n   113\t   that intake stream; and\n   114\t5. returns the manifest and its content address required by the publishing\n   115\t   layer.\n   116\t\n   117\t```text\n   118\tagent-local dao.stream\n   119\t        │\n   120\t        │ dao.space.index/publish-index!\n   121\t        ▼\n   122\tselected DaoJing intake stream\n   123\t        │\n   124\t        │ opaque payloads\n   125\t        ▼\n   126\tDaoJing observer ── canonical encode + hash ──► content-addressed KV\n   127\t```\n   128\t\n   129\tSelecting an intake stream is an operational routing decision. It does not\n   130\tbecome part of an index payload's identity. The same index payload published\n   131\tthrough a different intake stream receives the same address.\n   132\t\n   133\tPublication acknowledges that every payload was appended to the selected\n   134\tintake stream. It does not acknowledge that an asynchronous observer has yet\n   135\tmaterialized those payloads; see *Open items and current limitations*.\n   136\t\n   137\tPublication changes representation and access cost, not the meaning of the\n   138\tcovered data. Consumers whose explicit read coordinates name the published\n   139\tmanifest address can consume the persisted covered indexes.\n   140\t\n   141\t## Materialization rule\n   142\t\n   143\tThe materialization is an idempotent union of content-addressed values. If\n   144\t`H(x)` is the address of the canonical encoding of `x`, observing `x` means:\n   145\t\n   146\t```clojure\n   147\t(assoc-if-absent target (H x) x)\n   148\t```\n   149\t\n   150\tRe-observing the same value is a no-op. Observing equal values through\n   151\tdifferent pool streams is also a no-op after the first insertion. Consequently:\n   152\t\n   153\t- replay is safe;\n   154\t- pool streams may be consumed in any interleaving;\n   155\t- duplicate publication is harmless;\n   156\t- immutable content writes do not require cross-stream coordination; and\n   157\t- a materialization can be reconstructed by replaying the available pool\n   158\t  streams.\n   159\t\n   160\tThe implemented write is `dao.jing/materialize!`: it derives the address from\n   161\tthe payload alone (`segment-key`) and asks the backend's `:put-content-fn`\n   162\tfor an explicit verdict. `:inserted` means the value is durably stored now;\n   163\t`:present` means an equal value is already stored there, in which case the\n   164\tstored value is read back and verified. A collision in which an existing\n   165\taddress contains different canonical bytes is an integrity failure, not an\n   166\toverwrite, and throws loudly.\n   167\t\n   168\t### Canonical encoding\n   169\t\n   170\tContent addressing is meaningful only when equal supported values produce the\n   171\tsame bytes on every participating platform. The canonical encoder is therefore\n   172\tpart of the storage contract even though the meaning of the encoded value is\n   173\tnot.\n   174\t\n   175\tCanonicalization may understand representation-level structure such as maps,\n   176\tsets, numbers, strings, and byte arrays. It must not understand domain concepts\n   177\tsuch as datoms, index orders, manifests, or any notion of a root.\n   178\t\n   179\tThe target encoding is a canonical flat byte representation suitable for\n   180\tcross-platform hashing and in-place reading. The current implementation uses\n   181\tan order-normalized `pr-str` as a transitional encoder — deterministic and\n   182\torder-insensitive, but not yet the pinned canonical byte encoding. This is the\n   183\tfirst open item under *Open items and current limitations*.\n   184\t\n   185\t## Storage ignorance\n   186\t\n   187\tDaoJing assigns identity to opaque content but does not interpret the data\n   188\tstructure stored at that identity. In particular, it does not:\n   189\t\n   190\t- traverse B-trees;\n   191\t- distinguish EAVT, AEVT, AVET, or VAET nodes;\n   192\t- merge indexes;\n   193\t- evaluate Datalog;\n   194\t- infer authorship or provenance from the intake stream;\n   195\t- discover which streams or published indexes a query should read;\n   196\t- construct a tuple space; or\n   197\t- maintain a root, CAS, or delete surface.\n   198\t\n   199\tThose responsibilities remain above the storage boundary. This allows a new\n   200\timmutable data structure to be introduced without changing DaoJing.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
