{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '201,361p' docs/design/dao.jing.md | nl -ba -v201\"","aggregated_output":"   201\t\n   202\t## Physical intake versus semantic composition\n   203\t\n   204\tTwo forms of multi-stream work must remain separate:\n   205\t\n   206\t- **Physical intake:** DaoJing observes every stream in its configured pool and\n   207\t  content-addresses their opaque elements into the KV materialization.\n   208\t- **Semantic composition:** `dao.space.query` receives an explicit collection\n   209\t  of published sources and decides how their covered datoms are folded and\n   210\t  interpreted.\n   211\t\n   212\tDaoJing owns the first operation. It has no knowledge of the second. Observing\n   213\tmany intake streams does not mean that DaoJing semantically merges their\n   214\tcontents.\n   215\t\n   216\t## Reads\n   217\t\n   218\tThe storage read operation resolves a strict content address to the exact\n   219\topaque value stored at that address. `dao.jing/get` accepts only\n   220\t`:segment/sha256-<64 lowercase hex>` addresses; arbitrary keys and mutable\n   221\troots are outside DaoJing and throw before a backend is consulted.\n   222\t\n   223\tA reader may access the target locally or through a remote transport\n   224\t(`dao.jing.remote`, `dao.jing.dht`). Location changes how bytes are obtained,\n   225\tnot how their identity or meaning is determined.\n   226\t\n   227\tHigher layers expose the semantic compositions the stream participates in. A\n   228\tquery reads a published index descriptor containing a serializable\n   229\tcontent-store coordinate plus an immutable manifest address.\n   230\t`dao.jing.coordinate/open!` interprets coordinates such as\n   231\t`{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file :path ...}` or,\n   232\ton the JVM, `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :url ...}` into local handles;\n   233\tunsupported coordinates fail closed. These coordinates are caller-supplied\n   234\tvalues, not state inferred from storage. DaoJing does not infer a source or\n   235\tcoordinate from the stream that carried a payload, and no content address is\n   236\tever treated as a mutable root.\n   237\t\n   238\t## Cursor tracking and recovery\n   239\t\n   240\tAn observer retains one cursor per active pool member and repeatedly calls\n   241\t`ds/next`:\n   242\t\n   243\t- `{:ok payload, :cursor next-cursor}`: materialize the payload and advance\n   244\t  that stream's cursor;\n   245\t- `:blocked`: no payload is currently available from that stream;\n   246\t- `:end`: that stream is closed; and\n   247\t- `:daostream/gap`: the cursor is behind the retention boundary and that\n   248\t  stream requires resynchronization.\n   249\t\n   250\tThe implemented observer is `dao.jing/observer-state` (build the immutable\n   251\tstate for an explicit pool, one entry per member) and\n   252\t`dao.jing/observe-step!` (poll the pool round-robin and process at most one\n   253\tpayload; on success it materializes the payload before advancing the member\n   254\tcursor). The `:next` scheduling index keeps a continuously ready member from\n   255\tstarving another. Scheduling does not affect the resulting content set,\n   256\tbecause materialization is content-addressed, commutative, and idempotent.\n   257\t\n   258\tA durable checkpoint records operational progress — a cursor per pool entry —\n   259\tseparately from the content-addressed KV data. Source identity may be needed\n   260\tby a checkpoint mechanism to resume the correct transport, but it must not\n   261\tenter the materialized content identity. Persisting such checkpoints and\n   262\trunning the observer loop over time are open items (see below).\n   263\t\n   264\t## Resource lifecycle\n   265\t\n   266\tThe observer itself is a value: `observe-step!` owns no resources and has\n   267\tnothing to close — streams and storage handles retain responsibility for\n   268\treleasing their transport, file, database, or network resources.\n   269\t`dao.jing/close!` delegates to a handle's optional `:close-fn`; a handle\n   270\twithout one has nothing to release. A long-running runner that drives the pool\n   271\tloop, and its resource policy, are open items (see below).\n   272\t\n   273\t## Implemented surface\n   274\t\n   275\tThe current `src/cljc/dao/jing*.cljc` code implements the architecture above\n   276\tdirectly.\n   277\t\n   278\t**Content-store handles are plain data.** A handle is a map\n   279\t`{:put-content-fn f, :get-content-fn g, :close-fn c?}`; the backend effects\n   280\tare explicit functions, not a protocol or hidden state. `dao.jing/materialize!`\n   281\tand `dao.jing/get` dispatch through the handle, and `close!` through its\n   282\toptional `:close-fn`.\n   283\t\n   284\t**Content-store coordinates are transportable data.**\n   285\t`dao.jing.coordinate/open!` is the explicit interpretation boundary from a\n   286\tcoordinate to a live handle. Its closed dispatch recognizes\n   287\t`:dao.jing/file` on every supported platform and `:dao.jing/remote` on the\n   288\tJVM. There is no name-to-handle registry; adding a backend is an explicit code\n   289\tchange to the coordinate interpreter.\n   290\t\n   291\tImplemented backends:\n   292\t\n   293\t- `dao.jing.mem/create-content-mem` — an ephemeral, thread-safe,\n   294\t  content-addressed in-memory store. Put is an atomic insert-if-absent; an\n   295\t  address already holding the same payload reports `:present` and is never\n   296\t  overwritten.\n   297\t- `dao.jing.file/create-content-file` — a content-addressed store backed by an\n   298\t  append-only log stream. Each log record is `[address payload]`, written\n   299\t  through a write lock, acknowledged only after the log is flushed, and\n   300\t  replayed on open to rebuild the in-memory content map. The framing layer\n   301\t  truncates an incomplete tail before replay; Jing then fails closed on any\n   302\t  complete record that cannot be decoded, validated, or matched to its content\n   303\t  address.\n   304\t- `dao.jing.remote` — exposes a local content handle as the `:jing/put-content`\n   305\t  and `:jing/get-content` RPC ops (`default-handlers`) and wraps an RPC client\n   306\t  as a content handle (`content-client`); `connect-content!` is the\n   307\t  synchronous WebSocket constructor, JVM-only. `:jing/get-content` returns the\n   308\t  exact wire envelope `{:found? boolean, :value value}`; caller-local\n   309\t  not-found sentinels never cross the RPC boundary.\n   310\t- `dao.jing.dht/create-content-dht` and\n   311\t  `dao.jing.dht.node/create-content-dht-udp` — the distributed backend over an\n   312\t  `IDhtNet` transport; see `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md`.\n   313\t\n   314\t**The observer is implemented.** `observer-state` and `observe-step!` provide\n   315\tthe explicit intake-pool walk described in *Cursor tracking and recovery*,\n   316\twith no atoms, globals, registration, or discovery. The source stream never\n   317\tenters an address or a stored value.\n   318\t\n   319\t**Content addressing is implemented, transitionally.** `content-hash` hashes\n   320\tthe order-normalized print of a value; `segment-key` mints\n   321\t`:segment/sha256-<hash>` addresses; `segment-address?` is the strict address\n   322\ttest the backend layer enforces. As recorded in *Canonical encoding*, the\n   323\tencoder is transitional until the pinned canonical byte encoding lands.\n   324\t\n   325\t## Open items and current limitations\n   326\t\n   327\t- **Canonical encoding.** The order-normalized `pr-str` encoder must be\n   328\t  replaced by a pinned, cross-platform canonical byte encoding. Until then,\n   329\t  content addresses are portable only between implementations sharing the\n   330\t  exact print rule; when the encoding lands, `content-hash`, `segment-key`,\n   331\t  and every minted address change together.\n   332\t- **Durable observer checkpoints / long-running runner.** `observer-state`\n   333\t  and `observe-step!` are single-step and in-process. The checkpoint format\n   334\t  for resuming pool cursors across restarts, and a runner that drives the\n   335\t  loop over time, remain to be built.\n   336\t- **Explicit materialization acknowledgement.** A publisher observes only\n   337\t  that its payloads were appended to an intake stream. The mechanism by which\n   338\t  it observes that those payloads have been materialized must be expressed\n   339\t  explicitly, potentially as a response stream.\n   340\t- **Garbage collection.** Content reachability and reclamation belong to a\n   341\t  higher-level retention policy; immutable content otherwise accumulates\n   342\t  forever.\n   343\t- **Async hydration.** Readers of remote or async B-tree content use the\n   344\t  hydration adapter (`dao.data.btree.storage/hydration-storage` and\n   345\t  `hydrate!`), but the async variants (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`)\n   346\t  are deferred until an async DaoJing backend exists. See\n   347\t  `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` §5.4.\n   348\t\n   349\t## Lineage\n   350\t\n   351\tDaoJing combines two constraints:\n   352\t\n   353\t- **Datomic:** storage retains immutable content while an embeddable reader\n   354\t  interprets indexes and queries above it.\n   355\t- **Plan 9:** location and transport are properties of streams and handles,\n   356\t  not of the values carried through them.\n   357\t\n   358\tThe result is a deliberately restricted observer: a pool of streams carries\n   359\topaque values in, canonical content addresses identify them, and a KV\n   360\tmaterialization makes them retrievable. All semantic structure remains in the\n   361\tlayers that publish and consume those values.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
