{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v1\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Design\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: agreed design target. This document is the DaoStream contract. It does\n     4\tnot redesign Yin.VM. Yin.VM is a downstream consumer and will be addressed only\n     5\tafter this contract is implemented and tested.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Purpose\n     8\t\n     9\tThis contract derives from the foundational axioms and non-negotiable\n    10\tinvariants of [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Two axioms bear on it\n    11\tdirectly, named here once so the sections below can cite rather than restate\n    12\tthem:\n    13\t\n    14\t1. **Everything is a Stream** — all IO and data flow through append-only\n    15\t   streams.\n    16\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics** — data is syntax; semantics emerge\n    17\t   only through interpretation. One truth, many perspectives.\n    18\t\n    19\tDaoStream is the passive substrate for IO in datom.world — passive because\n    20\tinterpretation and execution must not collapse into one layer. Axiom 1 is\n    21\twhat makes this the layer every host boundary crosses. A stream carries\n    22\tvalues and decides nothing about them:\n    23\t\n    24\t- **What the values mean** is not its business — Axiom 2. An element\n    25\t  judged, filtered, or typed at this layer destroys the perspectives nobody\n    26\t  has taken yet.\n    27\t- **Which interpreter observes them** is not recorded — a registry of who\n    28\t  is listening would be hidden global state.\n    29\t- **When an interpreter runs** is not its decision — \"do not introduce\n    30\t  callbacks; every callback is events on a stream\", seen from the stream's\n    31\t  side: DaoStream invokes nothing, so control is never inverted and\n    32\t  causality stays explicit (see The IO Model).\n    33\t\n    34\tThe Invariants below are these denials in operational form.\n    35\t\n    36\tThe design separates five concepts:\n    37\t\n    38\t1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.\n    39\t2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the\n    40\t   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.\n    41\t3. A **logical-stream identity** is transport-independent plain data shared by\n    42\t   every handle and descriptor for one stream.\n    43\t4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\n    44\t   stream; it includes the logical-stream identity but is not that identity.\n    45\t5. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one\n    46\t   logical stream.\n    47\t\n    48\tCreation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification or an\n    49\tidentity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\n    50\tstate.\n    51\t\n    52\tSeven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see\n    53\tResult Convention):\n    54\t\n    55\t| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |\n    56\t|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|\n    57\t| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |\n    58\t| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\n    59\t| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\n    60\t| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\n    61\t| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |\n    62\t| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |\n    63\t| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |\n    64\t\n    65\tA handle implements the subset of the **handle surface** its transport\n    66\tdeclares (see Surfaces). `create!` and `attach!` are not part of it: they\n    67\tconsume no handle and produce one.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n    88\t\n    89\tEvery clause below must be consistent with these invariants, and most derive\n    90\tfrom one directly. Where a clause is instead an interoperability decision — the\n    91\tdescriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\n    92\tstated as a decision rather than dressed as a deduction. A proposed addition\n    93\tthat contradicts an invariant does not belong in this contract; one that merely\n    94\tcannot be deduced from them must say which decision it is and why.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Result Convention\n    97\t\n    98\tEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n    99\t\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   114\t  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n   115\t  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n   116\t  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions\n   117\t  and never callbacks.\n   118\t- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`.\n   119\t\n   120\t## The IO Model\n   121\t\n   122\tThree models are available for expressing IO. DaoStream is deliberately the\n   123\tthird.\n   124\t\n   125\t**Blocking (synchronous) IO.** The operation parks until it has an answer,\n   126\tthen returns it. Control flow stays sequential and the call site reads as\n   127\tordinary code, which is the whole of its appeal. The cost is that something\n   128\tmust wait — a thread per pending operation, held for its duration — and it\n   129\trequires a host that can park at all.\n   130\t\n   131\t**Callback (asynchronous) IO.** The operation registers a continuation and\n   132\treturns at once; the runtime invokes the continuation when the answer\n   133\tarrives. Nothing waits, so one thread carries many pending operations: this\n   134\tis the model that scales. Its cost is inverted control. The caller no longer\n   135\tdecides when it observes the answer, and the inversion is viral — on a host\n   136\twithout blocking, a promise cannot be absorbed inside a function, because\n   137\tawaiting returns to the caller at the first suspension point and makes the\n   138\tcaller's own result a promise in turn. Whatever is reached from a callback\n   139\tis reached from a callback.\n   140\t\n   141\t**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is\n   142\ttrue now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\n   143\tnothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.\n   144\tThis is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.\n   145\t\n   146\t`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream\n   147\thas a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\n   148\tposition holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n   149\tanswer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\n   150\tcallback; the Result Convention's \"never exceptions and never callbacks\" is\n   151\tthis model stated as a rule about results.\n   152\t\n   153\tThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\n   154\tcannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n   155\tcalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\n   156\tdata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\n   157\tDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\n   158\treported back. It is a question about now.\n   159\t\n   160\t### Why this model and not the other two\n   161\t\n   162\tThe hosts do not offer the same choices. The JVM has both blocking and\n   163\tasynchronous IO. JavaScript and Dart have only the asynchronous kind: there\n   164\tis no blocking read to call and no way to wait for one, because the single\n   165\tevent loop that would deliver the answer is the one a waiting call would be\n   166\toccupying — waiting deadlocks rather than merely costing a thread.\n   167\t\n   168\tA blocking contract is therefore unimplementable on most of the targets. A\n   169\tcallback contract is implementable on all of them and inverts control on all\n   170\tof them, including the host that never needed it. Non-blocking polling is\n   171\tthe only one of the three that means the same thing everywhere — the same\n   172\toperations, the same outcome sets, the same control flow — which is what\n   173\tlets one surface serve clj, cljs, and cljd without the shape of the API\n   174\tdiffering by host.\n   175\t\n   176\t### What it costs\n   177\t\n   178\tPolling without readiness notification is O(n) in streams observed: a reader\n   179\tholding ten thousand idle streams must ask all ten thousand to learn which\n   180\thave data. This is `select` without `select`, and it is a real limit rather\n   181\tthan an oversight — the extension that answers it is reserved below. The\n   182\tcost also lands where it can be paid: DaoStream schedules nothing, so\n   183\tcadence belongs to the runtime driving the interpreters, and a readiness\n   184\tmechanism belongs there too.\n   185\t\n   186\t### The readiness extension\n   187\t\n   188\tThis contract has no readiness or waiter extension. A reader that receives\n   189\t`:dao.stream/blocked` retries `next` when it chooses; retry cadence is the\n   190\tconcern of the interpreter or the runtime driving it, not of the stream.\n   191\t\n   192\tA future readiness extension would be additive: an optional protocol on\n   193\ttransports that support it, plus optional keys in operation results (for\n   194\texample a wake token list on `append!`), which the open-map rule already\n   195\tpermits. Nothing in this contract needs to change for that; no consumer of\n   196\tthis contract may depend on it existing.\n   197\t\n   198\t## Creation and Attachment\n   199\t\n   200\tCreation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an\n   201\texisting one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.\n   202\t\n   203\t- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a\n   204\t  new logical stream. `descriptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\n   205\t  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\n   206\t  `:dao.stream/identity`, the transport-independent logical-stream identity.\n   207\t  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\n   208\t  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\n   209\t  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n   210\t  no failure outcome.\n   211\t- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\n   212\t  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching\n   213\t  never creates.\n   214\t- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only\n   215\t  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a\n   216\t  request to load a plugin.\n   217\t\n   218\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization\n   219\tboundary. An in-memory stream whose admission declaration permits host\n   220\treferences can carry a handle intact. A dynamically delivered handle travels\n   221\tas composition data together with its declared surface, for example\n   222\t`{:dao.stream/handle h :dao.stream/surface #{:reader :writer}}`; the receiving\n   223\tinterpreter uses only that declared surface, and attaching to what it already\n   224\tholds is meaningless. A composition may omit the wrapper only when the\n   225\treceiver's fixed contract already declares the surface. But a handle does not\n   226\tsurvive encoding, and any\n   227\ttransport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —\n   228\tis a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those\n   229\tboundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a\n   230\ttransport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a\n   231\tvalue it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)\n   232\t`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and\n   233\tresolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the\n   234\tstreams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the\n   235\tfiles a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no\n   236\tregistry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving\n   237\tsomewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n   238\thost, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —\n   239\thonestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.\n   240\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
