{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^## |''^### |What v1 callers lose|Boundary of this plan|End condition'\"' docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md\"","aggregated_output":"10:## Strategy\n35:## Settled before Phase 1\n81:## Phase 1 — Contract core (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)\n170:## Phase 2 — Ring buffer reference (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc`)\n179:  *Boundary of this plan*).\n242:## Phase 3 — Descriptor round trip\n297:## Phase 4 — WebSocket transport, forwarding, and serving composition\n302:### 4a — The transport (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc`)\n384:### 4b — Forwarding (`src/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc`)\n462:### 4c — The serving composition (not in `v2/ws.cljc`, not in `forward.cljc`)\n488:## Phase 5 — The slice, end to end\n520:## Host matrix\n538:## What v1 callers lose, and what replaces it\n569:## Boundary of this plan\n628:## Explicit residual risks\n646:## End condition — v2 is not a permanent fork\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '538,659p' docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md | nl -ba -v538\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '538,659p' docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md | nl -ba -v538\"","aggregated_output":"   538\t## What v1 callers lose, and what replaces it\n   539\t\n   540\tThe contract is a clean-room redesign: `dao.stream.md` neither mentions v1 nor\n   541\towes it compatibility, and nothing in `dao.stream` may consult this table.\n   542\tIt lives here because this plan is the migration, and because the 52 `open!`\n   543\tcall sites need to know what happened to the call they were making. It is\n   544\tconsumed with the rest of this document.\n   545\t\n   546\t| v1 (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)     | v2                            | Why                                                                                       |\n   547\t|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   548\t| `IDaoStreamReader` — `next`         | reader — `cursor`, `next`     | Cursors are minted by the stream instead of built inline as `{:position 0}`, so a transport owns its cursor representation and `cursor-mismatch` becomes detectable. |\n   549\t| `IDaoStreamWriter` — `append!`      | writer — `append!`            | Unchanged in shape; the outcome set is now exhaustive and `append!` never throws on a closed stream — it answers `closed`. |\n   550\t| `IDaoStreamBound` — `close!`        | closable — `close!`           | Outcome set is `{ok}`, idempotent.                                                        |\n   551\t| `IDaoStreamBound` — `closed?`       | **gone**                      | A predicate answer is stale the moment it returns; operate the handle and read the outcome. |\n   552\t| `IDaoStreamWaitable` — 2 fns        | **gone**                      | No readiness extension. A `blocked` reader retries when it chooses; cadence belongs to the runtime driving it. |\n   553\t| `IDaoStreamDrainable` / `drain-one!` | **gone**                     | Destructive read makes one reader's progress every other reader's data loss.               |\n   554\t| `open!` multimethod, `defopen` macro | **gone**                     | Ambient namespace-global registry with load-time registration. Replaced by an ordinary host-owned map from `:dao.stream/type` to `{create attach}`, looked up by the host. |\n   555\t| `open!` on a descriptor              | `create!` or `attach!`        | v1 conflated them. Creation takes a creation specification; attachment takes a portable descriptor and never creates. |\n   556\t| `take!!`                             | **gone**                      | Blocking. JVM-only by construction, and unimplementable on cljs/cljd.                      |\n   557\t| `strict-vec`                         | **gone**                      | Throws on ordinary operational signals; those are outcomes.                                |\n   558\t| `->seq`                              | **gone**                      | An interpreter over a stream, not part of the contract (see the contract's Composition).   |\n   559\t| bare `:ok` / `:blocked` / `:end` / `:daostream/gap` returns | outcome maps under `:dao.stream/…` | Every operation returns an open map keyed by `:dao.stream/outcome`, so extensions add keys without breaking consumers. |\n   560\t| `{:position 0}` cursors              | transport-owned cursor values | There is no `seek`; every valid cursor comes from the stream.                              |\n   561\t| `descriptor` via handle metadata     | `descriptor` operation        | Every handle returns distinct reachability and logical-stream identity projections, both surviving attachment close. |\n   562\t\n   563\tMigrating a call site is therefore mostly mechanical — `open!` splits, results\n   564\tbecome maps, cursors come from `cursor` — with two places that need a decision\n   565\trather than a translation: anything relying on `closed?` must be restructured\n   566\tto act on an outcome, and anything relying on `drain-one!` or `take!!` needs a\n   567\tcursor and a retry policy its runtime owns.\n   568\t\n   569\t## Boundary of this plan\n   570\t\n   571\tCarried, with a term:\n   572\t\n   573\t- The slice's boundary deposits into a ring buffer rather than into the\n   574\t  medium ADR 0003 names, under a time-boxed exception that ADR records. The\n   575\t  exception ends when a conforming writer answers with data and a composition\n   576\t  indexes and publishes what the boundary deposits. Until then this plan is\n   577\t  where the exception is being spent, so it is named here and not only there.\n   578\t\n   579\tForbidden, not deferred:\n   580\t\n   581\t- **Retention that waits for readers.** A stream that declines to evict until\n   582\t  every attached cursor has passed a position is one line from the design\n   583\t  above and has a failure mode none of the rest do: a single stalled or\n   584\t  departed reader holds the stream open indefinitely, and a hostile one does\n   585\t  it on purpose. Bounded retention must stay bounded. Loss is reported as\n   586\t  `gap`; it is never prevented by waiting.\n   587\t\n   588\tNot in this plan, by design:\n   589\t\n   590\t- Migration of any consumer (dao.runtime, yin.vm, dao.jing, dao.space)\n   591\t  — each gets its own plan against the finished slice.\n   592\t- Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, apply/RPC layers).\n   593\t- The waiter/readiness extension, ws resumption protocol, cursor\n   594\t  serialization across hosts, live ring buffer resize — deferred in the\n   595\t  contract, deferred here.\n   596\t- Gating who may attach. The contract reserves nothing for it, so neither does\n   597\t  this plan; it would arrive as an addition, not as the filling-in of a slot\n   598\t  left open here.\n   599\t- **Flow control — a reader pausing a sender.** Deferred, with its dependency\n   600\t  named rather than left as a date. A pause has to be a lease and not a switch:\n   601\t  a switch has a stuck state, since a lost resume or a reader that dies\n   602\t  mid-pause leaves the sender stopped forever, while a grant that lapses absent\n   603\t  renewal returns the system to sending on its own. Those semantics now live in\n   604\t  `dao.lease.md`, whose facts — the grant, its renewals, and the release or\n   605\t  reclaim left as a trace — are datoms on a medium, which for this system means\n   606\t  `dao.space`. `dao.space` is not yet built against this contract, so the chain\n   607\t  bottoms out beyond this slice.\n   608\t\n   609\t  What the slice ships without it is the honest default the contract already\n   610\t  specifies: a reader that falls behind is evicted past and told so with a\n   611\t  `gap`. That is right for a live feed and wrong only as the *sole* option, so\n   612\t  its absence costs an alternative rather than a correctness property.\n   613\t\n   614\t  Two items travel with it. The **pause wire vocabulary** — which the ws spec's\n   615\t  Deferred list calls interoperable wire content needing a durable home — is\n   616\t  therefore not owed by the wire-contract gate, and the gate's enumeration\n   617\t  stands as written. And **liveness probing** stays deferred in\n   618\t  `dao.stream.ws.md` on that document's own terms; this plan sets no interval\n   619\t  and no unanswered-probe threshold, because a subordinate transient document\n   620\t  may not settle what its superior left open.\n   621\t\n   622\t  Resuming it needs three things in order: `dao.space` on the v2 contract, a\n   623\t  `dao.lease.md` that claims the pause and liveness semantics its *Neighbouring\n   624\t  deferrals* section currently declines, and a reconciliation of the pause\n   625\t  request with that contract's negotiation — a reader asking \"hold for *n*\" is\n   626\t  a proposal, and a proposal creates no state until the serving side grants.\n   627\t\n   628\t## Explicit residual risks\n   629\t\n   630\t- A reader that repeatedly accepts a recovery cursor while producers evict\n   631\t  faster than it advances can livelock in repeated `gap` outcomes. Budgets and\n   632\t  caller policy bound each step; the contract does not promise catch-up.\n   633\t- Cursor authenticity is provenance-by-construction, not cryptographic\n   634\t  anti-forgery. Tamper-resistant portable cursors remain deferred with cursor\n   635\t  serialization.\n   636\t- The bounded linearizability oracle runs wherever the host can support the\n   637\t  harness, but useful concurrency exploration is principally the clj run;\n   638\t  single-threaded cljs/cljd parity tests are not equivalent evidence.\n   639\t- Attachment identities are unique only during one boundary lifetime. A\n   640\t  process restart may reuse a representation, so persisted observations must\n   641\t  scope it with boundary/session identity rather than treating it as global.\n   642\t- During migration, v1/v2 coexistence can drift. The per-consumer migration\n   643\t  plans and the end condition below are the control; coexistence is not a\n   644\t  permanent compatibility promise.\n   645\t\n   646\t## End condition — v2 is not a permanent fork\n   647\t\n   648\tThe v2 namespace exists to protect the running system during migration,\n   649\tnot to live forever. The slice is complete when Phases 1–5 pass on clj and\n   650\tcljs — Phase 4 being 4a, 4b and 4c, flow control having been deferred out of\n   651\tit — and it is explicitly **incomplete on cljd** until the cljd ws transport\n   652\tlands, so the end condition is not met before then. Flow control is not part\n   653\tof the end condition and does not hold it open.\n   654\t\n   655\tWhen the slice is complete and the last consumer has migrated (under its\n   656\town plan), legacy `dao.stream` is deleted and a single decision is taken\n   657\texplicitly: `dao.stream` is renamed to `dao.stream`, or keeps its name\n   658\tpermanently. An undecided coexistence of both namespaces is a defect of the\n   659\tmigration, not a steady state.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
