{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,290p' src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc | nl -ba -v1\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.stream.rpc\n     2\t  \"Transport-neutral, polling RPC state machines for DaoStream v2.\n     3\t\n     4\t   This layer deliberately owns no socket, scheduler, atom, promise, callback,\n     5\t   retry policy, or transport registry.  A composition supplies two explicit\n     6\t   handles (a request writer and a response reader), owns the returned state,\n     7\t   and chooses when to step `request!`, `poll!`, and `serve-once!`.\"\n     8\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n     9\t            [dao.stream.apply :as apply]))\n    10\t\n    11\t\n    12\t;; =============================================================================\n    13\t;; Public data vocabulary\n    14\t;; =============================================================================\n    15\t\n    16\t(def max-safe-id\n    17\t  \"Largest integer that remains exactly representable by every supported host.\"\n    18\t  9007199254740991)\n    19\t\n    20\t\n    21\t(def event-key :dao.stream.rpc/event)\n    22\t(def response-key :dao.stream.rpc/response)\n    23\t(def lifecycle-key :dao.stream.rpc/lifecycle)\n    24\t(def diagnostic-key :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic)\n    25\t(def code-key :dao.stream.rpc/code)\n    26\t\n    27\t\n    28\t(def lifecycle-values\n    29\t  \"The lifecycle vocabulary belongs to dao.stream.apply.  Transports decode\n    30\t   their local events into these values before the RPC core sees them.\"\n    31\t  #{:dao.stream.apply/established\n    32\t    :dao.stream.apply/detached\n    33\t    :dao.stream.apply/ended\n    34\t    :dao.stream.apply/not-found\n    35\t    :dao.stream.apply/transport-error\n    36\t    :dao.stream.apply/diagnostic})\n    37\t\n    38\t\n    39\t(defn response-event\n    40\t  [response]\n    41\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/response response-key response})\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\t(defn lifecycle-event\n    45\t  [lifecycle]\n    46\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/lifecycle lifecycle-key lifecycle})\n    47\t\n    48\t\n    49\t(defn diagnostic-event\n    50\t  ([code]\n    51\t   (diagnostic-event code nil))\n    52\t  ([code value]\n    53\t   {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic\n    54\t    diagnostic-key {code-key code\n    55\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/value value}}))\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(defn ignore-event\n    59\t  []\n    60\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/ignore})\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\t(defn safe-id?\n    64\t  \"True for an integer which round-trips without precision loss across CLJ,\n    65\t   CLJS, and CLJD.  IDs are routing tokens, not application values.\"\n    66\t  [id]\n    67\t  (and (integer? id) (<= 0 id max-safe-id)))\n    68\t\n    69\t\n    70\t;; =============================================================================\n    71\t;; Explicit client state\n    72\t;; =============================================================================\n    73\t\n    74\t(defn client-state\n    75\t  \"Create caller-owned RPC client state.\n    76\t\n    77\t   `writer` is the request path. `reader` and `response-cursor` are the\n    78\t   independent response path.  `:decode`, when present, transforms one raw\n    79\t   response-medium value into an RPC event made by this namespace; nil means a\n    80\t   bare apply response stream.  No cursor is fabricated here.\"\n    81\t  ([writer reader response-cursor]\n    82\t   (client-state writer reader response-cursor {}))\n    83\t  ([writer reader response-cursor {:keys [me decode]}]\n    84\t   {:writer writer\n    85\t    :reader reader\n    86\t    :cursor response-cursor\n    87\t    :me me\n    88\t    :decode decode\n    89\t    :next-id 0\n    90\t    :outstanding {}\n    91\t    :unsent nil\n    92\t    :completed []\n    93\t    :diagnostics []\n    94\t    :terminal nil}))\n    95\t\n    96\t\n    97\t(def init-client client-state)\n    98\t\n    99\t\n   100\t(defn server-state\n   101\t  \"The server state is owned by dao.stream.apply, which owns the envelope.\"\n   102\t  [request-cursor]\n   103\t  (apply/server-state request-cursor))\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn serve-once!\n   107\t  \"Advance one server request/response step.  This is an aliasing boundary, not\n   108\t   a second server implementation; apply remains the sole envelope owner.\"\n   109\t  [handlers request-handle response-handle state]\n   110\t  (apply/serve-once! handlers request-handle response-handle state))\n   111\t\n   112\t\n   113\t(defn- rpc-result\n   114\t  [outcome state & {:as extra}]\n   115\t  (merge {:dao.stream.rpc/outcome outcome\n   116\t          :dao.stream.rpc/state state}\n   117\t         extra))\n   118\t\n   119\t\n   120\t(defn- valid-operation-result\n   121\t  [op result]\n   122\t  (if (stream/valid-outcome? op result)\n   123\t    result\n   124\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   125\t     :dao.stream/error :dao.stream/invalid-operation-result}))\n   126\t\n   127\t\n   128\t(defn- completion\n   129\t  [request & {:keys [response reason]}]\n   130\t  (cond-> {:dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   131\t           :dao.stream.rpc/op (:op request)\n   132\t           :dao.stream.rpc/args (:args request)}\n   133\t    response (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/response response)\n   134\t    reason (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/reason reason)))\n   135\t\n   136\t\n   137\t(defn- append-completion\n   138\t  [state request & {:as fields}]\n   139\t  (update state :completed conj (apply completion request (mapcat identity fields))))\n   140\t\n   141\t\n   142\t(defn- append-diagnostic\n   143\t  [state code value]\n   144\t  (update state :diagnostics conj {code-key code\n   145\t                                   :dao.stream.rpc/value value}))\n   146\t\n   147\t\n   148\t(defn- ids-in-use?\n   149\t  [state id]\n   150\t  (or (= id (get-in state [:unsent :id]))\n   151\t      (contains? (:outstanding state) id)\n   152\t      (boolean (some #(= id (:dao.stream.rpc/id %)) (:completed state)))))\n   153\t\n   154\t\n   155\t(defn- allocation-failure\n   156\t  [state code]\n   157\t  (let [state (-> state\n   158\t                  (assoc :terminal :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error)\n   159\t                  (append-diagnostic code (:next-id state)))]\n   160\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error state\n   161\t                :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state)))))\n   162\t\n   163\t\n   164\t(defn- allocate-request\n   165\t  [state op args]\n   166\t  (let [id (:next-id state)]\n   167\t    (cond\n   168\t      (not (safe-id? id))\n   169\t      (allocation-failure state :dao.stream.rpc/id-exhausted)\n   170\t\n   171\t      (ids-in-use? state id)\n   172\t      (allocation-failure state :dao.stream.rpc/id-collision)\n   173\t\n   174\t      :else\n   175\t      (let [request {:id id\n   176\t                     :op op\n   177\t                     :args args\n   178\t                     :encoded (apply/request id op args)}]\n   179\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/allocated\n   180\t                    (-> state\n   181\t                        (assoc :unsent request)\n   182\t                        (update :next-id inc)))))))\n   183\t\n   184\t\n   185\t(defn- accept-unsent\n   186\t  [state request]\n   187\t  (-> state\n   188\t      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   189\t      (assoc-in [:outstanding (:id request)]\n   190\t                {:op (:op request) :args (:args request)})))\n   191\t\n   192\t\n   193\t(defn- attempt-unsent\n   194\t  [state]\n   195\t  (let [request (:unsent state)\n   196\t        append-result (valid-operation-result\n   197\t                        :append!\n   198\t                        (apply/put-request! (:writer state) (:encoded request)))\n   199\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome append-result)]\n   200\t    (case outcome\n   201\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   202\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/requested\n   203\t                  (accept-unsent state request)\n   204\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   205\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)\n   206\t\n   207\t      :dao.stream/full\n   208\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/pending-request state\n   209\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   210\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)\n   211\t\n   212\t      (:dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/invalid-value :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   213\t      (let [state (-> state\n   214\t                      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   215\t                      (append-completion request :reason outcome))]\n   216\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/request-undeliverable state\n   217\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   218\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason outcome\n   219\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result))\n   220\t\n   221\t      ;; A malformed writer answer becomes transport-error in\n   222\t      ;; valid-operation-result, so this branch is defensive only.\n   223\t      (let [state (-> state\n   224\t                      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   225\t                      (append-completion request :reason :dao.stream/transport-error))]\n   226\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/request-undeliverable state\n   227\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   228\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/transport-error\n   229\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)))))\n   230\t\n   231\t\n   232\t(defn request!\n   233\t  \"Attempt one request append, returning an explicit next state.\n   234\t\n   235\t   If a previous append was full, this retries the exact already-allocated\n   236\t   envelope and ignores `op`/`args`; it never allocates a replacement id.  The\n   237\t   function makes one append attempt at most and never spins on `:full`.\"\n   238\t  [state op args]\n   239\t  (cond\n   240\t    (:terminal state)\n   241\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/terminal state)\n   242\t\n   243\t    (:unsent state)\n   244\t    (attempt-unsent state)\n   245\t\n   246\t    (not (and (keyword? op) (vector? args)))\n   247\t    (let [state (append-diagnostic state :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request\n   248\t                                   {:op op :args args})]\n   249\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request state\n   250\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   251\t\n   252\t    :else\n   253\t    (let [allocated (allocate-request state op args)]\n   254\t      (if (= :dao.stream.rpc/allocated (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome allocated))\n   255\t        (attempt-unsent (:dao.stream.rpc/state allocated))\n   256\t        allocated))))\n   257\t\n   258\t\n   259\t(defn unsent?\n   260\t  \"True while an allocated request is still owed one accepted append.  A caller\n   261\t   that allocates ids from this state must not submit new work while it holds:\n   262\t   `request!` retries the unsent envelope and ignores the operation it is given.\"\n   263\t  [state]\n   264\t  (some? (:unsent state)))\n   265\t\n   266\t\n   267\t(defn abandon-unsent\n   268\t  \"Give up on an allocated-but-unsent request, appending its completion with\n   269\t   `reason`.\n   270\t\n   271\t   A composition that changes what the writer *is* — an operator disconnect, a\n   272\t   rebind onto a fresh attachment — owns the decision that the retained\n   273\t   envelope no longer belongs to the new binding.  Abandoning it here reports\n   274\t   the loss on the ordinary completion path, so it is neither silently dropped\n   275\t   nor resent in place of the caller's next request.  The id is retired with\n   276\t   it; `:next-id` never goes back.\"\n   277\t  ([state] (abandon-unsent state :dao.stream.rpc/abandoned))\n   278\t  ([state reason]\n   279\t   (if-let [request (:unsent state)]\n   280\t     (-> state\n   281\t         (assoc :unsent nil)\n   282\t         (append-completion request :reason reason))\n   283\t     state)))\n   284\t\n   285\t\n   286\t;; =============================================================================\n   287\t;; Response decoding and correlation\n   288\t;; =============================================================================\n   289\t\n   290\t(defn- normalize-event\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '233,336p' collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | nl -ba -v233\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '233,336p' collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | nl -ba -v233\"","aggregated_output":"   233\t### Decision 3 — `dao.jing.remote`: remote content needs a driver its caller owns\n   234\t\n   235\t**The honest answer is yes.** A synchronous `get`/`put` over a polling RPC is\n   236\tnot implementable without waiting: on cljs and cljd there is nothing to wait\n   237\twith, and on the JVM a spin-poll inside `:get-content-fn` would be `take!!`\n   238\treintroduced by another name, diverging per host exactly where the contract\n   239\twas chosen to make hosts agree. So the v2 remote adapter does **not** produce\n   240\ta content handle. `jing/materialize!` and `jing/get` cannot be called against\n   241\ta remote store under v2. What replaces them is a client state machine the\n   242\tcaller steps, in the exact shape `yin.repl` already drives.\n   243\t\n   244\t**Server side — `dao.jing.v2.remote/default-handlers`.** The handler map is\n   245\talready the v2 shape: `dao.stream.apply/dispatch-request` calls\n   246\t`(apply handler args)` and wraps the value in a correlated response. The two\n   247\tops stay `:jing/put-content` (returns `:inserted` or `:present`) and\n   248\t`:jing/get-content` (returns the exact presence envelope\n   249\t`{:found? boolean, :value value}`; local not-found sentinels never cross the\n   250\twire). The address is validated against the payload hash before the local\n   251\tstore is touched, as today. A serving composition runs `apply/serve-once!` per\n   252\tattachment with this map. DaoJing owns the handlers; it does not own an\n   253\tendpoint — see *Boundary*.\n   254\t\n   255\tThe v1 `default-handlers` is duplicated (about twenty lines), not required:\n   256\tv1 `dao.jing.remote` requires `dao.stream.rpc.*` on the JVM, and pulling that\n   257\tinto a v2 namespace would make the new namespace v1-dependent. The duplication\n   258\tends at Phase J5.\n   259\t\n   260\t**Client side — a state machine, stepped by the composition.**\n   261\t\n   262\t```clojure\n   263\t(jing.v2.remote/client-state rpc-state)      ; wraps dao.stream.rpc client state; no cursor is fabricated\n   264\t(jing.v2.remote/request-put state address payload) ; => {:outcome k :state s' :id n?}\n   265\t(jing.v2.remote/request-get state address)         ; => {:outcome k :state s' :id n?}\n   266\t(jing.v2.remote/step state budget)                 ; => {:state s' :completions [...]}\n   267\t```\n   268\t\n   269\t- `request-put` and `request-get` validate the address as `jing/get` does —\n   270\t  a non-segment address is a defect in the caller and throws before any\n   271\t  request is allocated — then call `rpc/request!`. Their `:outcome` is the\n   272\t  RPC layer's: `requested`, `pending-request` (the writer answered `full`;\n   273\t  the envelope is retained and re-attempted on the next request or step),\n   274\t  `request-undeliverable`, or `terminal`. One addition: while `rpc/unsent?`\n   275\t  holds, both return `:dao.jing.v2.remote/busy` and allocate nothing, because\n   276\t  `rpc/request!` would silently retry the unsent envelope and ignore the new\n   277\t  operation. A caller must observe `busy` and try again after a step.\n   278\t- `step` calls `rpc/poll!` with the budget, takes completions and diagnostics\n   279\t  exactly once, and decodes each completion into DaoJing vocabulary:\n   280\t  `{:id n :op :jing/put-content :result :inserted}` or `:present`;\n   281\t  `{:id n :op :jing/get-content :found? true :value v}` or `:found? false`;\n   282\t  `{:id n :op … :error {code message}}` for a handler error or unknown\n   283\t  operation; `{:id n :op … :lost reason}` for a request the RPC layer reported\n   284\t  lost (`gap`, `/detached`, `/ended`, `/not-found`, `/transport-error`, or an\n   285\t  append that could not be delivered). A response whose value is not the exact\n   286\t  presence envelope, or not one of `:inserted`/`:present`, is a malformed\n   287\t  response and completes as `:error` with a `dao.jing.v2.remote/malformed-response`\n   288\t  code — the v1 rule that presence envelopes are exact, kept, but as data\n   289\t  rather than an exception, because it now surfaces inside a driver step.\n   290\t- Nothing here loops on `blocked`, holds a clock, retries on its own, or\n   291\t  touches a socket. Timeouts, reattachment (`rpc.ws/rebind` on `/detached`),\n   292\t  cadence, and what to do with a lost request are the driver's.\n   293\t- The namespace is pure `.cljc` with no host branch at all. The v1 adapter's\n   294\t  network client was JVM-only; the v2 client runs on all three hosts by\n   295\t  construction. That widening is the concrete gain of the polling shape.\n   296\t\n   297\t**The `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate.** `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` turns\n   298\t`{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :url …}` into a synchronous handle that\n   299\t`dao.space.index`'s published-index `defopen` then reads through `jing/get` to\n   300\trestore B-tree nodes. Under v2 that coordinate cannot yield a handle; it would\n   301\tyield a client state plus an `attach!` result plus a driver obligation — a\n   302\tcomposition, not a value. This plan leaves `dao.jing.coordinate` and its\n   303\t`:dao.jing/remote` branch **untouched on v1**. The v2 remote client has no\n   304\tcoordinate here. Reading remote content on the query side is the async\n   305\thydration `dao.data.btree.md` §5.4 defers \"until an async DaoJing backend\n   306\texists\" — this client is that backend, and the hydration and the coordinate\n   307\tthat opens into it belong to `dao.space`'s migration plan, whose index\n   308\t`defopen` is the only consumer.\n   309\t\n   310\t### Decision 4 — Namespace layout, in one table\n   311\t\n   312\t| namespace               | this plan                                          | v1 dependency after this plan | consumer that keeps v1 alive                          |\n   313\t| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n   314\t| `dao.jing`              | untouched; v1 observer stays until J5              | `dao.stream` (observer only)  | `dao.space.*` tests via `observe-step!` on v1 intakes |\n   315\t| `dao.jing.v2`           | **new** — the observer on v2                       | none                          | —                                                     |\n   316\t| `dao.jing.file`         | **in-place**: stream coupling removed              | none                          | —                                                     |\n   317\t| `dao.jing.mem`          | untouched                                          | none                          | —                                                     |\n   318\t| `dao.jing.remote`       | untouched                                          | `dao.stream.rpc.*` (clj)      | `dao.jing.coordinate`, `dao.space` tests              |\n   319\t| `dao.jing.v2.remote`    | **new** — handlers + client state machine          | none                          | —                                                     |\n   320\t| `dao.jing.coordinate`   | untouched                                          | via `dao.jing.remote` (clj)   | `dao.space.index`                                     |\n   321\t| `dao.jing.dht*`         | untouched                                          | `dao.stream.transit`, UDP     | out of scope (see *Boundary*)                         |\n   322\t\n   323\t**No registry, restated.** v1's `defopen`/`open!` is gone in v2 and nothing in\n   324\t`dao.jing*` reinvents it. `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` is already the precedent:\n   325\ta closed `case` over coordinate types, an explicit code change to add one. The\n   326\tv2 observer takes handles; the v2 remote takes an RPC state built from handles\n   327\tthe composition attached; neither looks anything up by name.\n   328\t\n   329\t**Reader-conditional discipline.** `dao.jing.file` keeps three-branch\n   330\t`#?(:cljd … :clj … :cljs …)` forms with `:cljd` first, as it does today.\n   331\t`dao.jing.v2` and `dao.jing.v2.remote` contain no host branch. No new file may\n   332\tcontain a bare `#?(:clj …)` form — `#?(:clj …)` does not exclude code from the\n   333\tcljd build; `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)` with `:cljd` first is the only safe spelling,\n   334\tand `:cljd` in tail position silently fails. Full cljd namespace compilation\n   335\tgates every phase.\n   336\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
