{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '361,480p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v361\"","aggregated_output":"   361\tThe entries are per-transport and may be partial. A transport that creates\n   362\tno streams supplies no `:dao.stream/create` — the WebSocket transport owns\n   363\tno stream, it serves streams the host composed, so nothing there creates\n   364\tone. A creation specification naming it finds no create implementation and\n   365\treturns `:dao.stream/not-found`, the same answer as an absent transport and\n   366\tthe correct one: nothing here creates that.\n   367\t\n   368\tEntry values are **host-composed**: ordinarily closures produced by a\n   369\ttransport constructor that already received whatever composition state the\n   370\ttransport needs — a boundary's deposit destination and its admission\n   371\tdeclaration, a directory some composition keeps. The bare vars above are\n   372\tschematic. A bare namespace var could reach that state only through namespace\n   373\tglobals, which is the hidden state this contract retires.\n   374\t\n   375\tThis table is ordinary data owned by the host, and the dispatch is an\n   376\tordinary map lookup done by the host — DaoStream neither performs nor\n   377\tstandardizes it. A host that cannot dispatch on `:dao.stream/type` returns\n   378\t`:dao.stream/not-found` — never creation or discovery.\n   379\t\n   380\tCode that knows its transport statically calls the transport's functions\n   381\tdirectly; no dispatch is involved. There is no ambient namespace-global\n   382\tregistry and no namespace-load registration side effect.\n   383\t\n   384\t## Surfaces\n   385\t\n   386\tA handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can\n   387\thonor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n   388\t(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\n   389\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   390\t\n   391\t`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it with both\n   392\treachability and logical-stream identity, because both outlive any attachment.\n   393\tAn operation a handle does not\n   394\tdeclare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there\n   395\tis no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n   396\tasked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may\n   397\tanswer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n   398\toperation exists.\n   399\t\n   400\tA transport may also offer operations of its own beside the public surface —\n   401\tanswering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n   402\ttrails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n   403\toperations, and nothing here defines or constrains them. **Code written\n   404\tagainst this contract may not reach for one**: it must work on any transport\n   405\tdeclaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n   406\tdefinition absent from some of them.\n   407\t\n   408\tAn interpreter may still use one, on the same terms as any other thing a\n   409\tcomposition supplies. What it may not do is *find* it — reach through a handle\n   410\tfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\n   411\tit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\n   412\tcomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\n   413\tpolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\n   414\tthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n   415\tparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n   416\tcontract-generic may be built on it.\n   417\t\n   418\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   419\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   420\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   421\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   422\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   423\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   424\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   425\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   426\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   427\tdefect. (`dao.stream.ws.md` specifies one such transport and the admission\n   428\trule in detail.)\n   429\t\n   430\tA boundary that can no longer deposit must make itself **observably gone**. Its\n   431\tone permitted action is closing the host resource it itself holds; it invokes\n   432\tnothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing. This is the sole\n   433\taction a boundary adapter takes beyond appending, and it is not a judgement\n   434\tabout any event — it is the same action for every cause. One corner is\n   435\tirreducible: when the deposit that failed was the boundary's only channel, the\n   436\trecord of its own departure has nowhere to land, and the failure is observable\n   437\tonly to whoever holds the deposit result and through subsequent operations on\n   438\tthe handle.\n   439\t\n   440\tA transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it\n   441\texcludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is\n   442\timpossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never\n   443\treports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n   444\tevicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a reported `gap` and never\n   445\tas `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\n   446\tthat cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An\n   447\texclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.\n   448\t\n   449\t### Where the asynchrony goes\n   450\t\n   451\tThe IO Model makes this contract non-blocking; hosts are asynchronous\n   452\twhether or not it is. A network message arrives when the runtime says so, in\n   453\ta callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does\n   454\tnot pretend otherwise; it confines the inversion to one place. The host's\n   455\tboundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what\n   456\tarrived. Past that append the event holds a position in a sequence, and\n   457\tevery reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,\n   458\twhen it chooses.\n   459\t\n   460\tThis is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but\n   461\tthat the inversion spreads through everything it touches; depositing into a\n   462\tretained sequence pays that cost once, at the boundary, and returns ordinary\n   463\tcode to everyone above. The reader-surface rule above is the same thing\n   464\tstated structurally: a transport that retains nothing has no reader surface,\n   465\tand its inbound events are deposited onto a stream that does.\n   466\t\n   467\t## Cursors\n   468\t\n   469\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   470\t\n   471\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   472\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   473\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   474\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   475\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   476\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   477\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   478\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   479\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   480\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md | nl -ba -v1\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Implementation Plan — the WebSocket Slice\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: migration plan, derived from and subordinate to `dao.stream.md`\n     4\t(the contract, review-approved) and `dao.stream.ws.md` (the WebSocket\n     5\tspecification). Where this plan and the contract disagree, the contract\n     6\twins. This document is transient: it is consumed as the phases complete,\n     7\twhile the contract remains. Revised against the `gpt-5.6-sol` plan review\n     8\tof 2026-08-30 (`collab/review-dao-stream-plan.gpt-5.6-sol.stdout.log`).\n     9\t\n    10\t## Strategy\n    11\t\n    12\tTwo deliberate narrowings:\n    13\t\n    14\t1. **A `dao.stream` namespace, not an in-place rewrite.** The legacy\n    15\t   `dao.stream` has 52 `open!` call sites and every subsystem as a\n    16\t   consumer; rewriting it in place is an atomic big bang. The new\n    17\t   namespace is `dao.stream` — one new file,\n    18\t   `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc` — building the contract greenfield while\n    19\t   legacy keeps the system running.\n    20\t   Consumers migrate one at a time, later, under their own plans. The\n    21\t   legacy implementation and its tests are evidence about behavior, not\n    22\t   constraints: no compatibility facade, alias, or dual protocol.\n    23\t2. **The WebSocket slice, end to end, and nothing else.** One vertical\n    24\t   slice that forces the contract's riskiest promises early: the\n    25\t   descriptor round trip across a real serialization boundary, Model A\n    26\t   serving, writer-only surfaces, deposit admission, and observable\n    27\t   teardown. Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, RPC) wait until\n    28\t   this slice stands.\n    29\t\n    30\tThe slice still requires a minimal reader-surface core — the served stream\n    31\tand the deposit destination are both ring buffers — so the ring buffer is\n    32\tin scope, but only what the contract requires of it, nothing the legacy\n    33\tone happens to have.\n    34\t\n    35\t## Settled before Phase 1\n    36\t\n    37\tThree questions a phase would otherwise be forced to invent under delivery\n    38\tpressure. Each is decided here so no implementer decides it alone.\n    39\t\n    40\t**`attach!` is synchronous and never waits.** The Result Convention makes\n    41\tevery operation return an outcome map; the contract's IO Model generalizes\n    42\tit — no operation waits. No transport in this slice returns a promise, a\n    43\tfuture, or a host-native async value from any of the seven operations, on\n    44\tany host. A ws `attach!` answers what is locally decidable — `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`,\n    45\tand `transport-error` for a handler that fails here and now — while how the\n    46\tconnection resolved arrives as deposited\n    47\tlifecycle data and through the handle's own `append!` outcomes; see\n    48\t`dao.stream.ws.md`'s Operations section, corrected to match. A promise\n    49\tleaking out of a v2 operation is a defect, not a host accommodation.\n    50\t\n    51\tThe rule covers `create!` equally, though this slice never exercises it:\n    52\tthe ring buffer is in-memory and creates synchronously on every host, and\n    53\tthe ws transport has no `create!` at all — it owns no stream, and starting\n    54\tan endpoint is a host composition step. A later transport whose creation\n    55\tneeds host resources it cannot acquire synchronously (binding a port,\n    56\topening a handle through an async-only API) answers the same way `attach!`\n    57\tdoes: the logical stream's identity exists at once, the handle reports\n    58\tacquisition state through its own operations, and the result of the\n    59\tacquisition arrives as data. Nothing waits.\n    60\t\n    61\t**Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated.** The contract's\n    62\tpublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy, and must\n    63\tnot gain one — a predicate answer about a live handle is exactly what\n    64\t`closed?` was retired for. So the boundary constructor takes the deposit\n    65\tdestination *and its admission declaration as data*, derived from the same\n    66\tcreation specification that produced the handle. The declaration names both\n    67\tretention (evict-oldest and a capacity) and value domain (`:host-values` or\n    68\t`:portable-values`); the constructor refuses reject-mode retention or a domain\n    69\tthat cannot carry every event it will deposit. This is configuration\n    70\tprovenance, not runtime introspection. Runtime behavior is unchanged: every\n    71\tnon-`ok` deposit result tears the connection down.\n    72\t\n    73\t**Logical-stream identity is minted in Phase 2.** `cursor-mismatch` in\n    74\tPhase 2 and the `:dao.stream/identity` projection carried with descriptors in\n    75\tPhase 3 use the same value. It is allocated when a logical stream is created;\n    76\tcursors carry it while descriptors pair it with transport-specific\n    77\treachability. Cursor and descriptor representations need not match and need\n    78\tnot be equally serializable — cursor serialization across hosts stays TBD per\n    79\tthe contract — but identity comparison never compares whole descriptors.\n    80\t\n    81\t## Phase 1 — Contract core (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)\n    82\t\n    83\tThe protocol and data surface, with no transport. The seven operations are\n    84\ttwo kinds, and the split is the point:\n    85\t\n    86\t- **Five handle operations**, as protocols a handle implements per its\n    87\t  declared surface: `descriptor` (universal reachability and identity projections), `cursor` and `next`\n    88\t  (reader), `append!` (writer), `close!` (closable).\n    89\t- **Two transport entry functions**, `create!` and `attach!`, which take no\n    90\t  handle because none exists yet. These are per-transport functions.\n    91\t  Dynamic selection is a host-owned map from `:dao.stream/type` to a\n    92\t  `{create attach}` pair — ordinary data, ordinary lookup, performed by the\n    93\t  host. DaoStream ships no dispatch machinery: no multimethod, no registry,\n    94\t  no load-time side effect. Host dispatch is documentation plus an example\n    95\t  map in tests.\n    96\t\n    97\tPutting `create!` or `attach!` on a protocol is how the retired registry\n    98\tgrows back; the file must not do it.\n    99\t\n   100\tAlso in Phase 1:\n   101\t\n   102\t- The result convention: every operation returns an outcome map keyed by\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome`, open maps, all qualified keywords under\n   104\t  `:dao.stream/…`, per-operation closed outcome sets exactly as the\n   105\t  contract's tables state, with no outcome held open for a mechanism that does\n   106\t  not exist.\n   107\t- Generic envelope validation: `:dao.stream/type` present and qualified;\n   108\t  everything else transport-owned.\n   109\t- `descriptor` on every handle regardless of surface — its `{ok}` result\n   110\t  requires both `:dao.stream/descriptor` and `:dao.stream/identity`; both\n   111\t  outlive an attachment, so the operation must not be bundled into the reader\n   112\t  or writer protocol. The conformance harness asserts that the sibling identity\n   113\t  equals the identity carried inside the descriptor envelope.\n   114\t\n   115\tDeliverable: a contract-conformance test suite, **declaration-driven by\n   116\tconstruction**. Its input is a manifest — ordinary data, not code the harness\n   117\tinspects — carrying the transport's entry functions and handle factories, its\n   118\tdeclared surfaces, its per-operation outcome subsets with a reason for every\n   119\texclusion, and a fixture able to induce each outcome it does declare.\n   120\t\n   121\tThe harness runs the blocks the manifest licenses: result shapes, envelope\n   122\tvalidation, and descriptor identity always, since `descriptor` belongs to no\n   123\tsurface; reader laws (cursor provenance — mint / successor / gap-recovery only\n   124\t— non-destructive multi-reader reads, anchors) only for a declared reader;\n   125\twriter laws only for a writer; **close laws only for a declared closable**,\n   126\twhich the earlier \"always\" wrongly assumed of every handle.\n   127\t\n   128\tTwo assertions come from the manifest rather than from any one transport: no\n   129\tobserved outcome falls outside the declared subset, and for each declared\n   130\toutcome the fixture actually produces it. That is what makes the contract's\n   131\t\"declare which outcomes you produce, and why you exclude the rest\" checkable\n   132\trather than aspirational — an exclusion whose substitute never materializes\n   133\tfails, and an outcome that never appears proves nothing on its own.\n   134\t\n   135\t**Concurrency oracle.** The conformance harness uses bounded, offline\n   136\tlinearizability checking separately for each sequence a handle surface is\n   137\ton. It records invocation and completion boundaries, arguments, and\n   138\toutcomes; appended test values are unique tokens. The checker searches for a\n   139\tsequential history accepted by the transport's pure abstract model.\n   140\t\n   141\tThe only real-time edges it preserves are harness-observable happens-before\n   142\tedges: when one invocation has completed before another is begun, the first\n   143\tprecedes the second. Overlapping operations receive no real-time order and\n   144\tmay linearize in any model-valid order. Operations on distinct logical\n   145\tstreams or distinct ordered outbound paths are checked independently; the\n   146\toracle asserts no global order across them.\n   147\t\n   148\tFor the ring buffer, the model contains logical-stream identity, ordered\n   149\tpositions, the bounded evict-oldest window, immutable cursors, attachment\n   150\tlifecycle, and logical-stream close. It linearizes each operation against\n   151\tthat abstract sequence — not against atom swap order, wall-clock start\n   152\torder, thread scheduling, or a particular implementation field.\n   153\t\n   154\tThe Phase 2 concurrency cases include concurrent append/append, append/next,\n   155\tappend/close, cursor/close, and independent readers, with bounded histories\n   156\tsmall enough for exhaustive search. A history for which no legal\n   157\tsequentialization exists fails conformance.\n   158\t\n   159\tThis oracle detects duplicate or lost successful appends, impossible\n   160\toutcomes, cursor corruption, non-atomic append-versus-close behavior, and\n   161\tdestructive interference between readers. It cannot prove liveness or\n   162\tfairness, detect failures in schedules the generator did not produce,\n   163\tvalidate WebSocket host-library ordering, or prove correctness for unbounded\n   164\texecutions. Stress repetition supplements it but is not itself the oracle.\n   165\t\n   166\tThe suite is *authored* in Phase 1 and first *demonstrated* in Phase 2.\n   167\tPhase 1 has no transport, so it proves nothing on its own; that is expected\n   168\tand is not a reason to defer writing it.\n   169\t\n   170\t## Phase 2 — Ring buffer reference (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc`)\n   171\t\n   172\tThe reference reader+writer+closable transport, scoped to the contract:\n   173\t\n   174\t- Logical-stream identity minted at `create!`, per *Settled before Phase 1*.\n   175\t- Evict-oldest retention with declared capacity; `append!` never returns\n   176\t  `full`; monotonic retention; `gap` with earliest-retained-else-tail\n   177\t  recovery cursor. Eviction applies whatever any reader is doing — a\n   178\t  retention policy that waits for readers is forbidden, not deferred (see\n   179\t  *Boundary of this plan*).\n   180\tNo high-water figure in the creation specification: the ring buffer holds no\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '241,360p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v241\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '241,360p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v241\"","aggregated_output":"   241\t### Envelopes\n   242\t\n   243\tA **creation specification** is a map with one required key:\n   244\t\n   245\t- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the\n   246\t  dispatch key.\n   247\t\n   248\tEvery other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace\n   249\t(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler\n   250\t— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its\n   251\town keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.\n   252\t\n   253\tThe **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\n   254\treference. It is reachability data, not the logical-stream identity itself.\n   255\tIts exact definition is not designed yet; these properties are settled, and\n   256\tthe key set that realizes them is TBD:\n   257\t\n   258\t- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch\n   259\t  key.\n   260\t- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   261\t  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n   262\t  the same logical stream through different endpoints; their reachability data\n   263\t  may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n   264\t  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n   265\t- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is\n   266\t  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is\n   267\t  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n   268\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.\n   269\t- A transport whose attachments are distinguishable assigns each attachment\n   270\t  an opaque, structurally serializable value, unique among the attachments\n   271\t  that transport can tell apart. Every attachment handle the transport mints\n   272\t  has such an identity, whether the handle is returned by `attach!` or\n   273\t  minted from a host connection accepted without an `attach!` call. An\n   274\t  `attach!` success map carries the value under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   275\t  When no `attach!` call produces the handle, the transport specification\n   276\t  names the deposited event or other stream-native mechanism by which the\n   277\t  composition receives the identity. Wherever an answer or event for that\n   278\t  attachment is displaced onto another channel, the same value correlates it\n   279\t  there. The key and correlation rule are the contract's; minting,\n   280\t  representation, and the server-side delivery mechanism are the\n   281\t  transport's. Transports that cannot distinguish attachments omit it.\n   282\t- It carries no authorization. Nothing in this contract gates who may attach:\n   283\t  a descriptor is a name, and a host that can reach what it names attaches.\n   284\t  Whether attachment should be gated at all, and by what, is a question this\n   285\t  contract does not answer and does not reserve room for — an outcome or key\n   286\t  held open for an undesigned mechanism constrains that mechanism's design\n   287\t  from a document with no business specifying it. Should gating arrive, it\n   288\t  arrives as an addition, which the open-map rule and this section's own\n   289\t  transport-owned keys already permit.\n   290\t\n   291\tFor the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`\n   292\ton a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,\n   293\ta value that can be sent on a stream and reconstructed on the other end, and\n   294\t`:dao.stream/identity`, equal through every handle and descriptor for the\n   295\tlogical stream,\n   296\twhere `attach!` on the reconstructed value attaches to the stream it names —\n   297\tprovided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and\n   298\tAttachment on reachability), and allowing that a transport which cannot know\n   299\tthat at call time reports it later as data.\n   300\t\n   301\tBoth envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive\n   302\tthe host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is\n   303\tstructural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or\n   304\tidentity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —\n   305\ta consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.\n   306\t\n   307\t`create!` outcomes:\n   308\t\n   309\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |\n   310\t|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   311\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists, its identity settled at once. Implies not that every resource backing it has finished being acquired. | `:dao.stream/handle` |\n   312\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |\n   313\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |\n   314\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |\n   315\t\n   316\t`attach!` outcomes:\n   317\t\n   318\t| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |\n   319\t|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   320\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle exists through which this attachment is operated. Implies neither that a far end has confirmed the stream it names nor that establishment has completed. Where either answer is one only the far end can give, it arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\n   321\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |\n   322\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |\n   323\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |\n   324\t\n   325\tWhere a transport acquires its medium through a process rather than at once —\n   326\tbinding a port, opening a handle through an API that only answers later — `ok`\n   327\tstill means the logical stream exists: its identity is minted, its sequence is\n   328\tempty, and the handle is operable. What it does not mean is that the stream can\n   329\tyet accept values. The handle reports that through its own operations, exactly\n   330\tas any other transient condition is reported, and the result of the acquisition\n   331\tarrives as data.\n   332\t\n   333\tThis is permitted only where logical existence is genuinely independent of the\n   334\tresource. A transport that cannot mint identity and lifecycle before its\n   335\tacquisition completes is not creating a stream and deferring its medium; it is\n   336\tdeferring the creation itself, and `ok` would be false. Such a transport does\n   337\tnot implement `create!` — what it needs is a request whose result arrives\n   338\tlater, which is an ordinary interpreter over streams, not an operation here.\n   339\t\n   340\tCreation defers differently from attachment, and the difference is why this is\n   341\ta caveat rather than a new outcome. `attach!` defers a **remote confirmation**:\n   342\tan answer only a far end can give, which is why it arrives as deposited data.\n   343\t`create!` has no far end. What it defers is **local acquisition**, whose\n   344\tfailure surfaces through the handle the caller already holds. A \"not yet\"\n   345\toutcome would have to return no handle — leaving the caller an identity it\n   346\tcannot operate, and nothing to retry against.\n   347\t\n   348\t### Host Dispatch\n   349\t\n   350\tWhen a creation specification or portable descriptor arrives dynamically —\n   351\tfor example off a stream — the host looks at it and dispatches on\n   352\t`:dao.stream/type`. The type resolves to a map that has the transport's\n   353\t`:dao.stream/create` and `:dao.stream/attach` implementations:\n   354\t\n   355\t```clojure\n   356\t{:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!\n   357\t                         :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}\n   358\t :dao.stream/ws         {:dao.stream/attach ws/attach!}}\n   359\t```\n   360\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '361,537p' docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md | nl -ba -v361\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '361,537p' docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md | nl -ba -v361\"","aggregated_output":"   361\t  composition partners — including the concrete capacity-one handoff slots —\n   362\t  wired by the host composition, not by the transport. A transport requiring\n   363\t  another transport is the legacy registry defect reborn.\n   364\t- **Acceptance-handoff tests**: no wire `:ws/accept` or value delivery\n   365\t  before matching acknowledgement; an unread offer survives arbitrary\n   366\t  traffic-medium eviction; a slot is not reused before acknowledgement; slot\n   367\t  exhaustion closes the new connection without overwriting an offer; stale\n   368\t  or wrong-identity acknowledgements do not accept a connection; a matching\n   369\t  malformed acknowledgement releases its slot and closes pre-accept; pre-accept\n   370\t  terminal events use the control medium while post-accept events use the\n   371\t  acknowledged per-attachment medium; and\n   372\t  offer-deposit failure, acknowledgement-deposit failure, endpoint stop,\n   373\t  peer loss, and admission expiry each close the connection, release the slot,\n   374\t  ignore a later stale acknowledgement, and deposit exactly one terminal\n   375\t  lifecycle event per endpoint.\n   376\t- **Wire-close conformance**: on every supported host library, prove codes\n   377\t  `4000`, `4002`, and `4004` and their reasons can be sent and observed, and\n   378\t  prove the required event sequences for ended, protocol failure, disclaimer,\n   379\t  locally initiated `close!`, and a host close failure. If any library fails\n   380\t  this gate, Phase 4a does not ship the code-only design: first amend the wire\n   381\t  spec to send `{:ws/frame :ws/end}` before close and treat code 4000 as a\n   382\t  secondary fast path, then rerun cross-host conformance.\n   383\t\n   384\t### 4b — Forwarding (`src/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc`)\n   385\t\n   386\tServing is what makes a stream remotely attachable, and nothing in the\n   387\ttransport transfers a served stream's values to a connected socket. Under\n   388\tthe contract that transfer is an interpreter: it reads one stream through\n   389\tits own cursor and appends to another. Streams notify no one, so nothing\n   390\tdrives it from below — a driver does, by calling it.\n   391\t\n   392\tThat transfer is not WebSocket-specific. It reads *any* reader handle and\n   393\tappends to *any* writer handle: ring buffer to socket, ring buffer to ring\n   394\tbuffer, the code is identical. Its transport-agnosticism is the proof that\n   395\tit belongs neither inside a transport nor inside one composition. It is a\n   396\tthird layer between them.\n   397\t\n   398\tThe step-versus-loop question is settled: the deliverable is a **single\n   399\tstep**, `forward-step`, not a self-rescheduling loop. A loop that hands\n   400\titself to a scheduler is a relocated callback — a continuation handed to\n   401\thost machinery to invoke later — and it needs a cancellation mechanism\n   402\tnothing here names. A step needs neither: a driver calling a function\n   403\trepeatedly is ordinary control flow. `forward-step` consumes the source\n   404\thandle, the destination handle, its state — the cursor to read from — and\n   405\tthe two policies below; it drains at most the batch budget and returns its\n   406\tnext state: the cursor to continue from, plus a status saying either that it\n   407\tshould be called again (progress made, or a retryable `blocked` or `full`)\n   408\tor that it is finished, with the terminal condition carried as data.\n   409\t\n   410\t`forward-step` owns the three disciplines that are the same everywhere and\n   411\tare the easiest things in this slice to get wrong:\n   412\t\n   413\t- `full` **does not advance the cursor** — nothing was appended, and\n   414\t  treating the value as forwarded drops it silently;\n   415\t- `blocked` returns to the driver rather than spinning — on a cooperative\n   416\t  event loop a spin starves the runtime, so the send buffer never drains\n   417\t  and `full` never clears; returning is the yield;\n   418\t- `end` returns the terminal state `source-ended` and closes nothing.\n   419\t  `forward-step` is transport-agnostic, so it cannot select the close code\n   420\t  `dao.stream.ws.md` requires for an ended served stream — a generic\n   421\t  `close!` is a plain disconnect, and the client's fast path would silently\n   422\t  degrade to reattach-and-be-disclaimed. The transport-specific close\n   423\t  belongs to the composition that reads the state and knows its transport\n   424\t  (4c).\n   425\t\n   426\tIt owns nothing else. Two policies are arguments, because each has different\n   427\tright answers in different compositions:\n   428\t\n   429\t- **the `gap` policy** — resume at the recovery cursor, or terminate. A\n   430\t  serving pump may skip evicted values; an interpreter that dropped\n   431\t  submitted work must not. Same outcome, opposite correct responses;\n   432\t- **the batch budget** — how many values to drain before returning, which\n   433\t  exists only because a cooperative event loop needs yielding.\n   434\t\n   435\tEverything else the loop form would have owned belongs to the **driver**,\n   436\tthe composition-supplied code that calls `forward-step`. Cadence is the\n   437\tdriver's — \"run this again later\" is `setTimeout` on Node, a sleeping thread\n   438\ton the JVM, and the concern of the runtime driving the interpreter, never of\n   439\tthe stream (contract, *The readiness extension*). Starting is the driver's\n   440\tfirst call; nothing self-installs. Stopping is the driver ceasing to call it\n   441\twhile keeping the returned state — which is also the cancellation mechanism —\n   442\tand restarting is calling it again with that state. The kept state's cursor\n   443\tnever moved, which is why a future flow-control layer needs no protocol for\n   444\tsaying where to continue: the sender never lost its place, so a paused\n   445\tconnection resumes without being told anything.\n   446\t\n   447\t`forward-step` is written against the contract rather than against a named\n   448\ttransport, so it is the one interpreter that must be **total over the outcome\n   449\talgebra** — every outcome of `next` and of `append!`, including those its\n   450\tparticular endpoints never produce. Beyond the disciplines above that means an\n   451\texplicit branch for destination `closed`, `invalid-value`, and\n   452\t`transport-error`, and for source `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor` (its\n   453\tstart cursor is caller-supplied and may not belong to the source), and\n   454\t`transport-error`. These return a terminal state carrying the outcome rather\n   455\tthan retrying: only `full` and `blocked` are the retryable answers. Otherwise\n   456\tthe contract's totality is bought by moving partiality into its first\n   457\tconsumer.\n   458\t\n   459\tTestable with two ring buffers and no socket, so it may be written any time\n   460\tafter Phase 2; only its *use* is Phase 4.\n   461\t\n   462\t### 4c — The serving composition (not in `v2/ws.cljc`, not in `forward.cljc`)\n   463\t\n   464\tWhat is left once the step is infrastructure is the wiring and policy only\n   465\tthe host has. This is a named deliverable, not test wiring, and it must\n   466\tdefine:\n   467\t\n   468\t- the serving lifecycle: what starts and stops an endpoint, and who owns it;\n   469\t- the single driver that calls transport-owned `endpoint-step` with its clock,\n   470\t  cadence, and admission-expiry policy before advancing application sessions;\n   471\t- the `:ws/path` → served-stream resolution table, host-owned data;\n   472\t- which identities this endpoint serves — the resolution table's contents.\n   473\t  The handshake's wire vocabulary is not composition; it belongs to 4a;\n   474\t- how a connection is associated with the served stream it joined, and how\n   475\t  that identity reaches deposited events;\n   476\t- one forwarder per connection — a driver stepping `forward-step` from the\n   477\t  served stream to that connection's ws handle — with the cadence, batch\n   478\t  budget, and `gap` policy this composition chooses;\n   479\t- whether inbound payload events are interpreted into appends on the served\n   480\t  stream, and if so by which interpreter — the boundary itself judges\n   481\t  nothing;\n   482\t- close behavior: what a server-side owner closing the logical stream does\n   483\t  to connected attachments. When a connection's forwarder returns\n   484\t  `source-ended`, this composition performs the transport-specific close —\n   485\t  the ended-stream close code per `dao.stream.ws.md`, settled at the\n   486\t  wire-contract gate — which `forward-step` deliberately cannot (see 4b).\n   487\t\n   488\t## Phase 5 — The slice, end to end\n   489\t\n   490\tTwo processes, not two compositions in one. A same-process socket test is\n   491\tworth writing earlier for diagnostics, but it cannot show that a descriptor\n   492\tis self-contained or that no accidental shared state carries identity\n   493\tacross — and those are central contract promises.\n   494\t\n   495\tProcess A creates a ring buffer, serves it over ws, appends. Process B\n   496\treceives the descriptor as data, attaches, and appends outbound; B's host\n   497\tboundary deposits inbound traffic into B's medium; an interpreter in B\n   498\treads that medium with cursors. State the bootstrap channel explicitly:\n   499\thow B obtains A's descriptor is part of the test, not an assumption.\n   500\t\n   501\tKill the connection, then verify four separable facts:\n   502\t\n   503\t1. Detachment is observable on both sides — B's handle answers `closed`,\n   504\t   A's boundary deposits the departure.\n   505\t2. The served stream survives on A, and `attach!` with the same descriptor\n   506\t   rejoins the same logical stream.\n   507\t3. Two simultaneous attachments to the same stream carry distinct\n   508\t   `:dao.stream/attachment` values in their `attach!` results, and every event\n   509\t   deposited for one — payload, resolution, and lifecycle alike — carries that\n   510\t   same value under `:ws/attachment`.\n   511\t4. B's cursor **on B's deposit medium** still resumes — that medium\n   512\t   outlives the socket. The cursor is not handed to `attach!` and not used\n   513\t   through the ws handle, which has no reader surface. WebSocket-level\n   514\t   resumption — what history a rejoining client receives — is deferred in\n   515\t   `dao.stream.ws.md` and is not tested here.\n   516\t5. Serving A's stream through a second endpoint produces different reachability\n   517\t   descriptors whose `:dao.stream/identity` values are equal; cursors compare\n   518\t   that identity, never either descriptor.\n   519\t\n   520\t## Host matrix\n   521\t\n   522\t- **Phases 1–2** are pure `.cljc` with no host-specific codec: they must compile\n   523\t  and pass on clj, cljs (Node), and cljd. Phase 3 is the first host-specific\n   524\t  phase because `dao.stream.transit` selects a Transit implementation per\n   525\t  host; its shared corpus must pass identically on all three.\n   526\t- **Phase 4–5** target clj and cljs (Node) — Node server plus Node client is\n   527\t  the slice. Browser-client behavior is not validated by a Node target and\n   528\t  is out of the slice. cljd ws follows once the slice is proven; beware the\n   529\t  `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)` reader-conditional trap.\n   530\t- **`forward.cljc` is host-agnostic** — `forward-step` takes no scheduler\n   531\t  and has no host dependency at all; cadence lives in the driver — so it\n   532\t  compiles and its ring-buffer-to-ring-buffer tests pass on all three hosts,\n   533\t  including where the ws transport does not yet exist.\n   534\t- Per host, name explicitly: how transient send-buffer `full` is detected,\n   535\t  how a send racing a concurrent close is handled, and that the codec\n   536\t  produces structurally identical values across all three.\n   537\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
