{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '181,360p' docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md | nl -ba -v181\"","aggregated_output":"   181\treader positions and so can never act on one. The threshold at which a reader\n   182\tconsiders itself behind is that reader's own configuration, belonging to\n   183\twhatever interpreter measures it — which this slice does not build.\n   184\t- Cursor anchors `:dao.stream/oldest` and `:dao.stream/newest`;\n   185\t  `invalid-anchor`; cursors carry the logical-stream identity\n   186\t  (`cursor-mismatch` / `invalid-cursor` detectable); valid across handles\n   187\t  of the same logical stream.\n   188\t- One coherent logical-stream atom containing sequence state, logical close,\n   189\t  every attachment's open/closed state, and each closed attachment's frozen\n   190\t  tail. `next` makes one deref and decides identity, retention, attachment\n   191\t  freeze, and availability from that snapshot, satisfying the Concurrency\n   192\t  section.\n   193\t- Phase 2 also implements the ring buffer's attachment entry. It is not a\n   194\t  namespace-global resolver: `make-attacher` receives a host-owned directory\n   195\t  or resolver and returns the unary `attach!` function that a host places in\n   196\t  its dispatch table. The returned function validates the descriptor,\n   197\t  resolves its logical-stream identity against that captured composition\n   198\t  state, and returns `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, or `not-found`.\n   199\t\n   200\t  A successful attachment is a fresh attachment handle over the same\n   201\t  logical-stream state, not the creator handle reused by reference. Its own\n   202\t  `close!` closes only that attachment; `descriptor` and cursors still\n   203\t  denote the underlying logical stream. Where these attachment lifecycles\n   204\t  are distinguishable, the success map carries `:dao.stream/attachment` as\n   205\t  the contract requires.\n   206\t\n   207\t  Phase 2 tests the entry with a minimal supplied resolver. Phase 3 owns the\n   208\t  real test composition's directory population, Transit round trip, and\n   209\t  kept-cursor proof; it does not first implement attachment there.\n   210\t\n   211\tExplicitly absent: waiters, drain, take, seq views, `closed?`, **live\n   212\tresize** — compatible with the contract but proving nothing the slice needs,\n   213\tsince fixed declared capacity is sufficient for deposit admission — and the\n   214\ttransport-owned **`lag`** operation, whose only consumer was flow control (see\n   215\t*Not in this plan*). Both follow the slice.\n   216\t\n   217\t**Ring-buffer manifest and exclusions.** The manifest records the following\n   218\texact subsets and reasons:\n   219\t\n   220\t| Operation | Produces | Excluded, and why |\n   221\t|---|---|---|\n   222\t| `create!` | `ok`, `invalid-spec` | `not-found`: host dispatch answers absence before this handler is selected. `transport-error`: allocation and state transition use only in-memory values and have no operational failure channel after validation. |\n   223\t| `attach!` | `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, `not-found` | `transport-error`: lookup against the supplied in-memory resolver has no operational failure channel. |\n   224\t| `descriptor` | `ok` | None; this is the contract's complete set. |\n   225\t| `cursor` | `ok`, `invalid-anchor`, `closed` | `transport-error`: cursor minting reads only coherent in-memory state. `closed` is induced through a closed attachment handle; the creator handle remains on the logical stream. |\n   226\t| `next` | `ok`, `blocked`, `end`, `gap`, `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor` | `transport-error`: reading coherent in-memory state has no operational failure channel. |\n   227\t| `append!` | `ok`, `closed` | `full`: evict-oldest answers retention pressure by eviction, reported later as `gap`, so it never refuses for capacity. `invalid-value`: an in-memory reference stream performs no encoding and can carry every host value admitted as a stream element. `transport-error`: its in-memory state transition has no operational failure channel. |\n   228\t| `close!` | `ok` | None; this is the contract's complete set. |\n   229\t\n   230\t**Reject mode is not a deferred ring-buffer variant.** It is intentionally\n   231\tabsent because v2 has no destructive drain: once such a buffer reached\n   232\tcapacity, no operation could free a slot, so `full` would be permanent.\n   233\tReintroducing reject mode would recreate the withdrawn deadlock rather than\n   234\tadd usable backpressure. Backpressure, if later required, belongs in the\n   235\tdeferred interpreter/lease path, not in this reference transport.\n   236\t\n   237\tThe Phase 1 conformance suite runs here for the first time against a transport\n   238\tdeclaring all three surfaces. Laws that need a resolvable descriptor wait for\n   239\tPhase 3's directory; everything else runs now. Ring-buffer-specific tests cover\n   240\tretention, anchors, and eviction reporting.\n   241\t\n   242\t## Phase 3 — Descriptor round trip\n   243\t\n   244\tThe portable identity story, still in-memory, and constructible without\n   245\tWebSockets: the contract explicitly permits a host composition to keep a\n   246\tdirectory, which is what makes a ring buffer's descriptor resolvable on its\n   247\town host.\n   248\t\n   249\t- `descriptor` on a ring buffer handle yields a plain-data envelope that\n   250\t  survives the **DaoStream v2 portable codec** structurally unchanged. That\n   251\t  codec is Transit JSON text, implemented behind `dao.stream.transit`,\n   252\t  with the portable value domain and no-custom-handler profile specified by\n   253\t  `dao.stream.ws.md`.\n   254\t- There is **one value codec, not two**: Phase 3 encodes the descriptor\n   255\t  directly as a Transit value, while WebSocket frames encode their control\n   256\t  or value envelope with the same codec. Framing differs; value encoding\n   257\t  does not.\n   258\t- Phase 3 owns the cross-host codec conformance corpus and round trip on\n   259\t  clj, cljs, and cljd. `dao.stream.transit` has host implementations:\n   260\t  Cognitect Transit CLJ on clj, Cognitect Transit CLJS on Node, and a new\n   261\t  v2-owned cljd implementation adapted from the algorithms in\n   262\t  `src/cljd/dao/stream/transit.cljd`. The v2 namespace must not depend on that\n   263\t  legacy namespace merely because it uses the same Transit format.\n   264\t- A **host-kept local directory** — a map from stream identity to live\n   265\t  handle, owned by the test composition, not by DaoStream and not by any\n   266\t  transport. It is deliberately test-only: the ws serving side in Phase 4\n   267\t  resolves `:ws/path` against its own served-stream table and does not\n   268\t  reuse this. Say so in the code, so nobody later mistakes it for\n   269\t  infrastructure.\n   270\t- Round-trip test: descriptor → encode → decode → the Phase 2\n   271\t  `make-attacher` closure over that composition's directory → handle on the\n   272\t  same logical stream; a kept cursor resumes through the new handle. This is\n   273\t  where the kept-cursor promise is proven, on a transport that has a reader\n   274\t  surface.\n   275\t- `not-found` for descriptors nothing backs.\n   276\t\n   277\t**Decision gate — descriptor key set.** The contract fixes\n   278\t`:dao.stream/identity` and leaves the transport-specific descriptor envelope\n   279\tTBD; this phase produces the evidence for settling the remaining keys. The\n   280\tgate must keep identity distinct from reachability and prove that distinct\n   281\tdescriptors for two endpoints serving one stream carry equal identity. Phase 4 must not start\n   282\tagainst provisional keys: review the round trip's key set, settle it in\n   283\t`dao.stream.md`, and settle `:ws/…` in `dao.stream.ws.md` against it.\n   284\tProvisional keys carried into a wire format under delivery pressure become\n   285\tpermanent by accident.\n   286\t\n   287\t**Decision gate — wire contract, settled in `dao.stream.ws.md`.** The\n   288\tHandshake and Elements and Serialization sections there now specify the\n   289\trequest-target presentation, the `:ws/accept`/`:ws/disclaim` first frame,\n   290\tclose codes 4000/4002/4004, the Transit-JSON value codec, and decode-failure\n   291\tbehaviour. Phase 4a implements that wire verbatim and its wire-level\n   292\tconformance tests are a prerequisite to Phase 5 — for the same reason as the\n   293\tdescriptor gate: provisional choices carried into a wire format under\n   294\tdelivery pressure become permanent by accident. The gate adds nothing beyond\n   295\twhat `dao.stream.ws.md` now settles.\n   296\t\n   297\t## Phase 4 — WebSocket transport, forwarding, and serving composition\n   298\t\n   299\tPer `dao.stream.ws.md`, both ends. Three deliverables in three layers —\n   300\ttransport, infrastructure, composition — deliberately separate:\n   301\t\n   302\t### 4a — The transport (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc`)\n   303\t\n   304\t- **Client side**: `attach!` connects and returns without waiting, answering\n   305\t  only what is decidable here and now — `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, or\n   306\t  `transport-error` for a handler that fails locally; see *Settled before\n   307\t  Phase 1*. The composition creates its traffic medium and mints its\n   308\t  `:dao.stream/newest` cursor before it calls `attach!`. The handle is\n   309\t  writer+closable only; `append!` with transient `full` (including until the\n   310\t  client has received `:ws/accept`),\n   311\t  `invalid-value` for unencodable values, `closed` once down; `close!`\n   312\t  means disconnect, reattachable;\n   313\t  connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited.\n   314\t- **Server side**: the accepted-connection handle — writer+closable, minted\n   315\t  from a socket the runtime handed over, so neither `create!` nor `attach!`\n   316\t  produces it — with the same `full`, `closed`, `transport-error`,\n   317\t  send-racing-close, and identity rules as the client handle; plus that\n   318\t  connection's own boundary adapter for its inbound events.\n   319\t- **Transport constructor**: a client host supplies its traffic deposit\n   320\t  destination and admission declaration. A serving endpoint receives bind\n   321\t  host/port, advertised host/port, a canonical-path resolution table, a\n   322\t  boundary control medium, and a fixed collection of capacity-one\n   323\t  acceptance-handoff slots —\n   324\t  offer and acknowledgement media plus their declarations — and the\n   325\t  composition's policy for expiring unacknowledged offers. The entry\n   326\t  functions a host puts in its dispatch table are closures this constructor\n   327\t  produces from that state. These are composition-supplied streams, not\n   328\t  namespace-global state; bare namespace vars could reach them only through\n   329\t  namespace globals.\n   330\t- **Endpoint stepping**: the transport exports `endpoint-step`, a total,\n   331\t  non-waiting operation over endpoint state and explicit `now`. It polls\n   332\t  acknowledgement slots, applies the constructor's admission-expiry policy,\n   333\t  sends accept control, and returns the next endpoint state. Path disclaimers\n   334\t  and slot-exhaustion closes happen immediately in the bounded upgrade callback\n   335\t  and never occupy acceptance state. `endpoint-step` never\n   336\t  self-schedules; the composition driver in 4c owns cadence.\n   337\t- **Handshake**: encode and recognize the served-stream presentation and the\n   338\t  authoritative disclaimer as settled at the wire-contract gate and recorded\n   339\t  in `dao.stream.ws.md` — wire protocol, so\n   340\t  both ends of it are transport, not composition.\n   341\t- **Boundary adapter and acceptance handoff**: client resolution, payload,\n   342\t  and lifecycle events are deposited into the client's ordinary host-composed\n   343\t  traffic medium. A server-side accepted handle is first offered through a free\n   344\t  capacity-one handoff slot under `:ws/event :ws/accepted`; the transport\n   345\t  retains the connection in bounded pre-accept state and sends no wire\n   346\t  `:ws/accept` until `endpoint-step` observes the matching stream\n   347\t  acknowledgement. That acknowledgement carries a composition-created\n   348\t  per-attachment traffic writer and its admission declaration; its reader\n   349\t  cursor was minted before the acknowledgement. Accepted payload is deposited\n   350\t  only there, while the endpoint control medium carries only pre-accept and\n   351\t  endpoint lifecycle facts. Every deposited event carries the client-side identity\n   352\t  returned by `attach!`, or the server-side identity carried by the accepted\n   353\t  offer, under `:ws/attachment`. Admission is enforced at assembly against\n   354\t  the declarations passed alongside the media, and every non-`ok` deposit\n   355\t  follows `dao.stream.ws.md`'s teardown rule.\n   356\t- No callbacks in the public surface; the legacy `on-open!`/`on-message!`/\n   357\t  `on-close!` shape does not reappear.\n   358\t- **Dependency check**: `v2/ws.cljc` requires only `dao.stream` — never\n   359\t  the ringbuffer namespace. The ws transport depends on the protocols; the\n   360\t  offer, acknowledgement, traffic, and served-stream media are all\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
