{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_55","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-06 15:58:14 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-06 22:58:14 +07 (Asia/Bangkok)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a0776c-dbcf-7343-a6de-ef18f705fec7\n\nP1 | collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:167-231 | Architectural defect: Decision 2 directly contradicts Axiom 1. The superior architecture requires all IO/data flow through streams and makes host boundaries stream boundaries (`docs/design/datom.world.md:16,40-42,51-68`; `docs/design/dao.stream.md:14-22`). Replacing `ds/append!` with direct `RandomAccessFile`/Node `fs`/Dart IO makes the synchronous backend function the file boundary. Reader count does not determine whether IO is a stream, and `dao.jing.mem` performs no IO, so it is not precedent. “Storage ignorance” prohibits interpreting stored content (`docs/design/dao.jing.md:185-200`), not exposing transport framing. | Choose the v2 append-log option and require its declared surfaces/outcomes and conformance evidence. If direct file IO is genuinely intended, amend the superior architecture first; this subordinate migration plan cannot create that exception.\n\nP1 | collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:54-73,310-320,405-436 | Architectural isolation defect: the asserted stream-free shared core does not exist. Both new namespaces would require `dao.jing`, which directly requires legacy `dao.stream` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:18-20`). Consequently J2 cannot demonstrate a v2-only dependency graph, and Decision 4’s “v1 dependency … none” claim is false during the parallel phase. Merely avoiding duplicated address minting does not require this coupling. | Extract the content-addressing and handle operations into one stream-free shared namespace, retain the old `dao.jing` surface as the v1-facing composition during migration, and have the v2 observer/remote depend only on that shared core plus v2. Gate J2 on absence of transitive—not merely direct—v1 dependencies.\n\nP1 | collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:269-279,453-454 | Implementation-blocking progress defect: the promised `pending-request → busy → step → requested` sequence cannot occur. `rpc/request!` is the only operation that retries `:unsent` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:193-256`); `rpc/poll!` only reads the response medium (`src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:386-445`). The proposed `step` calls only `poll!`, so `unsent?` remains true and every later request remains `busy` indefinitely. | Settle a bounded step algorithm that first retries the retained envelope through `rpc/request!` and threads that returned state before polling, or expose a separate explicit flush step. Specify and test all retry outcomes, including repeated `full`, undeliverable, and terminal.\n\nP1 | collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:260-289,297-308 | Architectural semantic loss: the proposed client supplies only raw `request-put address payload`; it does not replace `materialize!`. DaoJing materialization derives the address itself and, on `:present`, reads the stored value back before acknowledging success (`docs/design/dao.jing.md:160-166`; `src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:249-296`). This integrity behavior is pinned by collision and unreadable-present tests (`test/dao/jing_test.cljc:240-263`). Calling the proposed object the async DaoJing backend while omitting that state transition weakens the storage contract. | Specify a caller-stepped materialization operation that derives the address, issues put, conditionally issues get on `:present`, verifies equality, and only then completes with the address. Otherwise classify J3 as a raw RPC adapter and explicitly defer the async DaoJing backend rather than claiming it is delivered.\n\nP2 | collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:266-289 | Implementation gap: `step` is shown returning only `:state` and `:completions`, although it clears diagnostics. RPC can produce diagnostics for decoding failures, malformed values, unsolicited responses, and terminal-session events (`src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:290-305,318-383`), and `take-diagnostics` explicitly transfers that outbox to the driver (`src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:456-462`). Clearing without a specified output would silently discard observable failure data; an uncorrelatable malformed envelope may also leave its request outstanding rather than completing it. | Include `:diagnostics` in the step result, specify exactly-once publication, and distinguish correlated malformed application results from uncorrelatable RPC diagnostics. Test both the diagnostic and outstanding-request behavior.\n\nP2 | collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:378-403 | Compatibility evidence gap: the single complete golden record proves ordinary framing only. It does not exercise either incomplete-tail branch that J1 promises to preserve: a tail shorter than the four-byte header, or a length whose frame extends past EOF (`src/cljc/dao/stream/log.cljc:196-249`). The current Jing suite has corrupt complete records but no torn-tail test (`test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:138-176`). Copying the three host implementations also creates two independent owners until v1 deletion, so drift in recovery behavior is realistic. | Add legacy-byte recovery fixtures for a partial header, partial payload, and invalid/negative length on every host; assert truncation to the last complete frame, successful append afterward, and reopen durability. Prefer one neutral framing implementation or make byte-for-byte behavioral parity an explicit deletion gate.\n\nP2 | collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:493-510 | Unsettled architectural decision: the plan says J5 will “take one explicit decision” and offers only a recommendation, despite its own rule that decisions are settled before phases (`collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:82-84`) and the brief requiring the final naming decision up front (`collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.prompt.md:90-95`). Coupling the fold-back to whatever happens to `dao.stream` can keep DaoJing forked after its last v1 consumer disappears. | Decide the DaoJing end-state namespace now and make J5 execute that decision. The DaoJing fold-back need not wait for the stream namespace’s eventual name.\n\nNo P0 or P3 findings.\n\nReviewed and considered sound:\n\n- Decision 1’s stream-minted opaque cursor discipline, successor-only advancement after successful materialization, explicit gap recovery, round-robin fairness, and total handling of the seven `next` outcomes.\n- Decision 3’s core conclusion that the existing synchronous content-handle API cannot be implemented portably over polling RPC. DaoStream explicitly forbids waiting, and cljs/cljd cannot absorb asynchronous completion into a synchronous call (`docs/design/dao.stream.md:120-174`).\n- Keeping the existing v1 observer and remote adapter temporarily for `dao.space` is legitimate intentionally deferred work. The deletion gate and end condition prevent that coexistence from becoming steady state, consistent with `docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:642-659`.\n- The in-place file-handle surface appears compatible with current repository consumers: `dao.data.btree.storage` uses only `jing/materialize!`/`jing/get` (`src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc:43-48`), and the relevant `dao.space.index` reads do likewise (`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:243,274`). This does not cure Decision 2’s architectural violation or its persistence-test gap.\n- The explicit closed coordinate dispatch, absence of a replacement ambient registry, `:cljd`-first reader-conditional rule, and caller-owned state-machine/driver model are sound."}}
